    <div type="book" osisID="Titus">
      <title>Titus</title>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="Titus.1">
        <title>Titus 1 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.1">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">The document's genre and occasion:</hi> This is a letter from Paul to Titus, a co-worker who has been left behind on the island of Crete to complete organizational work that Paul began but could not finish before departing. The letter is, like 1 Timothy, a mandate letter — a formal authorization from a superior to a delegate, empowering the delegate to act on the sender's behalf. The parallel with 1 Timothy is close in several respects: both letters are addressed to individuals left behind in a specific location to manage a specific problem; both contain instructions about appointing qualified leaders; both warn against false teachers; both deploy the "God our Savior" language and the medical metaphor of "sound teaching." But the situations differ in important ways. Timothy was left in Ephesus, a major city with an established community already experiencing a crisis of false teaching. Titus is on Crete, where the communities appear to be newer and less organized — the work that remains is foundational: appointing elders "in every town" (1:5), which implies that no formal leadership structure yet exists. The crisis in Crete also has its own character. While the false teachers in Ephesus were described generally as promoters of myths and genealogies who desire to be law-teachers (1 Tim 1:4, 7), in Crete the false teachers are specifically identified as "those of the circumcision" (1:10, hoi ek tes peritomes), and their teaching involves "Jewish myths" and "commands of people who turn away from the truth" (1:14). The problem is more specifically defined: it is a Torah-observant faction imposing requirements on the communities that Paul considers contrary to the gospel.</p>
          <p>The island of Crete held a particular place in the first-century Mediterranean world. It was one of the largest islands in the Mediterranean, with a long history stretching back to the Minoan civilization. By the Roman period, Crete was a province administered jointly with Cyrenaica (Libya). Its Jewish population is attested: Acts 2:11 lists "Cretans" among those present in Jerusalem at Pentecost who heard the apostles speak in their own languages. The existence of Jewish communities on the island provides the social context for the "circumcision party" that Titus must confront. The island also had a reputation in the Greco-Roman world — a reputation Paul invokes through a quotation attributed to a Cretan prophet (1:12) — for dishonesty and moral laxity. The word kretizein ("to Cretanize") had become a Greek verb meaning "to lie," and the island's association with deception was proverbial.</p>
          <p>The letter is shorter and more compressed than 1 Timothy. Where 1 Timothy developed its themes at length — the proper use of the law (1 Tim 1:8-11), Paul's personal testimony of mercy (1 Tim 1:12-17), detailed qualifications for overseers and deacons (1 Tim 3:1-13) — Titus moves quickly. The elder qualifications in 1:5-9 are compressed into a few verses. The description of false teachers in 1:10-16 is pointed and specific rather than extended. The theological statements are dense summaries (1:1-4; 2:11-14; 3:3-7) rather than developed arguments. The letter reads as an efficient set of instructions for a trusted delegate who knows the situation and needs authorization more than explanation.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (Titus 2):</hi> Chapter 2 opens with a direct contrast to the false teachers described at the end of chapter 1: "But as for you, teach what accords with sound doctrine" (sy de lalei ha prepei te hygiainouse didaskalia, 2:1). The conjunction de ("but") and the emphatic pronoun sy ("you") set Titus in opposition to the false teachers just described. Where they teach for shameful gain and upset whole households (1:11), Titus is to teach what befits sound doctrine. What follows is a series of instructions organized by social category — older men (2:2), older women (2:3-4), younger women (2:4-5), younger men (2:6), and slaves (2:9-10) — each describing the behavior appropriate to that group. Titus himself is to be a model of good works (2:7-8). The section culminates in a theological grounding statement: "For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation for all people, training us to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age, waiting for our blessed hope, the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ, who gave himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for himself a people for his own possession who are zealous for good works" (2:11-14). This theological statement picks up and develops the themes compressed in the greeting (1:1-4): the appearing of God's saving purpose, the hope of eternal life, the purification of a people, and the connection between grace and godly living. The whole of chapter 2 is the positive content of what sound teaching produces — in contrast to the negative portrait of what false teaching produces in 1:10-16.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 1 connects to what follows:</hi> Chapter 1 establishes the foundation for the entire letter in three movements. First, the greeting (1:1-4) compresses the letter's theological framework into a dense statement: Paul's apostleship serves the faith and knowledge of God's elect, oriented toward the hope of eternal life promised before time and now manifested through preaching. Second, the appointment of elders (1:5-9) describes the structural response to the crisis: the communities need qualified leaders who can teach sound doctrine and refute opponents. Third, the description of false teachers (1:10-16) identifies the specific threat: insubordinate members of the circumcision party who teach Jewish myths and human commands for shameful gain, whose profession of knowledge is contradicted by their works. Every subsequent section of the letter responds to this foundation. Chapter 2's instructions about behavior by social category are the practical content of the "sound doctrine" that the elders must teach (1:9) and that Titus must model (2:1). Chapter 3's instructions about civic conduct ("be subject to rulers and authorities, obedient, ready for every good work," 3:1) and the theological statement about God's mercy and renewal (3:3-7) develop the themes of godliness (eusebeia) and good works (kala erga) that appear in the greeting (1:1) and the elder qualifications (1:8). And the closing warning to "avoid foolish controversies, genealogies, dissensions, and quarrels about the law" (3:9) circles back to the false teachers of 1:10-16 and their Jewish myths and human commands (1:14), closing the ring the letter opened. Chapter 1 is the architectural blueprint for the building the rest of the letter constructs.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.1.1-Titus.1.4">
          <title>Verses 1-4: The Greeting — Servanthood, Apostleship, and the Promise Before Time</title>
          <p>"Paul, a servant of God and an apostle of Jesus Christ, for the faith of God's elect and their knowledge of the truth that accords with godliness, in hope of eternal life, which God, who never lies, promised before the ages began, and at the proper time manifested in his word through the preaching with which I have been entrusted by the command of God our Savior; to Titus, my true child in a common faith: Grace and peace from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Savior."</p>
          <p>This greeting is the most theologically dense of any in the Pastoral Epistles. In 1 Timothy 1:1, the greeting was formal but compact: "Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus according to the command of God our Savior and of Christ Jesus our hope." In 2 Timothy 1:1, the greeting was warmer and more personally oriented: "Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus by the will of God according to the promise of life that is in Christ Jesus." Here in Titus, the greeting expands into a compressed theological summary that encompasses Paul's identity, his mission's purpose, the temporal scope of God's plan, and the means of its disclosure. The greeting functions almost as a creed — a summary of the entire gospel compressed into a single complex sentence.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Paul, a servant of God and an apostle of Jesus Christ"</hi> (Paulos doulos theou, apostolos de Iesou Christou). Two titles are stated, joined by de ("and, but, moreover"). The first is doulos theou — "servant of God" or "slave of God." This title does not appear in the greetings of any other Pauline letter. In Romans 1:1, Paul calls himself doulos Christou Iesou ("servant of Christ Jesus"); in Philippians 1:1, Paul and Timothy are douloi Christou Iesou ("servants of Christ Jesus"). But here the servanthood is attributed specifically to God (theou), and the apostleship to Jesus Christ (Iesou Christou). The pairing creates a deliberate structure: Paul serves God and is sent by Christ. The two roles are complementary, not competitive: the one who serves God is the one whom Christ commissioned.</p>
          <p>The noun doulos ("slave, servant, bondservant") in the LXX carries a distinctive weight when applied to those who serve God. Moses is called doulos theou or doulos kyriou repeatedly: Joshua 1:2 ("Moses my servant," Mouses ho doulos mou); Deuteronomy 34:5 ("Moses the servant of the LORD," Mouses doulos kyriou); 2 Kings 18:12 (LXX 4 Kingdoms 18:12); Nehemiah 1:7-8; Daniel 9:11; Malachi 4:4 (LXX 3:22). Joshua is called doulos kyriou at his death (Joshua 24:29; Judges 2:8). David is doulos throughout the Psalms and the historical books: "David my servant" (Psalm 89:3 LXX [88:4]; 2 Samuel 7:5; 1 Kings 11:13). The prophets collectively are "my servants the prophets" (2 Kings 17:13; Jeremiah 7:25; Amos 3:7). Abraham is doulos (Psalm 105:42 LXX [104:42]). The title doulos theou in the LXX is not a mark of degradation but of intimate commission — it designates the person who stands in a special relationship to God, authorized to act on God's behalf, carrying God's purposes. By using this title, Paul places himself within the line of Israel's commissioned servants: Moses, Joshua, David, the prophets.</p>
          <p>The second title, apostolos ("apostle, one sent, authorized representative"), functions as in the other letters. But the combination of the two titles — doulos theou and apostolos Iesou Christou — creates a dual identification that roots Paul's mission in both the God of Israel's history and the Christ who has now sent him. A first-century hearer would understand: this man serves the same God Moses served, and he has been dispatched by the Messiah.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For the faith of God's elect and their knowledge of the truth that accords with godliness"</hi> (kata pistin eklekton theou kai epignosin aletheias tes kat' eusebeian). The preposition kata with the accusative here indicates purpose or direction: Paul's servanthood and apostleship operate "for" or "toward" these ends. Two purposes are stated:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">The faith of God's elect</hi> (pistin eklekton theou). Paul's apostleship serves the pistis ("faith, trust, believing") of the eklektoi ("chosen ones, elect, selected") of God. The noun eklektos in the LXX describes those whom God has selected for his purposes. Israel collectively is God's chosen: Isaiah 45:4, "For the sake of my servant Jacob, and Israel my chosen one" (ton eklekton mou Israel); Isaiah 65:9, "my chosen shall inherit it" (hoi eklektoi mou); Isaiah 65:15, "my chosen" (tois eklektois mou); Isaiah 65:22, "my chosen shall long enjoy the work of their hands" (hoi eklektoi mou). The designation is corporate and national in the prophetic tradition — Israel is God's elect people, chosen not because of their merit but because of God's purpose (Deuteronomy 7:7-8; 9:4-6). In Paul's usage, the eklektoi are those — Jew and Gentile alike — whom God has called into his people through Christ. Romans 8:33: "Who shall bring any charge against God's elect?" (tis enkaleisei kata eklekton theou); Colossians 3:12: "Put on then, as God's chosen ones" (hōs eklektoi tou theou); Romans 11:7: "the elect obtained it" (he eklogē epetychèn). The term carries the full weight of Israel's identity as God's chosen people, now extended to encompass all who have responded to the gospel call.</p>
          <p>Paul's apostleship exists to serve the faith of these chosen ones. He does not create the faith; he serves it. His preaching, teaching, and organizing are directed toward the establishment, strengthening, and preservation of the trust that God's people place in God.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Knowledge of the truth that accords with godliness</hi> (epignosin aletheias tes kat' eusebeian). The noun epignosis ("knowledge, recognition, full knowledge, discernment") is an intensified form of gnosis ("knowledge"), with the prefix epi- adding a sense of directedness or thoroughness. In Paul's usage, epignosis often describes the knowledge of God or the gospel that is the goal of the community's growth. Colossians 1:9-10: "asking that you may be filled with the knowledge (epignosin) of his will in all spiritual wisdom and understanding, so as to walk in a manner worthy of the Lord." Colossians 2:2: "the knowledge (epignosin) of God's mystery, which is Christ." Ephesians 1:17: "that the God of our Lord Jesus Christ... may give you the Spirit of wisdom and of revelation in the knowledge (epignosei) of him." Epignosis is not mere intellectual awareness but participatory knowledge — the kind of knowing that transforms the knower.</p>
          <p>The truth (aletheia) that is known is qualified: it is truth kat' eusebeian — "according to godliness" or "that accords with godliness." The preposition kata ("according to, in accord with, corresponding to") links truth and godliness as inseparable companions. The noun eusebeia ("godliness, piety, reverence, proper worship") is one of the most important terms in the Pastoral Epistles, appearing ten times in 1 Timothy and Titus (1 Tim 2:2; 3:16; 4:7, 8; 6:3, 5, 6, 11; 2 Tim 3:5; Titus 1:1). In Greek usage, eusebeia described the proper reverence owed to the gods, parents, and civic institutions — a comprehensive orientation of life that honored what deserved honor. In the LXX and Jewish usage, it describes the piety that flows from the fear of God. The phrase kat' eusebeian establishes a criterion: the truth Paul serves is truth that produces godliness. This is not abstract or speculative knowledge; it is knowledge that changes how people live. The distinction is aimed at the false teachers, whose "knowledge" (they "profess to know God," 1:16) produces not godliness but disobedience and detestable works. True knowledge and godliness travel together; false knowledge and ungodliness travel together. The quality of a teaching's fruit reveals the nature of the teaching.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"In hope of eternal life"</hi> (ep' elpidi zoes aioniou). The preposition epi with the dative (ep' elpidi) expresses the basis or ground: Paul's apostleship rests upon, is grounded in, operates on the basis of the hope (elpis) of eternal life (zoes aioniou). The phrase zoe aionios ("life of the age, eternal life, life that belongs to the coming age") in Jewish usage describes the life of the age to come — the quality of existence that belongs to God's future, when death and corruption are overcome. Daniel 12:2 (LXX): "And many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life (eis zoen aionion)." The phrase appears in the Gospels as what the faithful inherit or enter into (Matthew 19:16, 29; 25:46; Mark 10:17, 30; Luke 10:25; 18:18, 30; John 3:15-16, 36; 5:24; 6:40, 47, 54, 68; 17:2-3). In Paul's other letters: Romans 2:7 ("to those who by patience in well-doing seek for glory and honor and immortality, he will give eternal life"); Romans 5:21 ("so that, as sin reigned in death, grace also might reign through righteousness leading to eternal life"); Romans 6:22-23 ("the end is eternal life... the free gift of God is eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord"); Galatians 6:8 ("the one who sows to the Spirit will from the Spirit reap eternal life"). Hope of eternal life is not a secondary benefit of faith but its foundational orientation: the entire mission is oriented toward the life of the coming age.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Which God, who never lies, promised before the ages began"</hi> (hen epengeilato ho apseudes theos pro chronon aionion). The relative pronoun hen ("which") refers back to eternal life. This life was promised (epengeilato, from epangellesthai, "to promise, to pledge, to undertake") by God. The verb is the middle voice of epangellein, and in Paul's usage it consistently describes God's solemn pledges — particularly the promise to Abraham (Romans 4:21; Galatians 3:19) and the promise of the Spirit (Galatians 3:14).</p>
          <p>God is described with a striking adjective: apseudes — "who never lies, truthful, incapable of falsehood." The word is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) and pseudos ("lie, falsehood, deception"). It appears only here in the New Testament. In Greek literature, the word was used of oracles and divine speech — utterances that are reliable because the speaker cannot deceive. The description has deep roots in Israel's testimony about God. Numbers 23:19: "God is not man, that he should lie, or a son of man, that he should change his mind. Has he said, and will he not do it? Or has he spoken, and will he not fulfill it?" 1 Samuel 15:29: "The Glory of Israel will not lie or have regret, for he is not a man, that he should have regret." Psalm 89:35 (LXX 88:36): "Once for all I have sworn by my holiness; I will not lie to David." The attribute of divine truthfulness is not an abstract philosophical claim but a covenantal assertion: the God who made promises to Israel is the God who keeps them. He cannot lie because his character does not permit it.</p>
          <p>The timing of the promise is specified as pro chronon aionion — "before the ages of time" or "before eternal times." This is the same phrase that appeared in 2 Timothy 1:9, where God's purpose and grace were said to have been "given us in Christ Jesus before the ages began." The concept is the same: God's saving purpose precedes created time. Before history began, the promise of eternal life already existed in God's intention. This is not a late amendment to God's plan or a response to unforeseen circumstances. It is the original purpose, the plan from before the beginning.</p>
          <p>The combination of the two elements — God's inability to lie and the pre-temporal origin of the promise — creates an extraordinarily strong basis for confidence. The promise is (a) made by one who cannot deceive, and (b) established before time itself. These two anchors — the character of the promisor and the antiquity of the promise — together form the ground on which Paul's hope and the community's hope rest. No subsequent event can destabilize a promise that predates all events and is guaranteed by one who is constitutionally incapable of falsehood.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And at the proper time manifested in his word through the preaching with which I have been entrusted by the command of God our Savior"</hi> (ephanerdsen de kairois idiois ton logon autou en kerygmati ho episteuthen ego kat' epitagen tou soteros hemon theou). Several elements require attention:</p>
          <p>The verb phaneroun ("to make visible, to reveal, to bring to light, to manifest") describes the transition from hidden to visible. What was promised before time has now been manifested — brought into the open, made accessible to perception and understanding. The same verb appeared in 2 Timothy 1:10 for the manifestation of grace "through the appearing of our Savior Christ Jesus." The hidden-then-revealed pattern is fundamental to Paul's understanding of the gospel: God's purpose existed before time, was partially disclosed through Israel's history and scriptures, and has now been fully manifested through Christ and the proclamation about Christ.</p>
          <p>The timing is described as kairois idiois — "at the proper times" or "in his own appointed times." The noun kairos ("time, season, opportune moment, appointed time") differs from chronos ("time, duration, span"). While chronos describes time as duration (the passage of hours, days, years), kairos describes time as occasion — the right moment, the appointed season, the fitting hour. The adjective idios ("one's own, proper, personal, appointed") indicates that these times belong to God: they are God's own appointed seasons. God determines when the hidden becomes visible. In Galatians 4:4, Paul writes: "But when the fullness of time had come (to pleroma tou chronou), God sent forth his Son." The same concept operates here: the manifestation occurred at the moment God had appointed.</p>
          <p>What was manifested is ton logon autou — "his word." The possessive autou ("his") identifies the word as God's. And the means of manifestation is en kerygmati — "in preaching" or "through proclamation." The noun kerygma ("proclamation, preaching, what is proclaimed") is the public announcement of the gospel message. In 1 Corinthians 1:21: "it pleased God through the folly of what we preach (tou kerygmatos) to save those who believe." In 1 Corinthians 2:4: "my speech and my message (kerygma) were not in plausible words of wisdom but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power." In 1 Corinthians 15:14: "if Christ has not been raised, then our preaching (kerygma) is in vain." The kerygma is the public proclamation of the gospel — the herald's announcement of what God has done in Christ.</p>
          <p>Paul has been entrusted (episteuthen, aorist passive of pisteuein, "to be entrusted with") with this preaching. The passive voice indicates that the commission came from outside Paul — he did not take it upon himself. And the authority behind the commission is specified: kat' epitagen tou soteros hemon theou — "by the command of God our Savior." The noun epitage ("command, order, decree, injunction") is the same word that appeared in 1 Timothy 1:1 to describe the authority behind Paul's apostleship. It is the language of formal mandate, military commission, official dispatch. Paul preaches not because he chose to but because he was ordered to.</p>
          <p>The title tou soteros hemon theou ("God our Savior") is a signature designation of the Pastoral Epistles. In 1 Timothy 1:1 it appeared in the greeting; in 1 Timothy 2:3, God our Savior "desires all people to be saved"; in 1 Timothy 4:10, "we have our hope set on the living God, who is the Savior of all people." In Titus, the title will appear again at 2:10 and 3:4. As noted in the analysis of 1 Timothy 1, the LXX consistently uses soter for God in the context of his demonstrated acts of deliverance — the exodus, the return from exile, the rescue of individuals from death and danger. The title is not a philosophical predicate but a historical summary: this is the God who saves, as he has shown by doing so.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To Titus, my true child in a common faith"</hi> (Tito gnesiō teknō kata koinen pistin). The addressee receives the same relational designation as Timothy: gnesios teknon — "true/legitimate child." As in 1 Timothy 1:2, the adjective gnesios ("genuine, legitimate, born in wedlock") establishes Titus as Paul's authentic spiritual offspring, not a pretender. But where Timothy was Paul's true child en pistei ("in faith"), Titus is his true child kata koinen pistin — "according to a common faith" or "in a shared faith." The adjective koinos ("common, shared, mutual") and the preposition kata ("according to") indicate that the faith binding Paul and Titus is one they hold in common. The faith is not private or exclusive to their personal relationship; it is the shared faith of the community, the common inheritance of all who believe. The word koinos here functions positively — the faith is common in the sense of universally shared among believers, not common in the sense of ordinary or vulgar. In Acts 2:44, the early community "had all things in common" (eichon hapanta koina). The faith Paul and Titus share is the one faith (Ephesians 4:5) held by the one people of God.</p>
          <p>Titus himself does not appear in the book of Acts, but he figures prominently in Paul's letters. In Galatians 2:1-3, Paul describes bringing Titus to Jerusalem: "I went up again to Jerusalem with Barnabas, taking Titus along with me... But even Titus, who was with me, was not forced to be circumcised, though he was a Greek." Titus's uncircumcised status became a test case for the principle that Gentile believers need not submit to circumcision. In 2 Corinthians, Titus serves as Paul's emissary to Corinth during the painful conflict: Paul sent him with the "severe letter" (2 Cor 7:6-7, 13-15; 8:6, 16-17, 23; 12:18). Paul describes Titus as his "partner and fellow worker" (2 Cor 8:23) and expresses deep confidence in him. Titus is a Gentile believer, a tested co-worker, and a delegate Paul trusts with difficult assignments. His deployment to Crete follows the pattern established in Corinth: Paul sends Titus into a challenging situation, trusting his competence and character.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Grace and peace from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Savior"</hi> (charis kai eirene apo theou patros kai Christou Iesou tou soteros hemon). The greeting here is the standard twofold "grace and peace," without the third element "mercy" (eleos) that appeared in the greetings to Timothy (1 Tim 1:2; 2 Tim 1:2). The omission may reflect the different character of this letter: 1 and 2 Timothy were deeply personal, and the inclusion of mercy anticipated themes of God's compassion toward those who suffer or who have sinned. Titus is more administrative in register, and the standard greeting suffices.</p>
          <p>The most notable feature of this greeting is the distribution of the title soter ("savior"). In verse 3, God is called "God our Savior" (tou soteros hemon theou). Here in verse 4, Christ Jesus is called "our Savior" (tou soteros hemon). The same title applied to both God and Christ creates a theological pairing that runs throughout the letter. In chapter 2, the pattern will continue: 2:10 speaks of "God our Savior," while 2:13 speaks of "our great God and Savior Jesus Christ." In chapter 3: 3:4 speaks of "God our Savior," while 3:6 speaks of "Jesus Christ our Savior." The alternation is deliberate and creates a theological unity: the saving work belongs to God and is executed through Christ. The God who saves and the Christ through whom he saves share the same title because they share the same purpose. For a first-century Jewish hearer, steeped in the conviction that salvation belongs to YHWH alone (Isaiah 43:11, "I, I am the LORD, and besides me there is no savior"), the application of soter to both God and Christ would be remarkable: it places Christ within the sphere of Israel's God's own saving activity.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.1.5-Titus.1.9">
          <title>Verses 5-9: The Appointment of Elders — Structure for the Communities</title>
          <p>"This is why I left you in Crete, so that you might put in order what remained to be done, and appoint elders in every town as I directed you — if anyone is above reproach, the husband of one wife, and his children are believers and not open to the charge of debauchery or insubordination. For an overseer, as God's steward, must be above reproach. He must not be arrogant or quick-tempered or a drunkard or violent or greedy for gain, but hospitable, a lover of good, self-controlled, upright, holy, and disciplined. He must hold firm to the trustworthy word as taught, so that he may be able to give instruction in sound doctrine and also to rebuke those who contradict it."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The greeting (1:1-4) established the theological framework — Paul's apostleship serving the faith of God's elect, grounded in the hope of eternal life promised by the God who cannot lie, now manifested through preaching. <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> The description of false teachers (1:10-16) identifies the specific threat that makes the appointment of qualified elders urgent. <hi type="bold">How this section connects the two:</hi> The theological framework of the greeting establishes what the communities need (faith, knowledge of truth, godliness, hope of eternal life), and the appointment of elders provides the structural means of delivering and protecting these things. The elder qualifications culminate in the ability to teach sound doctrine and refute opponents (1:9) — which leads directly into the identification of those opponents in 1:10-16. The elder qualifications are not an abstract ideal but a direct response to a concrete threat: the communities need leaders who can withstand the false teachers described in the verses that follow.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"This is why I left you in Crete, so that you might put in order what remained to be done"</hi> (toutou charin apelipon se en Krete, hina ta leiponta epidiorthose). The phrase toutou charin ("for this reason, on this account") indicates that Titus's presence in Crete has a defined purpose. The verb apoleipein ("to leave behind, to leave remaining") in the aorist describes a past action: Paul left Titus in Crete at some point and has now departed. The island of Crete (Krete) is named as the location.</p>
          <p>The purpose is stated with two infinitives joined by kai ("and"):</p>
          <p>(1) Ta leiponta epidiorthose — "that you might set right what remained" or "put in order what was left unfinished." The verb epidiorthoun ("to set right, to correct, to put in proper order") appears only here in the New Testament. It is composed of epi ("upon, further") + dia ("through") + orthoun ("to make straight, to set upright"). The prefix epi- may intensify ("thoroughly set right") or indicate continuation ("further set right, complete the setting-right already begun"). The neuter plural participle ta leiponta ("the things remaining, what was left over, the unfinished matters") indicates that Paul began work on Crete but left before completing it. The organizational task was partially done; Titus is to finish it.</p>
          <p>(2) The second purpose, joined by kai, follows: appoint elders.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And appoint elders in every town as I directed you"</hi> (kai katasteses kata polin presbyterous, hos ego soi dietaxamen). The verb kathistanai ("to appoint, to put in charge, to establish in a position") describes the formal installation of persons in an office. In Acts 6:3, the congregation is told to select seven men "whom we may appoint (katastesomen) over this duty." In the LXX, the verb is used for the appointment of leaders: Exodus 18:21, where Jethro advises Moses to appoint (katasteseis) capable men as officials over the people. The verb implies formal, authorized appointment — not informal emergence of leadership but deliberate installation.</p>
          <p>The persons to be appointed are presbyteroi ("elders, older men, members of the council of elders"). The noun presbyteros in its basic sense means "older person" and carries the connotation of seniority, maturity, and the wisdom that comes with age. In Jewish communal life, the presbyteroi constituted the governing body of a synagogue or community. Elders governed Israel from the earliest times: Exodus 3:16 ("Go and gather the elders of Israel together"); Exodus 18:12; Numbers 11:16-17 ("Gather for me seventy men of the elders of Israel"); Deuteronomy 21:19; Ruth 4:2; 1 Kings 8:1. In the synagogue, the presbyteroi were the recognized leaders who managed the community's affairs, adjudicated disputes, and provided pastoral oversight. The appointment of presbyteroi in the new communities on Crete follows the synagogue pattern: each community needs a recognized body of senior, qualified leaders.</p>
          <p>The phrase kata polin ("in every town, city by city") indicates that the appointment is to be comprehensive: not just in one or two locations but across the island, in every community that has been established. This suggests that Paul's work on Crete had resulted in multiple communities in multiple towns, none of which yet had formal leadership.</p>
          <p>The clause hos ego soi dietaxamen ("as I directed you") specifies that the qualifications about to be listed are not Titus's invention but Paul's instruction. The verb diatassein ("to direct, to order, to arrange, to give detailed instructions") describes authoritative instruction: Paul laid out specific criteria, and Titus is to follow them. The first person pronoun ego ("I") is emphatic: this comes from Paul's authority.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"If anyone is above reproach"</hi> (ei tis estin anenkletos). The conditional ei tis ("if anyone") introduces the criteria by which candidates are to be evaluated. The first and overarching qualification is anenkletos — "above reproach, blameless, not able to be accused, without indictment." The adjective is composed of the alpha-privative (an-) and enkletos (from enkalein, "to bring a charge against, to accuse, to indict"). An anenkletos person is one against whom no legitimate charge can be brought. In Colossians 1:22, Paul writes that Christ's purpose is to present believers "holy and blameless and above reproach (anenklétous) before him." In 1 Corinthians 1:8, Christ "will sustain you to the end, guiltless (anenklétous) in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ." The qualification is not sinless perfection but a life against which no public accusation can stand. This is the baseline: everything that follows specifies what "above reproach" looks like in practice.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The husband of one wife"</hi> (mias gynaikos aner). Literally: "a one-woman man" or "a man of one woman." The phrase is identical to 1 Timothy 3:2, where the same qualification appeared for overseers, and 1 Timothy 3:12, where it appeared for deacons. The genitive construction mias gynaikos ("of one wife/woman") describes a characteristic: the elder is a man characterized by faithfulness to one woman. In the first-century context, where polygamy (though rare in the Roman world) still existed in some Jewish communities, and where serial marriage through easy divorce was common in both Jewish and Roman practice, the qualification insists on marital fidelity and singularity. The elder's domestic life must be characterized by undivided commitment to one spouse.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And his children are believers and not open to the charge of debauchery or insubordination"</hi> (tekna echon pista, me en kategoria asotias e anypotakta). The elder must have children (tekna) who are pista — the adjective pistos here can mean either "believing/faithful" (they have embraced the faith) or "trustworthy/reliable" (they are well-behaved and dependable). The context points toward the first sense: the elder's household has responded to the gospel. The logic is similar to 1 Timothy 3:4-5: "He must manage his own household well, with all dignity keeping his children submissive, for if someone does not know how to manage his own household, how will he care for God's church?" The household is the proving ground for leadership: if a man's own children have not responded to his influence and instruction, his capacity to lead a community is in question.</p>
          <p>Two negative conditions are specified. The children must not be en kategoria asotias — "under accusation of debauchery." The noun asotia ("debauchery, profligacy, dissipation, reckless wastefulness") describes a life of excess and moral abandonment. In Ephesians 5:18: "And do not get drunk with wine, for that is debauchery (asotia)." In Luke 15:13, the prodigal son "squandered his property in reckless living" (zōn asōtōs). The word implies not merely occasional lapses but a pattern of uncontrolled excess. And the children must not be anypotakta — "insubordinate, unruly, not subject to authority." The same adjective appeared in 1 Timothy 1:9 in the vice list describing those for whom the law is laid down. It will appear again in Titus 1:10 to describe the false teachers. The connection is deliberate: the elder must not have children who exhibit the very quality that characterizes the opponents he is being appointed to confront.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For an overseer, as God's steward, must be above reproach"</hi> (dei gar ton episkopon hos theou oikonomon anenkleton einai). The conjunction gar ("for") introduces the reason for the requirements: an overseer must be above reproach because of what an overseer is — God's steward. The transition from presbyteros ("elder") in verse 5 to episkopos ("overseer") in verse 7 is significant. The two terms refer to the same office, described from different angles. The presbyteros designation emphasizes the person's standing within the community (senior, mature, respected). The episkopos designation emphasizes the person's function (one who watches over, supervises, oversees). The same identification of the two roles appears in Acts 20:17, 28, where Paul summons the presbyterous ("elders") of the Ephesian church and tells them: "Pay careful attention to yourselves and to all the flock, in which the Holy Spirit has made you episkopous ("overseers"), to care for the church of God." The terms are functionally interchangeable: the elders are the overseers, and the overseers are the elders. One word describes who they are in the community; the other describes what they do.</p>
          <p>The overseer is described as theou oikonomon — "God's steward." The noun oikonomos ("steward, household manager, administrator") is composed of oikos ("house") + nemein ("to manage, to distribute, to administer"). The oikonomos was the person entrusted with managing a household's affairs on behalf of the owner. In Luke 12:42, Jesus asks: "Who then is the faithful and wise steward (oikonomos), whom his master will set over his household, to give them their portion of food at the proper time?" In Luke 16:1-8, the parable of the dishonest steward (oikonomos) explores what happens when a steward mismanages his master's property. In 1 Corinthians 4:1-2: "This is how one should regard us, as servants of Christ and stewards (oikonomous) of the mysteries of God. Moreover, it is required of stewards (oikonomois) that they be found faithful." The designation theou oikonomon — "steward of God" — means that the overseer manages not his own household but God's. The community belongs to God; the overseer administers it on God's behalf. This understanding transforms the nature of the authority: the overseer's authority is delegated, not inherent. He acts for the owner, not for himself. And stewardship demands accountability: the steward must give an account to the one whose property he manages.</p>
          <p>The verb dei ("it is necessary, it must be") indicates not a suggestion but a requirement. Given what the overseer is (God's steward), the quality that follows (above reproach) is not optional but structurally necessary. A steward who can be credibly accused of misconduct damages the reputation of the one he represents.</p>
          <p>The list of qualifications that follows divides into negative (what the overseer must not be) and positive (what the overseer must be):</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Negative qualifications — what the overseer must not be:</hi></p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">"Not arrogant"</hi> (me authade). The adjective authades ("self-willed, arrogant, self-pleasing, stubborn") is composed of autos ("self") + hedesthai ("to please"). An authades person is one who pleases himself, who follows his own desires without regard for others, who insists on his own way. In 2 Peter 2:10, the word describes false teachers who are "bold and willful" (tolmetai authadeis). Aristotle (Nicomachean Ethics 4.3.30) defines the authades person as one who disregards others' opinions and acts solely for self-gratification. For a steward of God's household, self-will is disqualifying because the steward's task is to serve the owner's purpose, not his own preferences. An arrogant leader will shape the community around his own desires rather than God's instruction.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">"Not quick-tempered"</hi> (me orgilon). The adjective orgilos ("prone to anger, quick-tempered, irascible") describes a disposition, not a single act. The orgē ("anger") that characterizes this person is habitual and easily triggered. Proverbs 22:24 (LXX): "Do not befriend an angry man (andri thymōdei)" — the wisdom tradition recognizes that an easily angered person is dangerous to associate with and unfit for leadership. The elder must deal with difficult people (the false teachers of 1:10-16); an irascible temperament would escalate rather than resolve conflicts.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">"Not a drunkard"</hi> (me paroinon). The adjective paroinos ("given to wine, drunken, addicted to wine") is composed of para ("beside, alongside") + oinos ("wine"). The same word appeared in 1 Timothy 3:3 among the overseer qualifications there. Drunkenness destroys the self-control (sōphrosynē) that is central to the elder's character.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold">"Not violent"</hi> (me plekten). The noun plektes ("striker, bully, violent person") describes one who resorts to physical force. The same qualification appeared in 1 Timothy 3:3. A steward of God's household resolves disputes through instruction and persuasion, not through blows.</p>
          <p>(5) <hi type="bold">"Not greedy for gain"</hi> (me aischrokerde). The adjective aischrokerdes ("fond of dishonest gain, greedy for shameful profit") is composed of aischros ("shameful, base, disgraceful") + kerdos ("gain, profit, advantage"). The qualification takes on specific urgency in the context of the Cretan false teachers, who are described in verse 11 as teaching "for the sake of shameful gain" (aischrou kerdous charin). The elder must be the opposite of the false teacher: where the false teacher uses his position for profit, the elder must be free from the love of gain.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Positive qualifications — what the overseer must be:</hi></p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">"Hospitable"</hi> (philoxenon). The adjective philoxenos ("hospitable, loving strangers, welcoming guests") is composed of philos ("loving, fond of") + xenos ("stranger, foreigner, guest"). The same qualification appeared in 1 Timothy 3:2. In the ancient world, where inns were unreliable and often dangerous, hospitality was essential for the functioning of the community and the movement of itinerant workers. Romans 12:13: "Contribute to the needs of the saints and seek to show hospitality" (ten philoxenian diōkontes). Hebrews 13:2: "Do not neglect to show hospitality to strangers, for thereby some have entertained angels unawares." 3 John 5-8 praises Gaius for showing hospitality to traveling brothers. The hospitable elder opens his home to the community and to travelers, making the household a center of ministry rather than a private enclave.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">"A lover of good"</hi> (philagathon). The adjective philagathos ("loving what is good, devoted to good things") is composed of philos ("loving") + agathos ("good"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. It describes a comprehensive orientation toward what is good — good people, good actions, good purposes. The elder is not merely tolerant of good but actively drawn to it, seeking it out and promoting it.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">"Self-controlled"</hi> (sophrona). The adjective sōphrōn ("of sound mind, self-controlled, temperate, moderate, prudent") is one of the cardinal virtues of the Pastoral Epistles. The sōphrōn-word group appears throughout: sōphrosynē in 1 Timothy 2:9, 15; sōphronismos in 2 Timothy 1:7; sōphronizein in Titus 2:4; sōphronein in Titus 2:6; sōphronōs in Titus 2:12; sōphrōn here and in 1 Timothy 3:2. The concept describes the mental clarity and self-mastery that keeps a person from excess in any direction — neither panicked nor reckless, neither indulgent nor harsh, but balanced, measured, and in control of his own impulses. This is the antidote to the false teachers' influence: where they upset whole households (1:11), the self-controlled elder maintains stability.</p>
          <p>(4) <hi type="bold">"Upright"</hi> (dikaion). The adjective dikaios ("just, righteous, upright, fair") describes a person who acts rightly toward others, who gives each person what is due, who conducts his affairs with fairness. In the LXX, dikaios is one of the most important words in the vocabulary of covenant faithfulness: the righteous person lives according to God's standards and treats others justly.</p>
          <p>(5) <hi type="bold">"Holy"</hi> (hosion). The adjective hosios ("holy, devout, pious, pleasing to God") describes a person set apart for God's purposes, one whose life is characterized by reverence and devotion. In the LXX, hosios often translates the Hebrew hasid ("faithful, devoted, loyal to God"). Psalm 16:10 (LXX 15:10): "You will not allow your holy one (ton hosion sou) to see corruption." Deuteronomy 32:4: "A faithful God (theos pistos) and without injustice; righteous (dikaios) and holy (hosios) is the LORD." The pairing of dikaios and hosios in the present list echoes this pairing in the LXX: the elder is both just toward people and devoted toward God.</p>
          <p>(6) <hi type="bold">"Disciplined"</hi> (enkrate). The adjective enkrates ("self-controlled, mastering one's desires, having power over") is composed of en ("in") + kratos ("power, strength, might"). The enkrates person has power over himself — he governs his own appetites and impulses. In Galatians 5:23, enkrateia ("self-control") appears as the final fruit of the Spirit. In 1 Corinthians 7:9, the word describes the capacity to manage one's sexual desires. In 1 Corinthians 9:25, Paul uses the image of an athlete who exercises enkrateia — self-control in all things — to win the prize. The elder must be a person who demonstrates mastery over his own life before he can be trusted with the oversight of others' lives.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"He must hold firm to the trustworthy word as taught"</hi> (antechomenon tou kata ten didachen pistou logou). The verb antechesthai ("to hold fast, to cling to, to devote oneself to") in the middle voice describes active, deliberate attachment. The elder does not merely know the word; he holds onto it, grips it, does not let go. The object is tou pistou logou — "the faithful/trustworthy word." The adjective pistos ("trustworthy, reliable, faithful") describes the word's character: it is dependable, worthy of trust. The phrase kata ten didachen ("according to the teaching, as taught") specifies that this trustworthy word is not the elder's private interpretation but the word as transmitted through apostolic teaching. The elder holds fast to what he has been taught — the received deposit of sound instruction, the didache that was delivered by Paul and his associates.</p>
          <p>This qualification is the climax of the list and the bridge to the section that follows. All the preceding character qualities serve this purpose: the elder must be the kind of person who can hold the word firmly and deploy it effectively. Character qualifications without doctrinal competence would produce good men who cannot teach. Doctrinal competence without character would produce knowledgeable men who cannot lead. Both must be present.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So that he may be able to give instruction in sound doctrine and also to rebuke those who contradict it"</hi> (hina dynatos e kai parakalein en te didaskalia te hygiainouse kai tous antilegontas elenchein). The hina clause ("in order that, so that") states the purpose of holding firm to the trustworthy word. The purpose is twofold:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">To give instruction in sound doctrine</hi> (parakalein en te didaskalia te hygiainouse). The verb parakalein ("to exhort, to encourage, to instruct, to comfort, to appeal to") describes the positive ministry of teaching. The teaching is qualified as hygiainousa — "sound, healthy, in good condition." The medical metaphor that characterizes the Pastoral Epistles appears again: sound teaching is healthy teaching, and it produces health in those who receive it. The verb hygiainein ("to be healthy, to be sound") runs through these letters as the standard against which all teaching is measured (1 Tim 1:10; 6:3; 2 Tim 1:13; 4:3; Titus 1:9, 13; 2:1, 2). Teaching that does not produce health in the community is not sound, regardless of its other qualities.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">To rebuke those who contradict it</hi> (tous antilegontas elenchein). The verb elenchein ("to rebuke, to refute, to convict, to expose, to reprove") describes a confrontational ministry — not confrontation for its own sake, but the necessary work of correcting those who are wrong. The participle tous antilegontas ("those who speak against, those who contradict, those who oppose") identifies the targets: people who are actively opposing the sound teaching. The elder must not only teach the truth; he must also confront error. The two tasks are inseparable. In a community where no one contradicts sound teaching, only the first task would be needed. But Crete is not such a community — as the next verses will make clear.</p>
          <p>The transition from verse 9 to verse 10 is seamless: the elder must be able to rebuke those who contradict — "for" (gar, v. 10) there are many such contradictors. The qualifications are not hypothetical but responsive to an existing crisis.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.1.10-Titus.1.16">
          <title>Verses 10-16: The False Teachers — Identification, Condemnation, and the Test of Works</title>
          <p>"For there are many who are insubordinate, empty talkers and deceivers, especially those of the circumcision party. They must be silenced, since they are upsetting whole households by teaching for shameful gain what they ought not to teach. One of the Cretans, a prophet of their own, said, 'Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons.' This testimony is true. Therefore rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith, not devoting themselves to Jewish myths and the commands of people who turn away from the truth. To the pure, all things are pure, but to the defiled and unbelieving, nothing is pure; but both their minds and their consciences are defiled. They profess to know God, but they deny him by their works. They are detestable, disobedient, unfit for any good work."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The elder qualifications (1:5-9) culminated in the requirement that elders be able to refute opponents. <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> Chapter 2 opens with "But as for you, teach what accords with sound doctrine" (2:1), turning to the positive content of what Titus himself should teach. <hi type="bold">How this section connects the two:</hi> The false teachers (1:10-16) are the reason the elders need the refutation capability described in 1:9, and their teaching is the antithesis of the "sound doctrine" that Titus is to teach in 2:1. The section serves as the dark background against which both the elder qualifications and Titus's own teaching mission are defined. Everything the false teachers are, the elders must not be. Everything the false teachers teach, Titus must counter.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For there are many who are insubordinate, empty talkers and deceivers, especially those of the circumcision party"</hi> (eisin gar polloi [kai] anypotaktoi, mataiologoi kai phrenapatal, malista hoi ek tes peritomes). The conjunction gar ("for") connects directly to the need for elders who can refute opponents (v. 9). The reason is that polloi ("many") such persons exist on Crete. The problem is not a few isolated individuals but a widespread phenomenon.</p>
          <p>Three terms describe these persons:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Insubordinate</hi> (anypotaktoi). The adjective anypotaktos ("not subject to authority, rebellious, undisciplined, insubordinate") appeared already in verse 6 among the conditions that disqualify an elder's children. Its reappearance here creates a pointed connection: the very quality that would disqualify an elder's child from the community's respect is the defining quality of these false teachers. They refuse to submit to apostolic authority, to the teaching they have received, or to the order of the community. The word appeared also in 1 Timothy 1:9 in the vice list describing those for whom the law is laid down. Insubordination is the fundamental posture from which all their other errors flow.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Empty talkers</hi> (mataiologoi). The noun mataiologos ("one who talks idly, speaker of vanities, empty talker") is composed of mataios ("vain, empty, futile, purposeless") + logos ("word, speech"). The related noun mataiologia ("vain discussion, empty talk") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:6, where certain persons "wandered away into vain discussion." The designation identifies the content of their teaching as empty — not merely wrong in its conclusions but devoid of substance. It produces nothing. It leads nowhere. It is sound without significance.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Deceivers</hi> (phrenapatal). The noun phrenapates ("mind-deceiver, one who deceives the mind") is composed of phren ("mind, understanding, thinking") + apatan ("to deceive, to cheat, to mislead"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. It describes persons who mislead not through outright lies but through the corruption of the thinking process itself. They distort the way people reason, leading them to conclusions that seem plausible but are false. The deception operates at the level of the mind — the phren — the very faculty by which truth is evaluated. This makes the deception especially dangerous: the deceived person does not realize he has been misled because the instrument of evaluation (the mind) has itself been corrupted.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Especially those of the circumcision party"</hi> (malista hoi ek tes peritomes). The adverb malista ("especially, particularly, above all, most of all") indicates that the insubordinate empty-talkers are drawn primarily from a specific group: hoi ek tes peritomes — "those of the circumcision." The phrase identifies ethnic or religious origin: these are Jewish believers (or Jewish associates of the community) who insist on circumcision as a requirement for full participation in the people of God. The same phrase appears in Acts 10:45 and 11:2, where "the circumcision party" (hoi ek peritomes) are shocked that the Holy Spirit has been poured out on uncircumcised Gentiles. In Galatians 2:12, Peter drew back from eating with Gentiles "because he feared those of the circumcision" (tous ek peritomes). In Colossians 4:11, Paul mentions "the only ones of the circumcision among my fellow workers for the kingdom of God."</p>
          <p>The identification is specific and significant. The false teaching on Crete is not coming from pagan philosophy or Gentile religious practice. It comes from within the Jewish wing of the community — from people who bring Jewish interpretive traditions, Jewish purity regulations, and the insistence on circumcision into the community's life in ways that Paul considers contrary to the gospel. This identification will be confirmed by verse 14's mention of "Jewish myths" and by the purity discussion of verse 15.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"They must be silenced"</hi> (hous dei epistomizein). The verb epistomizein ("to stop the mouth, to silence, to muzzle") appears only here in the New Testament. It is composed of epi ("upon") + stoma ("mouth"). The word was used for muzzling an animal or stopping a person from speaking. The imperative is strong: these teachers must be prevented from continuing to teach. The verb dei ("it is necessary") indicates that this is not optional but required. The language goes beyond Paul's instruction in 1 Timothy, where Timothy was to "charge" the false teachers not to teach differently (1 Tim 1:3); here the instruction is to silence them entirely. The escalation reflects the situation's urgency: the damage being done is severe enough that the teaching must be stopped, not merely corrected.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Since they are upsetting whole households by teaching for shameful gain what they ought not to teach"</hi> (hoitines holous oikous anatrepousin didaskontes ha me dei aischrou kerdous charin). The relative pronoun hoitines ("who are of such a character that") introduces the reason for silencing them. The verb anatrepein ("to overturn, to upset, to subvert, to destroy") describes the effect: they are overturning holous oikous — "whole households." The noun oikos ("house, household, family") describes the basic social unit of the first-century world: the household, which included the head of household, spouse, children, slaves, and dependents. The early communities met in houses (Romans 16:5; 1 Corinthians 16:19; Colossians 4:15; Philemon 2), and households often came to faith as units (Acts 16:15, Lydia's household; Acts 16:33, the jailer's household; 1 Corinthians 1:16, the household of Stephanas). When a false teacher subverts a household, he destroys the basic unit of community life and faith. The adjective holous ("whole, entire") intensifies: they are not picking off individuals from the edges but overturning entire households.</p>
          <p>The content of their teaching is described negatively: ha me dei — "things that it is not necessary to teach" or "things they ought not to teach." The specific content is not detailed here but will be identified in verse 14 as "Jewish myths and commands of people who turn away from the truth."</p>
          <p>The motive is exposed: aischrou kerdous charin — "for the sake of shameful gain." The genitive aischrou kerdous is composed of aischros ("shameful, base, disgraceful") + kerdos ("gain, profit"). The preposition charin ("for the sake of, on account of") identifies the motive. These teachers teach what they teach not out of conviction but for profit. Their ministry is a business, and their doctrine is shaped by what pays. The accusation is devastating: the content of the teaching is driven by the prospect of financial return, not by fidelity to the truth. The elder qualification of verse 7 — "not greedy for shameful gain" (me aischrokerde) — was aimed precisely at this contrast: the elder must be the opposite of what these teachers are.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"One of the Cretans, a prophet of their own, said, 'Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons'"</hi> (eipen tis ex auton, idios auton prophetes, Kretes aei pseustai, kaka theria, gasteres argai). Paul quotes a saying attributed to one of the Cretans' own prophets. The identification of the source as tis ex auton — "one of them," i.e., a Cretan — means that the criticism comes from an insider, not an outsider. The title prophetes ("prophet") is applied with the possessive idios auton ("their own"), indicating that this person held a position of cultural authority among the Cretans themselves.</p>
          <p>The saying attributed to this prophet is traditionally associated with Epimenides of Crete, a semi-legendary figure of the sixth century BCE whom the Cretans regarded with reverence as a sage, seer, and purifier. Ancient sources credit Epimenides with various oracular and prophetic activities, including the purification of Athens from a plague. By Paul's time, Epimenides was a well-known figure in Greek literary tradition. The designation "prophet" (prophetes) for a pagan sage is notable: Paul uses the word not in the sense of a prophet of Israel's God but in the Greek sense of a divinely inspired speaker, a mantis or oracle. Paul meets the Cretans on their own cultural ground: he cites an authority they themselves recognize.</p>
          <p>The saying contains three charges:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">"Always liars"</hi> (aei pseustai). The adverb aei ("always, constantly, at all times") makes the accusation absolute. The noun pseustes ("liar, one who speaks falsely") identifies the Cretans' proverbial dishonesty. The association of Crete with lying was widespread in the ancient Mediterranean: the verb kretizein ("to act like a Cretan, to lie") was a recognized idiom. The Cretans' own claim that Zeus's tomb was on Crete (a claim that scandalized Greeks who believed Zeus was immortal) was cited as a prime example of Cretan mendacity. Callimachus, the third-century BCE poet, wrote: "Cretans are always liars; for a tomb, O Lord, Cretans built for you; but you did not die, for you are forever" (Hymn to Zeus 8-9).</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">"Evil beasts"</hi> (kaka theria). The adjective kakos ("evil, bad, harmful") modifies therion ("wild animal, beast"). The charge depicts the Cretans as savage, brutal, operating at the level of animal instinct rather than human reason. The association of Crete with bestial behavior may connect to the island's mythological associations — the Minotaur, the beast of the labyrinth, was Crete's most famous mythological figure.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">"Lazy gluttons"</hi> (gasteres argai). The noun gaster ("stomach, belly") used metonymically describes a person governed by appetite. The adjective argos ("idle, lazy, inactive, useless") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) and ergon ("work"). The combination describes persons who are driven by appetite but unwilling to work — consumers who produce nothing, devourers who contribute nothing. Paul uses gaster similarly in Romans 16:18: "such persons do not serve our Lord Christ, but their own belly (te heauton koilia), and by smooth talk and flattery they deceive the hearts of the naive." The stomach-as-master is a figure for the life governed by physical appetite rather than higher purposes.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"This testimony is true"</hi> (he martyria haute estin alethes). Paul endorses the quotation. The noun martyria ("testimony, witness") treats the Cretan prophet's saying as evidence. The adjective alethes ("true, genuine, real") affirms it. Paul does not cite the saying to ridicule an entire ethnic group for its own sake but to characterize the cultural milieu in which the false teachers operate. The false teachers are Cretans (or are operating among Cretans), and the character traits described by their own prophet — dishonesty, brutishness, appetite-driven laziness — are visible in the false teachers' behavior. They teach for shameful gain (appetite-driven laziness = lazy gluttons; shameful gain = governed by the belly), they teach what they ought not (deceitfulness = always liars), and they upset whole households (destructiveness = evil beasts). The Cretan prophet's diagnosis fits the false teachers' symptoms.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Therefore rebuke them sharply, that they may be sound in the faith"</hi> (di' hen aitian elenche autous apotomos, hina hygiainosin en te pistei). The phrase di' hen aitian ("for which reason, on this account") connects the rebuke to the preceding diagnosis. The verb elenchein ("to rebuke, to reprove, to refute, to convict") repeats from verse 9 — there, the elder was to rebuke opponents; here, Titus himself is to perform the rebuking until elders are established. The adverb apotomos ("sharply, severely, curtly") is composed of apo ("away from") + temnein ("to cut"). The rebuke is to cut sharply, leaving no room for ambiguity or diplomatic evasion. In 2 Corinthians 13:10, Paul uses the related adverb apotomōs: "I write these things while absent, in order that when present I may not have to be severe (apotomōs)." The severity of the rebuke corresponds to the severity of the situation: gentle correction would be inadequate when whole households are being overturned.</p>
          <p>The purpose of the rebuke is stated positively: hina hygiainosin en te pistei — "that they may be sound in the faith." The verb hygiainein ("to be healthy, to be sound, to be in good condition") returns the medical metaphor to the center of the argument. The false teachers and those they influence are sick — their teaching is diseased, their behavior is symptomatic. The rebuke aims at health. The goal is not punishment for its own sake but restoration to soundness in the faith (en te pistei). The preposition en ("in") locates the health within the sphere of faith: it is specifically their faith that needs to be restored to health. The fact that the purpose clause exists at all — "that they may be sound" — indicates that Paul has not given up on these people. They can still be corrected. The sharp rebuke serves a restorative purpose, like a surgeon's cut that serves healing.</p>
          <p>The pronoun autous ("them") is important. Who is being rebuked? In the immediate context, "them" could refer to the false teachers of verse 10 or to the broader Cretan community that has been influenced by them. The purpose clause "that they may be sound in the faith" points more naturally toward the community members who are being led astray: the false teachers are to be silenced (v. 11), but the misled community members are to be rebuked toward health. Both actions serve the same end: the protection and restoration of the community's faith.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Not devoting themselves to Jewish myths and the commands of people who turn away from the truth"</hi> (me prosechontes Ioudaikois mythois kai entolais anthropon apostrephomonon ten aletheian). The verb prosechein ("to give heed to, to devote oneself to, to pay attention to") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:4 in the identical construction: "nor to devote themselves to myths and endless genealogies." The parallel is deliberate and connects the two situations: the false teaching in Ephesus and the false teaching in Crete share the same root problem, even if the specific manifestations differ.</p>
          <p>The myths are now specified: they are Ioudaikois mythois — "Jewish myths." The adjective Ioudaikos ("Jewish, pertaining to the Jews") identifies the cultural and religious origin of these stories. They are not Greek myths or Roman myths but elaborations of Jewish scriptural and traditional narratives. As noted in the analysis of 1 Timothy 1:4, the most natural referent is the elaborate midrashic and haggadic tradition that grew around the biblical narratives in the Second Temple period — speculative stories about patriarchs, angels, cosmic origins, and hidden wisdom that were built on the scaffolding of the biblical text but went far beyond its actual content. The Book of Jubilees, the Testament of the Twelve Patriarchs, portions of 1 Enoch, and similar works represent the kind of elaborate narrative speculation that Paul considers mythoi — tales that, however interesting, distract from the gospel's core content.</p>
          <p>The second element, entolais anthropon apostrephomonon ten aletheian — "commands of people who turn away from the truth" — identifies a specific type of teaching: human commands that function as substitutes for divine truth. The noun entole ("command, commandment, instruction") is the word used for God's commandments throughout the LXX (Exodus 20:6; Deuteronomy 5:10; Psalm 19:8 LXX [18:9]; Psalm 119:96 LXX [118:96]). But these commands are not God's; they are anthropon — "of humans, of people." The genitive anthropon identifies the source: human beings, not God, are the originators of these commands.</p>
          <p>The participial phrase apostrephomonon ten aletheian ("who turn away from the truth") further characterizes these human commanders: they are people in the process of turning away from the truth. The verb apostrephein ("to turn away from, to turn one's back on") in the middle/passive describes a self-directed action: they are turning themselves away from the truth. Their commands are the product of their apostasy — they have turned from the truth and are now generating substitute commands that lead others in the same direction.</p>
          <p>The phrase echoes Jesus' critique of the Pharisees in Mark 7:7-8 (quoting Isaiah 29:13): "In vain do they worship me, teaching as doctrines the commandments of men (entalmatā anthrōpōn). You leave the commandment of God and hold to the tradition of men (tēn paradosin tōn anthrōpōn)." The parallel is precise: human commands that replace divine truth, traditions of human origin that claim divine authority. Paul's critique of the Cretan false teachers follows the pattern Jesus established for critiquing Torah-based tradition that obscures rather than serves God's purpose.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To the pure, all things are pure, but to the defiled and unbelieving, nothing is pure; but both their minds and their consciences are defiled"</hi> (panta kathara tois katharois; tois de memiammenois kai apistois ouden katharon, alla memiantal auton kai ho nous kai he syneidesis). This is one of the most dense and consequential statements in the chapter. It functions as the theological principle that undercuts the false teachers' program.</p>
          <p>The first clause, panta kathara tois katharois — "all things are pure to the pure" — is a compressed principle with far-reaching implications. The adjective katharos ("clean, pure, undefiled") in the LXX carries both ritual and moral connotations. Ritually, the clean/unclean distinction governed Israel's life: clean and unclean foods (Leviticus 11), clean and unclean bodily conditions (Leviticus 12-15), clean and unclean persons (Numbers 19), clean and unclean objects. Morally, the clean heart is the undivided heart that is oriented toward God (Psalm 51:10 LXX [50:12]). In this statement, Paul collapses the distinction between the external and the internal: for the person who is katharos (clean, pure), all external things are katharos. The purity of the person determines the purity of the thing encountered. This principle operates in the same sphere as Jesus' teaching in Mark 7:14-23, where Jesus declares: "There is nothing outside a person that by going into him can defile him, but the things that come out of a person are what defile him" (Mark 7:15). The source of impurity is not the external object but the internal condition.</p>
          <p>The relevance to the false teachers' program is immediate. If the false teachers are imposing food laws, purity regulations, or other external commands ("commands of people who turn away from the truth," v. 14), this principle undercuts their foundation: purity is a matter of the person, not of the thing. External regulations cannot produce internal purity. For the person already cleansed — cleansed by God's action in Christ, cleansed through faith — external things do not contaminate.</p>
          <p>Paul uses a similar principle in Romans 14:14, 20: "I know and am persuaded in the Lord Jesus that nothing is unclean in itself, but it is unclean for anyone who thinks it unclean" (14:14); "Everything is indeed clean, but it is wrong for anyone to make another stumble by what he eats" (14:20). In 1 Timothy 4:3-4: "[They] require abstinence from foods that God created to be received with thanksgiving by those who believe and know the truth. For everything created by God is good, and nothing is to be rejected if it is received with thanksgiving." The consistency across Paul's letters establishes this as a fundamental principle: the creation is good, and the believer's relationship to creation is governed by internal purity, not external regulation.</p>
          <p>The second clause introduces the contrast: tois de memiammenois kai apistois ouden katharon — "but to the defiled and unbelieving, nothing is pure." The perfect passive participle memiammenois (from miainein, "to stain, to defile, to pollute, to corrupt") describes persons who have been defiled and remain in that state. The adjective apistois ("unbelieving, faithless, without faith") adds the theological dimension: the defilement is connected to unbelief. The combination is deliberate: defilement and unbelief travel together. For such persons, ouden katharon — "nothing is pure." No amount of external regulation, dietary restriction, or ritual observance can produce purity for a person whose interior is defiled. The false teachers may insist on food laws and purity codes, but if their minds and consciences are defiled, their elaborate external observances accomplish nothing. They are polishing the outside of a cup while the inside remains filthy (cf. Matthew 23:25-26; Luke 11:39-40).</p>
          <p>The final clause specifies the location of the defilement: alla memiantal auton kai ho nous kai he syneidesis — "but both their mind and their conscience are defiled." Two faculties are named:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">The mind</hi> (ho nous). The nous is the faculty of understanding, reason, and judgment — the capacity by which a person evaluates, discerns, and makes decisions. In Romans 12:2: "be transformed by the renewal of your mind (tou noos)." In Romans 1:28, those who did not honor God were given up to "a debased mind (adokimon noun)." When the nous is defiled, the capacity to think rightly, to evaluate truth, to discern good from evil is corrupted. The defilement of the mind explains why the false teachers cannot see the truth even when it is presented to them: their instrument of perception is itself contaminated.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">The conscience</hi> (he syneidesis). The syneidesis is the moral awareness, the inner witness, the faculty that evaluates one's own conduct. In 1 Timothy 1:19, the rejection of "good conscience" was the mechanism by which faith was destroyed. In 1 Timothy 4:2, false teachers have "their consciences seared with a hot iron." Here, the conscience is memiantal — defiled, stained, polluted. A defiled conscience can no longer accurately distinguish right from wrong. It has lost its calibration. The person with a defiled conscience may feel justified in what he does precisely because the faculty that should accuse him has been compromised.</p>
          <p>The defilement of both mind and conscience constitutes a comprehensive internal corruption. The mind cannot think rightly; the conscience cannot judge rightly. External purity regulations cannot remedy what is fundamentally an internal problem. This is the theological ground on which Paul opposes the false teachers' program: they prescribe external remedies for an internal disease. Their commands about food, purity, and circumcision address the symptom (contact with things deemed impure) while ignoring the disease (a defiled mind and conscience that can only be cleansed by faith and the work of God).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"They profess to know God, but they deny him by their works"</hi> (theon homologousin eidenai, tois de ergois arnountai). The verb homologein ("to confess, to profess, to declare publicly, to acknowledge") describes their verbal claim: they say they know God. The verb eidenai ("to know") is the perfect infinitive of oida — it denotes settled knowledge, intimate acquaintance. Their profession is not timid or hesitant; they claim thorough knowledge of God.</p>
          <p>The adversative de ("but") introduces the contradiction: tois ergois arnountai — "by their works they deny him." The verb arneisthai ("to deny, to repudiate, to disown, to refuse to acknowledge") is the direct opposite of homologein. What the mouth confesses, the life denies. The dative tois ergois ("by their works, through their deeds") identifies the instrument of denial: their actual conduct, their observable behavior. The contrast between profession and practice is absolute: they say one thing and do another. Their verbal confession of knowledge is contradicted by their visible conduct.</p>
          <p>This principle — that works reveal the true content of a person's faith — has deep roots in Israel's wisdom tradition and in Jesus' own teaching. In Proverbs, the wise person is known by his actions, not merely by his words. In Jesus' teaching in Matthew 7:15-20: "You will recognize them by their fruits... every healthy tree bears good fruit, but the diseased tree bears bad fruit." In Matthew 7:21-23: "Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven." In James 2:17: "So also faith by itself, if it does not have works, is dead." The principle operates here as a diagnostic tool: the false teachers' profession of knowing God is evaluated not by its verbal content but by its practical fruit. Their works deny what their words affirm.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"They are detestable, disobedient, unfit for any good work"</hi> (bdelyktos ontes kai apeitheis kai pros pan ergon agathon adokimoi). Three adjectives deliver the final verdict:</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Detestable</hi> (bdelyktos). The adjective bdelyktos ("abominable, detestable, loathsome") appears only here in the New Testament. It is related to the noun bdelygma ("abomination, detestable thing"), which in the LXX is one of the strongest words for what God finds repulsive. In Deuteronomy 7:25-26: "The carved images of their gods you shall burn with fire... You shall not bring an abominable thing (bdelygma) into your house... You shall utterly detest (bdelygma bdelykse) it." In Proverbs 11:20 (LXX): "Crooked of mind are an abomination (bdelygma) to the LORD." In Proverbs 15:8-9 (LXX): "The sacrifices of the ungodly are an abomination (bdelygma) to the LORD"; "the ways of the ungodly are an abomination (bdelygma) to the LORD." The word carries the full weight of covenant revulsion: what is bdelyktos is what God finds repugnant. The irony is devastating. These people who profess to know God, who impose purity regulations and food laws in the name of godliness, are themselves bdelyktos — detestable to the God they claim to serve. The pursuit of external purity has produced internal abomination.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Disobedient</hi> (apeitheis). The adjective apeithes ("disobedient, not persuaded, stubborn, unbelieving") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) and peithein ("to persuade, to obey"). The apeithes person is one who will not be persuaded, who resists instruction, who refuses to obey. The word connects back to anypotaktoi ("insubordinate") in verse 10: the false teachers began as insubordinate and remain disobedient. In Romans 1:30, apeitheis appears among the characteristics of those whom God has given over to a debased mind. In Acts 26:19, Paul tells Agrippa: "I was not disobedient (apeithes) to the heavenly vision" — the implication being that disobedience to divine revelation was a real and terrible possibility that Paul consciously refused.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Unfit for any good work</hi> (pros pan ergon agathon adokimoi). The adjective adokimos ("not standing the test, failing the test, unqualified, disqualified, rejected upon examination") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) and dokimos ("tested, approved, proven, standing the test"). The word describes someone who has been tested and found wanting — like a coin examined for purity and discovered to be counterfeit, or an athlete subjected to qualification trials and found inadequate. In 1 Corinthians 9:27, Paul writes: "I discipline my body and keep it under control, lest after preaching to others I myself should be disqualified (adokimos)." In 2 Corinthians 13:5-7, Paul urges the Corinthians to test themselves: "Examine yourselves, to see whether you are in the faith. Test yourselves. Or do you not realize this about yourselves, that Jesus Christ is in you? — unless indeed you fail to meet the test (adokimoi)!" In Romans 1:28, God gives up those who reject him "to a debased mind (adokimon noun)" — using the same root. The false teachers have been subjected to the test of works — the test that examines whether profession matches practice — and they have failed. They are adokimoi: disqualified, rejected, counterfeit.</p>
          <p>The sphere of their disqualification is specified: pros pan ergon agathon — "for every good work." The preposition pros ("toward, for, with respect to") with the accusative indicates fitness or readiness for something. The adjective pas ("every, all, each") makes the disqualification comprehensive: not merely unfit for some good works but for every good work. The phrase ergon agathon ("good work") will become a major theme of the rest of the letter. In 2:7, Titus is to show himself "a model of good works" (typon kalon ergon). In 2:14, Christ "gave himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for himself a people for his own possession who are zealous for good works" (zeloten kalon ergon). In 3:1, the community is to be "ready for every good work" (pros pan ergon agathon hetoimos). In 3:8, those who have believed in God are to "devote themselves to good works" (kalon ergon proistasthai). In 3:14, "our people must learn to devote themselves to good works" (kalon ergon proistasthai). The entire letter is oriented toward the production of good works as the visible evidence of genuine faith. The false teachers, who profess to know God but deny him by their works, are the anti-model — the negative demonstration of what happens when profession replaces practice, when verbal knowledge replaces transformed conduct.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.1">
          <title>Summary of the Chapter's Argument</title>
          <p>Titus 1 establishes the complete foundation for the letter's mission: organizing the Cretan communities, appointing qualified leaders, and confronting the false teachers who are destroying the communities from within. Each section builds on the preceding one and prepares for what follows.</p>
          <p>(1) <hi type="bold">Verses 1-4</hi> compress the theological framework of the entire letter into the greeting. Paul's dual identity — servant of God, apostle of Jesus Christ — roots his authority in both Israel's history and Christ's commission. His apostleship serves two ends: the faith of God's elect and the knowledge of the truth that produces godliness. These two ends rest on the hope of eternal life, which was promised by the God who cannot lie before time began and has now been manifested through preaching. The greeting establishes the standard against which everything in the letter will be measured: truth that accords with godliness, knowledge that produces faithful living, a promise grounded in God's unchangeable character. The alternation of the title "Savior" between God (v. 3) and Christ (v. 4) announces the theological unity that the letter will maintain throughout.</p>
          <p>(2) <hi type="bold">Verses 5-9</hi> describe the structural response to the crisis. Titus was left in Crete to complete what Paul began, and the primary task is the appointment of elders in every town. The elder qualifications address character (above reproach, faithful in marriage, children who are believers), temperament (not arrogant, not quick-tempered, not violent), habits (not a drunkard, not greedy), and virtues (hospitable, lover of good, self-controlled, upright, holy, disciplined). The qualifications culminate in the single most important requirement: the elder must hold firm to the trustworthy word as taught, so that he can both teach sound doctrine and refute opponents. This final qualification is the hinge between the elder section and the false teacher section: the elders are needed because opponents exist, and the opponents are the subject of what follows.</p>
          <p>(3) <hi type="bold">Verses 10-16</hi> identify, diagnose, and condemn the false teachers. They are insubordinate, empty talkers, and mind-deceivers, drawn especially from the circumcision party. They must be silenced because they are overturning whole households by teaching forbidden things for profit. A quotation from the Cretans' own prophet — "Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons" — is endorsed as an accurate description of the cultural soil in which the false teaching grows. The community is to be rebuked sharply, with the goal of restoring them to health in the faith — specifically by turning them away from Jewish myths and human commands. The theological principle "to the pure all things are pure" undercuts the false teachers' program of external regulation by locating purity in the person rather than in the thing. And the final verdict exposes the fundamental contradiction: these people profess to know God but deny him by their works. They are detestable, disobedient, and disqualified for every good work.</p>
          <p>The chapter's architecture is built around the contrast between genuine and counterfeit knowledge of God. Genuine knowledge produces godliness (1:1, kat' eusebeian). It rests on God's truthful promise (1:2, ho apseudes theos). It is transmitted through sound teaching (1:9, te didaskalia te hygiainouse). It is guarded by qualified leaders of tested character (1:5-8). It produces good works (1:8, philagathon; 1:16, ergon agathon by implication of contrast). Counterfeit knowledge, by contrast, issues from Jewish myths and human commands (1:14). It overturns households (1:11). It operates from defiled minds and consciences (1:15). It confesses God verbally while denying him practically (1:16). And it produces not good works but the disqualification from every good work (1:16). The test that distinguishes the genuine from the counterfeit is not the verbal profession but the visible conduct — not "they profess to know" but "by their works they deny." The chapter makes the case that the communities in Crete need leaders who embody genuine knowledge of God — leaders whose lives, not merely whose words, demonstrate the truth they teach. The false teachers have words without corresponding works; the elders must have both.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="Titus.2">
        <title>Titus 2 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.2">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (Titus 1:10-16):</hi> The closing section of chapter 1 identified and condemned the false teachers operating in the Cretan communities. These are "especially those of the circumcision party" (1:10), who teach "Jewish myths and the commands of people who turn away from the truth" (1:14). Their teaching is characterized as empty talk and deception (1:10), their motive as shameful gain (1:11), and their effect as the overturning of whole households (1:11). The section concluded with a devastating verdict: "They profess to know God, but they deny him by their works. They are detestable, disobedient, unfit for any good work" (1:16). The contrast between verbal profession and actual conduct — between claiming to know God and denying him through behavior — was established as the diagnostic test for genuine versus counterfeit faith.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after (Titus 3:1-8):</hi> Chapter 3 opens by extending the behavioral instructions outward, from the internal life of the community to its relationship with the surrounding civic order: "Remind them to be submissive to rulers and authorities, to be obedient, to be ready for every good work, to speak evil of no one, to avoid quarreling, to be gentle, and to show perfect courtesy toward all people" (3:1-2). This is followed by a theological statement (3:3-7) that parallels the one in 2:11-14 — both describe the appearing of God's saving action and its transformative effect on believers. Where 2:11-14 speaks of "the grace of God" appearing and "training us," 3:4-7 speaks of "the goodness and loving kindness of God our Savior" appearing and saving us "by the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Holy Spirit." Both theological statements ground behavioral instruction in God's prior action. And 3:8 draws the conclusion: "so that those who have believed in God may be careful to devote themselves to good works."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 2 connects both:</hi> Chapter 2 is the hinge of the entire letter. It answers the question that chapter 1 posed: if the false teachers' program of external regulation and verbal profession produces detestable, disobedient people unfit for any good work (1:16), what does sound teaching produce? The answer is chapter 2: sound teaching produces specific, observable, transformed conduct across every social category — older men, older women, younger women, younger men, and slaves — all ordered toward the same qualities of self-control, dignity, and visible goodness. The chapter then provides the theological foundation for this transformed conduct (2:11-14): the grace of God itself is the agent of transformation, training believers to live in a specific way while they await a specific hope. Chapter 3 will then extend this same pattern outward to civic life and ground it in a second theological statement about God's saving mercy. The structure is: false teaching produces bad works (ch. 1) —&gt; sound teaching produces good works (ch. 2) —&gt; the theological ground of the transformation (2:11-14; 3:3-7) —&gt; the extension to all of life (ch. 3). Chapter 2 supplies both the practical content and the theological engine.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.2.1">
          <title>Verse 1: The Pivot — From False Teaching to Sound Doctrine</title>
          <p>"But as for you, teach what accords with sound doctrine."</p>
          <p>(Sy de lalei ha prepei te hygiainouse didaskalia.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The false teachers profess to know God but deny him by their works (1:16). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> The specific content of sound doctrine is spelled out in the instructions to older men, older women, younger women, younger men, and slaves (2:2-10). <hi type="bold">How this verse connects the two:</hi> The emphatic pronoun sy ("you") and the adversative conjunction de ("but") create a sharp pivot. The preceding section described what the false teachers do and are. Now: "But you" — Titus, in contrast to them. The structure is identical to 1 Timothy 6:11, where after describing the false teachers' love of money, Paul wrote: "But as for you, O man of God, flee these things" (sy de, o anthrope theou, tauta pheuge). The sy de construction draws a line between Titus and the false teachers and places Titus on the opposite side.</p>
          <p>The verb lalein ("to speak, to talk, to say, to utter") is a broader word than didaskein ("to teach") — it covers any kind of speaking, including informal instruction, conversation, and exhortation. The choice of lalein rather than didaskein may reflect the diverse contexts in which Titus will deliver this instruction: not only in formal teaching settings but in personal conversations, household visits, and community gatherings.</p>
          <p>The relative clause ha prepei te hygiainouse didaskalia — "what is fitting for sound doctrine" or "what accords with healthy teaching" — specifies the content. The verb prepein ("to be fitting, to be suitable, to be proper, to be becoming") describes appropriateness: what Titus teaches must be suitable to, consistent with, and produced by sound doctrine. The implication is that doctrine has practical consequences — sound doctrine produces fitting behavior, and fitting behavior is the visible evidence of sound doctrine. The word prepein will reappear in verse 10, where slaves are to "adorn the doctrine of God our Savior" — the same logic of doctrine expressing itself in visible, fitting conduct.</p>
          <p>The adjective hygiainousa ("sound, healthy, in good condition"), the present active participle of hygiainein ("to be healthy"), returns the medical metaphor that has run throughout the letter. In 1:9, the elder must be able to instruct in "sound doctrine" (te didaskalia te hygiainouse). In 1:13, the sharp rebuke aims at restoring the community so they may be "sound in the faith" (hygiainosin en te pistei). The participle is in the present tense, indicating ongoing health: the teaching is continually sound, perpetually healthy. It is not a teaching that was once healthy and has since declined; it is a teaching that maintains its health because it comes from the trustworthy word (1:9). The medical metaphor implies that false teaching is a disease and sound teaching is both the cure and the condition of health. The instructions that follow (2:2-10) are what healthy teaching looks like when it enters the life of a community.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.2.2-Titus.2.10">
          <title>Verses 2-10: Behavioral Instructions by Social Category</title>
          <title level="2">Verse 2: Older Men</title>
          <p>"Older men are to be sober-minded, dignified, self-controlled, sound in faith, in love, and in steadfastness."</p>
          <p>(Presbyteras nephalious einai, semnous, sophronas, hygiainontas te pistei, te agape, te hypomone.)</p>
          <p>The noun presbyteras ("older men") here is not the same word as presbyterous ("elders") in 1:5, though both derive from the same root presbyteros ("older, elder"). The context determines the difference: in 1:5, presbyterous designated an office to be filled by appointment (katasteses, "appoint"); here, presbyteras designates a demographic category — the older men of the community, regardless of whether they hold the office of elder. The instruction addresses their character as community members, not their qualifications for office.</p>
          <p>Six qualities are prescribed:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Sober-minded</hi> (nephalious). The adjective nephalios ("sober, temperate, clear-headed") in its primary sense describes abstention from excessive wine. In 1 Timothy 3:2, it appeared among the overseer qualifications; in 1 Timothy 3:11, it was required of the women (whether deaconesses or deacons' wives). The word carries both a literal and a metaphorical sense: the nephalios person is both free from intoxication and clear in judgment. He is not muddled by excess. In a community where the false teachers are described as "evil beasts, lazy gluttons" (1:12), the older men are to exhibit the opposite: clarity and restraint.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Dignified</hi> (semnous). The adjective semnos ("worthy of respect, dignified, honorable, serious, reverent") describes a person whose conduct commands respect. In 1 Timothy 3:8, deacons must be semnous; in 1 Timothy 3:11, women must be semnas. In Philippians 4:8, whatever is semnon is among the things the community should think about. The word implies gravity without grimness — a life that carries weight, that is taken seriously, that commands esteem not by demanding it but by deserving it. The older men are to be the kind of people whose presence in the community lends it stability and authority.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(3) Self-controlled</hi> (sophronas). The adjective sophron ("of sound mind, self-controlled, temperate, prudent, moderate") appeared in 1:8 among the positive elder qualifications. It is a keynote term of this chapter, appearing or being echoed in nearly every social category: sophronas here for older men; sophronizein in verse 4 for what older women teach younger women; sophronein in verse 6 for younger men; sophronos in verse 12 for how the grace of God trains believers to live. The word family (sophron, sophronein, sophroneo, sophrosyne, sophronismos, sophronizo) saturates this chapter more densely than any other section of the Pauline corpus. The concentration is deliberate: self-control — the mastery of one's own impulses, desires, and reactions — is the central behavioral theme of Paul's instruction for Crete. Given the portrait of the Cretan character drawn in 1:12 ("always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons"), self-control is precisely the antidote: the capacity to govern oneself against the cultural currents of deception, brutishness, and appetite.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(4-6) Sound in faith, in love, and in steadfastness</hi> (hygiainontas te pistei, te agape, te hypomone). The participle hygiainontas ("being sound, being healthy") applies the medical metaphor directly to the older men's inner life. They are to be healthy — not merely appearing healthy but actually being healthy — in three domains:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Faith</hi> (pistis) — their trust in God and in the gospel. <hi type="bold">Love</hi> (agape) — their active care for others, the self-giving orientation that defines the community's life. <hi type="bold">Steadfastness</hi> (hypomone) — their capacity to endure, to persist, to remain under pressure without breaking. The noun hypomone ("endurance, patient endurance, perseverance, steadfastness") is composed of hypo ("under") + menein ("to remain, to stay, to abide"). It describes the quality of remaining under a burden or trial without abandoning one's position. In Romans 5:3-4, Paul writes: "suffering produces endurance (hypomonen), and endurance produces character (dokimen), and character produces hope." In Romans 15:4-5: "through endurance (tes hypomones) and through the encouragement of the Scriptures we might have hope." In James 1:3-4: "the testing of your faith produces steadfastness (hypomonen). And let steadfastness (hypomone) have its full effect, that you may be perfect and complete, lacking in nothing."</p>
          <p>The triad of faith, love, and steadfastness parallels the well-known Pauline triad of faith, love, and hope (1 Thessalonians 1:3; 1 Corinthians 13:13; Colossians 1:4-5), but with hypomone ("steadfastness/endurance") replacing elpis ("hope"). The substitution is not accidental. Hope is the future orientation; steadfastness is the present practice that hope makes possible. The older men are called to endurance — the daily, sustained, unglamorous persistence that keeps faith and love active over time. In a community facing the disruption of false teachers and the pressures of a hostile cultural environment, the steadfastness of the older men provides an anchor.</p>
          <p>The fact that the older men must be "sound" (hygiainontas) in these three qualities implies that unsoundness is possible. Faith, love, and steadfastness can become diseased — weakened by false teaching, eroded by cultural compromise, or simply worn down by time and difficulty. The medical metaphor treats these as conditions requiring ongoing health, not achievements to be accomplished once and forgotten.</p>
          <title level="2">Verses 3-5: Older Women and Their Ministry to Younger Women</title>
          <p>"Older women likewise are to be reverent in behavior, not slanderers or slaves to much wine. They are to teach what is good, and so train the young women to love their husbands and children, to be self-controlled, pure, working at home, kind, and submissive to their own husbands, that the word of God may not be reviled."</p>
          <p>(Presbyteridas hosautos en katastemati hieroprepeis, me diabolous, me oino pollo dedoulomenas, kalodidaskalous, hina sophronizousin tas neas philandrous einai, philoteknous, sophronas, hagnas, oikourgous, agathas, hypotassomenas tois idiois andrasin, hina me ho logos tou theou blasphemetai.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Older women</hi> (presbyteridas). The feminine form of presbyteros in its demographic sense: older women in the community, not holders of an office.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Likewise</hi> (hosautos). The adverb hosautos ("in the same way, likewise, similarly") connects the instruction for older women to that for older men: they too are held to a standard of conduct befitting sound doctrine, just as the older men are.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Reverent in behavior</hi> (en katastemati hieroprepeis). The noun katastema ("deportment, demeanor, bearing, outward appearance and conduct") describes the overall manner in which a person carries herself — her composure, bearing, and visible conduct taken as a whole. The adjective hieroprepes ("befitting a sacred person, reverent, suitable to a holy place") is composed of hieros ("sacred, holy, belonging to the divine") + prepein ("to be fitting, to be proper"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. It describes conduct that would be appropriate in a sacred context — the bearing of a person who lives as though she inhabits holy space. The compound implies that the older woman's entire life is a sacred sphere: her behavior in everyday settings should have the quality of behavior appropriate to a temple. This does not mean artificial solemnity but the kind of seriousness, dignity, and purposefulness that reflects an awareness of God's presence in all of life.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Not slanderers</hi> (me diabolous). The adjective diabolos ("slanderous, given to malicious gossip") is the same word used as a noun for "the devil" or "the accuser" (ho diabolos). In its adjectival use, it describes a person who accuses others falsely, who spreads destructive talk, who damages reputations through speech. In 1 Timothy 3:11, the same qualification appeared for women: me diabolous ("not slanderers"). In 2 Timothy 3:3, the word appears in the vice list of the last days. The older women's speech must not be destructive. In a community where false teachers are already spreading harmful talk (1:10-11), the older women must not compound the damage with malicious gossip. Their speech, like their behavior, must be hieroprepeis — fitting for the sacred.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Not slaves to much wine</hi> (me oino pollo dedoulomenas). The perfect passive participle dedoulomenas (from douloun, "to enslave, to make a slave of, to bring into bondage") describes a settled condition of enslavement: they have been enslaved and remain enslaved to much wine. The metaphor is strong — wine is not merely consumed but has become a master. The dative oino pollo ("to much wine") specifies the enslaver. The qualification echoes the elder's requirement of not being paroinos ("given to wine," 1:7) but uses a stronger image: not merely given to wine but enslaved by it. The qualification is practical: in the Greco-Roman world, wine was consumed at virtually every meal and social gathering, and the line between moderate use and excess was easily crossed. For the older women, who are about to be charged with training younger women, the requirement has particular force: a person enslaved to wine cannot be trusted with the formation of others.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Teachers of what is good</hi> (kalodidaskalous). The compound adjective kalodidaskalos ("teacher of good things, teacher of what is noble and excellent") is composed of kalos ("good, noble, beautiful, fine, excellent") + didaskalos ("teacher"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. It designates a specific role for the older women: they are to be teachers, and the content of their teaching is to kalon — what is good, noble, and excellent. This is not teaching in the formal sense of doctrinal instruction in the assembly but the formation of character through personal example, mentoring, and practical guidance. The older women have a teaching ministry specifically directed toward the younger women (verse 4), and the content of this teaching is specified in what follows.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">So that they may train the young women</hi> (hina sophronizousin tas neas). The verb sophronizein ("to bring to one's senses, to train in self-control, to teach moderation, to encourage, to advise") is a causative form from the sophron root: to make someone sophron, to produce self-control in another person. The word appears only here in the New Testament. The hina ("so that, in order that") clause makes this the purpose of the older women's character and teaching role: they are to possess the qualities listed above so that they can form the younger women in self-control and wisdom. The younger women are tas neas — "the young women," using the general term neos ("young, new, fresh") rather than a more specific designation.</p>
          <p>The content of the training is specified in a list of seven qualities:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) To love their husbands</hi> (philandrous einai). The adjective philandros ("husband-loving, affectionate to one's husband") is composed of philos ("loving, fond of") + aner ("husband, man"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. In Greek usage, philandros described the devoted wife, the woman whose primary relational commitment was to her husband. The quality is listed first, establishing the marital relationship as the primary sphere of the young woman's relational life.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) To love their children</hi> (philoteknous). The adjective philoteknos ("child-loving, loving one's children") is composed of philos ("loving") + teknon ("child"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. The quality extends the relational commitment from husband to children. The order is deliberate: husband first, then children — the same order as the household structure in which the family operates.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(3) Self-controlled</hi> (sophronas). The adjective sophron returns for the younger women, just as it appeared for the older men (v. 2) and the elders (1:8). The repetition reinforces the centrality of this quality across every category. The younger women need the same self-mastery as every other group.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(4) Pure</hi> (hagnas). The adjective hagnos ("pure, chaste, innocent, morally pure") describes moral and sexual purity. In 2 Corinthians 11:2, Paul writes: "I betrothed you to one husband, to present you as a pure virgin (parthenon hagnen) to Christ." In Philippians 4:8, hagna is among the qualities believers are to contemplate. In 1 Timothy 5:22: "Keep yourself pure (hagnon)." The adjective describes a life free from moral contamination, particularly in the sphere of sexual conduct. For young married women, the qualification points to marital faithfulness and the avoidance of the sexual laxity that characterized much of Greco-Roman society.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(5) Working at home</hi> (oikourgous). The adjective oikourgos ("working at home, caring for the household, domestic") is composed of oikos ("house, household") + ergon ("work"). Some manuscripts read oikourous ("keeper of the home," from oikos + ouros, "guardian, keeper"). In either reading, the quality describes active engagement with the domestic sphere — not idleness but productive work within the household. The ancient household was an economic unit, not merely a residence: the wife's management of the household was a substantial economic and social contribution. Proverbs 31:10-31 describes the valiant woman whose household management includes commerce, textile production, agriculture, and charitable activity — all conducted from and through the household. The qualification is not about confinement to a physical space but about industrious engagement with the sphere of responsibility entrusted to the young wife.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(6) Kind</hi> (agathas). The adjective agathos ("good, kind, generous, beneficial") in this context describes a disposition of kindness and goodness toward others. The young women are to be characterized by active benevolence, not merely by the absence of malice.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(7) Submissive to their own husbands</hi> (hypotassomenas tois idiois andrasin). The present middle/passive participle hypotassomenas (from hypotassein, "to place under, to arrange under, to subordinate") describes a voluntary ordering — the placement of oneself under the authority structure of the household. The reflexive pronoun idiois ("their own") specifies that this submission is within the particular marriage relationship, not a general submission of all women to all men. The verb hypotassein in the middle voice carries the sense of voluntary arrangement: the young wife places herself within the household order. The same instruction appears in Ephesians 5:22 and Colossians 3:18. In each case, the context is the ordered household as a unit within the larger community.</p>
          <p>The instruction is given within the first-century household structure (the Greco-Roman oikos) where the household was the basic building block of society, and the relationship between husband and wife was embedded within a larger framework of mutual obligations. Paul's parallel instructions to husbands in Ephesians 5:25-33 (to love their wives as Christ loved the church) and Colossians 3:19 (to love their wives and not be harsh with them) indicate that the submission is not one-directional servitude but part of a mutual structure. In the present context, however, the instruction is given only to the wives through the older women's training, and the purpose clause that follows will explain why.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"That the word of God may not be reviled"</hi> (hina me ho logos tou theou blasphemetai). The hina me ("in order that not, lest") clause provides the purpose for the entire set of instructions to the younger women. The verb blasphemein ("to slander, to revile, to speak evil of, to defame, to blaspheme") describes hostile, contemptuous speech directed against something sacred. The subject of this blasphemy is ho logos tou theou — "the word of God." The concern is missionary and apologetic: the conduct of the community's members either commends or discredits the gospel message. If young women in the community behave in ways that the surrounding society regards as dishonorable, shameful, or subversive of social order, the result will be that outsiders speak evil of the word of God itself. The gospel will be blamed for producing bad behavior.</p>
          <p>This concern has precedent. In Isaiah 52:5 (LXX): "Because of you my name is continually blasphemed (blasphemeitai) among the nations." In Romans 2:24, Paul quotes this same passage: "The name of God is blasphemed among the Gentiles because of you." In 2 Samuel 12:14, Nathan tells David: "Because by this deed you have utterly scorned the LORD, the child who is born to you shall die" — David's conduct gave occasion for the enemies of YHWH to blaspheme. The pattern is consistent: the conduct of God's people either honors or dishonors God's name among those who observe them. The purpose clause reveals that Paul's behavioral instructions are not arbitrary social conservatism but strategic: the community's conduct is its primary testimony to the watching world, and conduct that provokes contempt from outsiders undermines the gospel's credibility. The younger women's faithfulness in their domestic roles serves the larger mission by keeping the word of God from being reviled.</p>
          <title level="2">Verses 6-8: Younger Men and Titus as Model</title>
          <p>"Likewise, urge the younger men to be self-controlled. Show yourself in all respects to be a model of good works, and in your teaching show integrity, dignity, and sound speech that cannot be condemned, so that an opponent may be put to shame, having nothing evil to say about us."</p>
          <p>(Tous neoterous hosautos parakalei sophronein, peri panta seauton parechomenos typon kalon ergon, en te didaskalia aphthorian, semnodeta, logon hygie akatagnodston, hina ho ex enantias entrape meden echon legein peri hemon phaulon.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Younger men</hi> (tous neoterous). The comparative adjective neoteros ("younger") designates the younger men of the community, the demographic counterpart to the presbyteras ("older men") of verse 2.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Likewise</hi> (hosautos). Again the connecting adverb: the younger men, like the older men, older women, and younger women, are subject to instruction that accords with sound doctrine.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Urge</hi> (parakalei). The verb parakalein ("to exhort, to urge, to encourage, to appeal to, to comfort") appeared in 1:9 as part of the elder's dual task: "to exhort with sound doctrine and to refute those who contradict." Titus is now doing what the elders will eventually be responsible for: exhorting with sound doctrine. The present imperative indicates ongoing action: Titus is to keep urging them.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">To be self-controlled</hi> (sophronein). The infinitive of sophronein ("to be of sound mind, to be self-controlled, to think soberly, to exercise moderation") is the verbal form of the adjective sophron that has appeared in every preceding category. The instruction to the younger men is strikingly compressed: where the older men received six qualities and the younger women received seven, the younger men receive one — self-control. This compression may reflect the fact that self-control encompasses all the other qualities: the young man who masters himself will naturally exhibit the sobriety, dignity, and soundness that characterize the older men. It may also reflect the particular vulnerability of younger men to impulsive, excessive, or reckless behavior — the single quality they most need is the one that governs all others.</p>
          <p>The concentration of the sophron word family in this chapter is now fully visible: sophronas for older men (v. 2); sophronizein for what older women do for younger women (v. 4); sophronas for younger women (v. 5); sophronein for younger men (v. 6); and sophronos for how the grace of God trains believers to live (v. 12). Self-control is the thread that runs through every social category and culminates in the theological statement that attributes it to divine grace. It is not merely a human virtue to be cultivated through willpower; it is the product of God's grace operating in the community through sound teaching.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Show yourself in all respects to be a model of good works"</hi> (peri panta seauton parechomenos typon kalon ergon). The verb parechesthai ("to show, to present, to furnish, to provide, to offer") in the middle voice means "to present yourself, to show yourself, to make yourself available as." The reflexive pronoun seauton ("yourself") and the universal qualifier peri panta ("in all things, in every respect") make the instruction comprehensive: Titus is to present himself in every area of life as a typon — "a model, a pattern, a type, an example."</p>
          <p>The noun typos ("type, pattern, model, example, impression") in its primary sense is the mark left by a stamp or a blow — the impression in wax or clay that reproduces the shape of the die. By extension, it came to mean "pattern, model" — the thing that others reproduce by imitation. In Philippians 3:17, Paul writes: "Brothers, join in imitating me, and keep your eyes on those who walk according to the example (typon) you have in us." In 1 Thessalonians 1:7: "so that you became an example (typon) to all the believers in Macedonia and in Achaia." In 1 Timothy 4:12: "set the believers an example (typos) in speech, in conduct, in love, in faith, in purity." The typos is not merely a verbal instructor but a living demonstration — a person whose life reproduces the pattern that others are to follow.</p>
          <p>The model is specified as kalon ergon — "good works" or "noble deeds." The adjective kalos ("beautiful, fine, noble, good, excellent") in the Pastoral Epistles describes works that are not merely morally correct but visibly admirable — works that can be seen and appreciated by observers. In 1 Timothy 3:1, the overseer's task is called kalon ergon. In 1 Timothy 5:10, a widow is evaluated by her kala erga. In 1 Timothy 5:25, good works (ta erga ta kala) "are conspicuous, and even those that are not cannot remain hidden." In 1 Timothy 6:18, the rich are to "be rich in good works" (ergois kalois). The phrase kalon ergon connects directly to the verdict on the false teachers in 1:16: they are "unfit for any good work" (pros pan ergon agathon adokimoi). Titus is to be the living opposite: a model of the very good works for which the false teachers are disqualified.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And in your teaching show integrity, dignity"</hi> (en te didaskalia aphthorian, semnodeta). Two qualities are required of Titus's teaching:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Integrity</hi> (aphthorian). The noun aphthoreia (or aphthoreia/aphthoria, "incorruptness, purity, integrity") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) and phtheirein ("to corrupt, to destroy, to ruin"). Teaching that has aphthoreia is teaching that has not been corrupted — not adulterated, not mixed with impurities, not diluted with false elements. The word implies that teaching can be corrupted — and that the false teachers' teaching has been corrupted. Titus's teaching must be the uncorrupted original, free from the contamination of Jewish myths and human commands (1:14).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Dignity</hi> (semnodeta). The noun semnoteta (semnotes, "dignity, seriousness, gravity, respectability") is the abstract noun from semnos ("worthy of respect, dignified"), which appeared in verse 2 for older men. The teaching itself must carry weight and gravity — it must be delivered with the seriousness that its content demands. Frivolous, casual, or careless teaching dishonors the word it conveys.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And sound speech that cannot be condemned"</hi> (logon hygie akatagnodston). The noun logos ("word, speech, message") here refers to Titus's actual discourse — the words he uses when he teaches. The adjective hygies ("sound, healthy, whole") is the adjective form of the verb hygiainein that has characterized "sound doctrine" throughout the letter. The word connects Titus's speech to the medical metaphor: his words are to be healthy, free from disease. The adjective akatagnostos ("not able to be condemned, beyond reproach, unable to be censured") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) + katagignoskein ("to condemn, to find fault with, to pass judgment against"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. Titus's speech is to be of such quality that no legitimate criticism can be brought against it — no factual errors, no logical fallacies, no ethical failures, no contradictions with practice.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So that an opponent may be put to shame, having nothing evil to say about us"</hi> (hina ho ex enantias entrape meden echon legein peri hemon phaulon). The purpose clause reveals the strategic dimension of Titus's modeling and teaching. The phrase ho ex enantias ("the one from the opposite side, the opponent, the adversary") describes anyone who opposes the community and its message — whether the false teachers within or hostile observers without. The verb entrepein ("to put to shame, to make ashamed, to cause to turn in upon oneself") in the passive describes the opponent's intended experience: he is to be shamed, turned back on himself, made to realize the emptiness of his criticism. And the means of his shaming is specified: meden echon legein peri hemon phaulon — "having nothing evil to say about us." The adjective phaulon ("evil, base, worthless, bad") describes speech that is harmful or contemptible. The opponent is silenced not by argument alone but by the absence of material for accusation. When Titus's life and teaching are beyond reproach, the opponent finds nothing to attack.</p>
          <p>The pronoun hemon ("us") is worth noting. Paul shifts from second person singular (you, Titus) to first person plural (us). The shift may indicate that Paul includes himself and the broader apostolic circle in the scope of what the opponent might criticize: Titus's conduct reflects not only on himself but on the entire mission. Alternatively, "us" may encompass the whole community: the opponent's inability to find fault with Titus's teaching removes a potential weapon against the entire community.</p>
          <title level="2">Verses 9-10: Slaves — Conduct That Adorns the Doctrine</title>
          <p>"Bondservants are to be submissive to their own masters in everything; they are to be well-pleasing, not argumentative, not pilfering, but showing all good faith, so that in everything they may adorn the doctrine of God our Savior."</p>
          <p>(Doulous idiois despotais hypotassesthai, en pasin euarestous einai, me antilegontas, me nosphizomenous, alla pasan pistin endeiknumenous agathen, hina ten didaskleian ten tou soteros hemon theou kosmousin en pasin.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Bondservants</hi> (doulous). The noun doulos ("slave, bondservant, servant") designates persons in the condition of slavery — a fundamental and pervasive institution in the first-century Greco-Roman world. Estimates suggest that slaves constituted between 15% and 35% of the population of Roman provinces. Slavery was not racially based in the Roman system; persons became slaves through war, debt, birth to enslaved parents, exposure of infants, or self-sale. Slaves served in every capacity from agricultural labor to household management, from tutoring to accounting. The early communities included both slaves and masters (Philemon 10-16; 1 Corinthians 7:21-22; Colossians 3:22-4:1; Ephesians 6:5-9; 1 Timothy 6:1-2). The inclusion of slaves among the social categories receiving instruction indicates that they are full members of the community whose conduct matters as much as anyone else's.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">To be submissive to their own masters</hi> (idiois despotais hypotassesthai). The verb hypotassesthai ("to submit, to be subject to, to place oneself under") in the middle/passive infinitive describes the same voluntary ordering that appeared for the young wives (v. 5). The dative idiois despotais ("to their own masters") specifies the relationship. The noun despotes ("master, lord, owner") designates the slaveholder — the person who has legal authority over the slave. The adjective idiois ("their own") specifies: each slave to his or her own master, not a general submission to all masters. The same instruction appeared in 1 Timothy 6:1: "Let all who are under a yoke as bondservants regard their own masters (tous idious despotas) as worthy of all honor." The instruction is practical: the slave's daily life is lived under the authority of the despotes, and the conduct of the slave in that relationship is the primary sphere in which his or her faith becomes visible.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">In everything</hi> (en pasin). The comprehensive scope: the submission covers all areas of the slave's service, not merely selected duties.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Well-pleasing</hi> (euarestous). The adjective euarestos ("well-pleasing, acceptable, satisfying") is composed of eu ("well, good") + arestos ("pleasing, acceptable"). In Paul's usage, the word frequently describes what is pleasing to God: Romans 12:1, "present your bodies as a living sacrifice, holy and acceptable (euareston) to God"; Romans 14:18, "whoever thus serves Christ is acceptable (euarestos) to God and approved by men"; Ephesians 5:10, "try to discern what is pleasing (euareston) to the Lord"; Colossians 3:20, "Children, obey your parents in everything, for this pleases (euareston) the Lord." Here the word describes the slave's relationship to the human master, but the echoes of divine-pleasing conduct are audible: the slave who serves the earthly master well is simultaneously serving in a way that pleases God.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Not argumentative</hi> (me antilegontas). The present participle of antilegein ("to speak against, to contradict, to argue, to oppose verbally") describes a disposition of verbal resistance. The same verb appeared in 1:9 (tous antilegontas, "those who contradict" — the false teachers). The slave is not to exhibit the same argumentative spirit that characterizes the opponents of sound doctrine. The word does not prohibit all speech but describes the habitual disposition of talking back, contradicting, challenging every instruction.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Not pilfering</hi> (me nosphizomenous). The present middle participle of nosphizein ("to misappropriate, to embezzle, to steal by setting aside for oneself, to pilfer") describes the practice of secretly taking from the master's goods for oneself. The verb appears in Acts 5:2-3, where Ananias and Sapphira "kept back" (enosphisato) part of the proceeds from the sale of their property — the same verb for secret misappropriation. In the LXX, the verb appears in Joshua 7:1 for Achan's taking of devoted things (enosphisanto apo tou anathematos). The instruction is practical: slaves had daily access to their masters' property and the temptation to take small amounts was constant. Pilfering damages the master, violates trust, and — in the context of this passage — discredits the gospel.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">But showing all good faith</hi> (alla pasan pistin endeiknumenous agathen). The adversative alla ("but, rather, on the contrary") introduces the positive counterpart. The verb endeiknysthai ("to show, to demonstrate, to display, to prove") in the middle voice means "to show forth from oneself, to demonstrate by one's own conduct." What is demonstrated is pasan pistin agathen — "all good faith" or "complete good faithfulness." The noun pistis here functions in its sense of "faithfulness, trustworthiness, fidelity" rather than "belief." The adjective agathen ("good") and the adjective pasan ("all, complete, total") together describe a comprehensive, thorough trustworthiness: the slave is to be demonstrably reliable in every respect. The master who employs a believing slave should find that slave more trustworthy, not less — more reliable, not less — more faithful in every duty, not less.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So that in everything they may adorn the doctrine of God our Savior"</hi> (hina ten didaskalian ten tou soteros hemon theou kosmousin en pasin). The purpose clause for the slaves' conduct matches the purpose clause for the younger women (v. 5, "that the word of God may not be reviled") but states the purpose positively rather than negatively. Where verse 5 aimed to prevent blasphemy, verse 10 aims to produce adornment.</p>
          <p>The verb kosmein ("to arrange, to put in order, to decorate, to adorn, to make beautiful") is the verb from which the noun kosmos ("order, adornment, world, universe") derives. The word describes the act of making something beautiful through arrangement, decoration, or embellishment. In Matthew 23:29, the Pharisees "adorn (kosmeite) the tombs of the righteous." In Matthew 25:7, the virgins "trimmed (ekosmesan) their lamps." In 1 Peter 3:5, "the holy women... used to adorn (ekosmoun) themselves." In 1 Timothy 2:9, women are to "adorn (kosmein) themselves in respectable apparel." The image is striking: the slave's conduct adorns the doctrine. Doctrine is treated as something visible that can be either adorned or disfigured by the conduct of those who profess it. The slave who is submissive, well-pleasing, non-argumentative, honest, and thoroughly faithful makes the doctrine look beautiful — puts it on display in its best possible form.</p>
          <p>The object adorned is ten didaskalian ten tou soteros hemon theou — "the doctrine of God our Savior." The full title tou soteros hemon theou ("of God our Savior") reappears from 1:3. The doctrine that the slaves adorn is not a human philosophy or social program but the teaching that comes from God himself — the God who saves. The effect is that the slave, occupying the lowest position in the social hierarchy, becomes the bearer of the gospel's public beauty. The most powerful apologetic for the doctrine is not the eloquence of its teachers but the transformed conduct of its most socially invisible adherents. When a slave who has every reason to be resentful, dishonest, and resistant instead demonstrates complete faithfulness, kindness, and willing service, the doctrine that produced that transformation is adorned.</p>
          <p>The phrase en pasin ("in everything, in all things") bookends the instruction: slaves are to submit "in everything" (en pasin, v. 9) and to adorn the doctrine "in everything" (en pasin, v. 10). The comprehensiveness of the scope matches the comprehensiveness of the claim: the doctrine shapes all of life, and the slave's entire conduct serves the doctrine's display.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.2.11-Titus.2.14">
          <title>Verses 11-14: The Theological Ground — The Grace of God Has Appeared</title>
          <p>"For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation for all people, training us to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age, waiting for our blessed hope, the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ, who gave himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for himself a people for his own possession who are zealous for good works."</p>
          <p>(Epephane gar he charis tou theou soterios pasin anthropois, paideuousa hemas hina arnesamenoi ten asebeian kai tas kosmikas epithymias sophronos kai dikaios kai eusebos zesomen en to nyn aioni, prosdechomenoi ten makarian elpida kai epiphaneian tes doxes tou megalou theou kai soteros hemon Iesou Christou, hos edoken heauton hyper hemon hina lytrousetai hemas apo pases anomias kai katharise heauto laon periousion zelouten kalon ergon.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The behavioral instructions for every social category (2:2-10), each oriented toward visible conduct that commends the gospel. <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> Titus is to "declare these things; exhort and rebuke with all authority" (2:15), then chapter 3 extends the behavioral instructions to civic life (3:1-2) and provides a second theological grounding statement (3:3-7). <hi type="bold">How this section connects the two:</hi> Verses 11-14 provide the theological engine for the behavioral instructions that precede them and the continued instructions that follow. The conjunction gar ("for") at the beginning makes this explicit: the reason the community members should live as described in 2:2-10 is that the grace of God has appeared and is actively training them. The behavioral instructions are not arbitrary moral requirements imposed from outside but the natural product of God's grace operating from within. The theological statement transforms the preceding social instructions from human ethics into the fruit of divine action.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 11: The Appearing of Grace</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation for all people"</hi> (Epephane gar he charis tou theou soterios pasin anthropois).</p>
          <p>The verb epiphainein ("to show forth, to appear, to become visible, to manifest") in the aorist passive (epephane) describes a past event: something that was not previously visible has now come into view. The verb shares its root with the noun epiphaneia ("appearing, manifestation"), which will appear in verse 13 for the future appearing of Christ. The two uses create a frame: there has been a first appearing (the grace of God has appeared) and there will be a second appearing (the glory of our great God and Savior, v. 13). The community lives between these two epiphanies.</p>
          <p>The concept of divine epiphany was well known in the first-century world. In Greek religion, an epiphaneia was the visible manifestation of a deity — a god appearing to humans in perceptible form. In the LXX, the language of appearing is used for God's self-disclosure: Exodus 3:2, the angel of the LORD "appeared" (ophthe) to Moses in the burning bush; Genesis 12:7, "The LORD appeared (ophthe) to Abram." The Feast of Hanukkah was called the feast of the Epiphaneia because it celebrated God's manifest intervention through the Maccabees (2 Maccabees 15:27). In the Roman imperial cult, the emperor's visit to a city was an epiphaneia — a visitation of the divine ruler. Paul's use of the term operates on multiple levels: it draws on Israel's tradition of divine visitation, and it implicitly contrasts the appearing of God's grace with the epiphanies claimed for emperors and pagan deities.</p>
          <p>What has appeared is he charis tou theou — "the grace of God." The noun charis ("grace, favor, kindness, gift, goodwill") in Paul's usage consistently describes God's unmerited favor — the disposition of God toward humanity that gives what humanity has not earned and cannot deserve. Romans 3:24: "being justified by his grace (te autou chariti) as a gift." Romans 5:15: "the free gift in the grace (te chariti) of the one man Jesus Christ." Ephesians 2:8-9: "For by grace (te chariti) you have been saved through faith. And this is not your own doing; it is the gift of God, not a result of works." Grace in Paul is not merely God's attitude but God's action — the concrete expression of divine favor in the events of salvation.</p>
          <p>The grace is described as soterios — "saving, bringing salvation, having the power to save." The adjective soterios ("saving, delivering, preservative") is related to soter ("savior") and soteria ("salvation"). The grace that has appeared is characterized by its saving purpose: it comes to deliver, to rescue, to bring soteria.</p>
          <p>The scope is specified: pasin anthropois — "for all people" or "to all humanity." The dative pasin ("all, every") with anthropois ("humans, people") indicates the universality of the grace's reach. The grace of God has appeared not for one ethnic group, one social class, or one demographic category but for all people. This is precisely the point of the preceding social instructions: older men, older women, younger women, younger men, slaves — the grace reaches every category because it is for all people. The universal scope of grace is also the theological refutation of the false teachers' narrowing program: they impose circumcision and Jewish regulations on the community, restricting access to God's favor; but the grace of God has appeared for all people, without such restrictions.</p>
          <p>The same universality appeared in 1 Timothy 2:4: God "desires all people (pantas anthropous) to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth." And in 1 Timothy 4:10: God "is the Savior of all people (panton anthropon), especially of those who believe." The consistency is deliberate: the grace of God is universally available, extending to every person without ethnic or social precondition.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 12: The Training Function of Grace</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Training us to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age"</hi> (paideuousa hemas hina arnesamenoi ten asebeian kai tas kosmikas epithymias sophronos kai dikaios kai eusebos zesomen en to nyn aioni).</p>
          <p>The present active participle paideuousa ("training, educating, instructing, disciplining") from paideuein ("to train a child, to educate, to discipline, to instruct, to correct") describes the ongoing activity of grace. The verb paideuein has a rich background. In Greek usage, it described the education and formation of a child (pais) — the comprehensive process of shaping character, instilling knowledge, and training in proper behavior. In the LXX, paideuein takes on the additional connotation of divine discipline: Proverbs 3:11-12 (LXX): "My son, do not despise the discipline (paideias) of the Lord... for the Lord disciplines (paideuei) the one he loves, as a father the son in whom he delights." Deuteronomy 8:5: "Know then in your heart that, as a man disciplines (paideuei) his son, the LORD your God disciplines (paideusei) you." The paideia of the Lord is formative: it shapes, corrects, and trains the person toward maturity and right conduct.</p>
          <p>The subject of paideuousa is he charis — "grace." This is a remarkable statement. Grace is not merely the initial act of forgiveness or the entry point into relationship with God. Grace is an active, ongoing trainer. It educates. It disciplines. It shapes behavior. The grace that "appeared" (v. 11) in a past event continues to operate in the present as a formative force in the lives of believers. The present tense of the participle (paideuousa, "training") indicates continuous action: grace keeps training, keeps educating, keeps forming.</p>
          <p>The object of the training is hemas — "us." The pronoun is first person plural, including Paul, Titus, and the entire community. Grace trains all of them, not just the leaders or the spiritually mature. Every social category addressed in 2:2-10 is included in the "us" whom grace is training.</p>
          <p>The content of the training is twofold — a negative renunciation and a positive manner of life:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(A) Renunciation: "to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions"</hi> (arnesamenoi ten asebeian kai tas kosmikas epithymias). The aorist middle participle arnesamenoi (from arneisthai, "to deny, to renounce, to refuse, to repudiate") describes a decisive act of denial — not gradual reduction but definitive renunciation. The same verb appeared in 1:16, where the false teachers "deny" (arnountai) God by their works. Now the believers are called to deny something: asebeian and kosmikas epithymias. The verb creates a deliberate contrast: the false teachers deny God; the believers deny ungodliness. The act of denial that the false teachers direct toward God, the believers redirect toward what is contrary to God.</p>
          <p>The noun asebeia ("ungodliness, impiety, irreverence, lack of proper worship") is the direct opposite of eusebeia ("godliness, piety"), which was the letter's goal from the greeting (1:1, "the truth that accords with godliness"). What is renounced is the entire orientation of life away from God — the fundamental irreverence that treats God as irrelevant, unimportant, or nonexistent in practical terms. The false teachers exemplify asebeia: they profess to know God but live as though God's actual requirements do not apply to them.</p>
          <p>The phrase tas kosmikas epithymias ("worldly passions, worldly desires") identifies the specific manifestations of ungodliness. The adjective kosmikos ("belonging to the world, worldly, of this present order") describes desires that are shaped by and oriented toward the present world-order — the system of values, priorities, and appetites that operates apart from God's purposes. The noun epithymia ("desire, longing, craving, lust, passion") in Paul's usage typically describes desires that pull a person away from God's purposes: Romans 1:24, "God gave them up in the lusts (epithymiais) of their hearts"; Galatians 5:16, "do not gratify the desires (epithymian) of the flesh"; Galatians 5:24, "those who belong to Christ Jesus have crucified the flesh with its passions and desires (epithymiais)." The phrase does not condemn all desire but identifies desires that are kosmikai — shaped by the world-system rather than by God's purpose.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(B) Positive manner of life: "to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age"</hi> (sophronos kai dikaios kai eusebos zesomen en to nyn aioni). The aorist subjunctive zesomen ("we might live") describes the manner of life that grace produces. Three adverbs specify its character:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Self-controlled</hi> (sophronos). The adverb of sophron — the word that has dominated this entire chapter. Self-control is now revealed as not merely a human achievement but the product of grace's training. When grace educates, the result is sophrosyne — the mastery of one's own impulses and desires.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Upright</hi> (dikaios). The adverb of dikaios ("just, righteous, fair"). Living justly — treating others rightly, honoring obligations, acting with fairness.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(3) Godly</hi> (eusebos). The adverb of eusebes ("godly, pious, devout"). Living with proper reverence toward God — the eusebeia that was the letter's goal from 1:1. The three adverbs address three relationships: sophronos governs the relationship to oneself (self-mastery); dikaios governs the relationship to others (justice); eusebos governs the relationship to God (reverence). Together they constitute a complete ethic — a life properly ordered in every direction.</p>
          <p>The phrase en to nyn aioni ("in the present age") specifies the temporal context. The noun aion ("age, era, epoch") in Jewish and early Christian usage describes a period of time characterized by certain conditions. The "present age" (ho nyn aion) is the age before the final consummation — the time in which the community lives after the first appearing of grace but before the second appearing of glory (v. 13). The phrase "in the present age" accomplishes two things: it acknowledges that the present conditions are not final (there is an age to come), and it insists that the present conditions are not irrelevant (godly living is required now, in this age, not deferred to the future). The community does not wait passively for the age to come; it lives actively in the present age according to the pattern grace has taught.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 13: The Blessed Hope — Awaiting the Second Appearing</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Waiting for our blessed hope, the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ"</hi> (prosdechomenoi ten makarian elpida kai epiphaneian tes doxes tou megalou theou kai soteros hemon Iesou Christou).</p>
          <p>The present middle participle prosdechomenoi ("waiting for, expecting, looking forward to, welcoming") from prosdechesthai ("to receive, to wait for, to expect, to look forward to") describes the community's posture as it lives in the present age: it is a community in active expectation. The participle is present tense, indicating ongoing waiting — not a one-time act of hope but a sustained orientation toward a future event. In Mark 15:43, Joseph of Arimathea was "waiting for the kingdom of God" (prosdechomenos ten basileian tou theou). In Luke 2:25, Simeon was "waiting for the consolation of Israel" (prosdechomenos paraklesin tou Israel). In Luke 2:38, Anna spoke of Jesus to "all who were waiting for the redemption of Jerusalem" (tois prosdechomenois lutrosin Ierousalem). The verb describes the posture of faithful Israel — the patient, expectant waiting for God to fulfill his promises. The community in Crete shares this posture: they live in the present age, but their orientation is toward what God will do next.</p>
          <p>What they await is described with two nouns joined by kai ("and"):</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) The blessed hope</hi> (ten makarian elpida). The noun elpis ("hope, expectation, confident anticipation") describes not a vague wish but a confident expectation grounded in God's promise. In the greeting (1:2), Paul's apostleship rests ep' elpidi zoes aioniou — "on the hope of eternal life." The hope of the greeting is now revisited: the community's daily life is oriented toward this same hope. The adjective makarios ("blessed, happy, fortunate, to be envied") describes the hope as blessed — it is a source of deep happiness, a ground of congratulation. The elpis is makaria because what it expects is certain: it rests on the promise of the God who cannot lie (1:2).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) The appearing of the glory</hi> (epiphaneian tes doxes). The noun epiphaneia ("appearing, manifestation, visible display") echoes the verb epephane ("appeared") in verse 11, creating the framework of two appearances. The first epiphaneia was the appearing of grace (v. 11, past). The second epiphaneia is the appearing of glory (v. 13, future). Between these two appearances, the community lives, trains, renounces, and waits. The noun doxa ("glory, splendor, brightness, radiance, magnificence, honor") in the LXX translates the Hebrew kavod ("weight, glory, honor") and describes the visible manifestation of God's presence and power. The kavod of YHWH appeared as fire on Sinai (Exodus 24:17), filled the tabernacle (Exodus 40:34-35), and filled the temple (1 Kings 8:10-11). The glory of God is God's presence made visible. The community awaits the moment when the glory that is presently hidden will become visible — the full, public manifestation of God's splendor.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ"</hi> (tou megalou theou kai soteros hemon Iesou Christou). This phrase is among the most theologically dense in the letter. The grammatical structure — a single article (tou) governing both megalou theou ("great God") and soteros hemon ("our Savior"), with Iesou Christou ("Jesus Christ") standing in apposition to the whole — most naturally reads as attributing both titles to one person: Jesus Christ is "our great God and Savior." This reading follows a well-established Greek grammatical pattern (the Granville Sharp rule): when two substantives of the same case are connected by kai and governed by a single article, they typically refer to the same person. This is how a first-century Greek speaker would most naturally parse the phrase.</p>
          <p>The title megalou theou ("great God") echoes the LXX and its descriptions of Israel's God. Deuteronomy 10:17: "For the LORD your God is God of gods and Lord of lords, the great God (ho theos ho megas), mighty and awesome." Psalm 95:3 (LXX 94:3): "For the LORD is a great God (theos megas), and a great King above all gods." Daniel 2:45 (LXX): "The great God (ho theos ho megas) has made known to the king what shall be after this." Nehemiah 8:6: "And Ezra blessed the LORD, the great God (ton theon ton megan)." The application of this language to Jesus Christ continues the pattern established in the greeting (1:3-4), where the title soter ("savior") was applied to both God and Christ. Throughout the letter, the titles of Israel's God flow to Christ: Savior (1:4; 2:13; 3:6), and now great God (2:13). The alternation between "God our Savior" (1:3; 2:10; 3:4) and "Jesus Christ our Savior" (1:4; 3:6) or "our great God and Savior Jesus Christ" (2:13) creates a theological unity in which Christ participates in the identity and activity of Israel's God.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 14: The Purpose of Christ's Self-Giving</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Who gave himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for himself a people for his own possession who are zealous for good works"</hi> (hos edoken heauton hyper hemon hina lytrousetai hemas apo pases anomias kai katharise heauto laon periousion zelouten kalon ergon).</p>
          <p>The relative pronoun hos ("who") refers back to Jesus Christ. The verb edoken ("he gave") is the aorist of didonai ("to give"). The reflexive pronoun heauton ("himself") specifies what was given: Christ gave himself. The preposition hyper ("for, on behalf of, for the sake of") with the genitive hemon ("us") specifies the beneficiaries: the giving was for us, on our behalf. In Galatians 1:4, Paul writes: "who gave himself (tou dontos heauton) for our sins to deliver us from the present evil age." In Galatians 2:20: "the Son of God, who loved me and gave himself (paradondos heauton) for me." In Ephesians 5:25: "Christ loved the church and gave himself up (heauton paredoken) for her." In 1 Timothy 2:6: "who gave himself (ho dous heauton) as a ransom for all." The language of self-giving is consistent across the Pauline corpus: Christ's death is understood as a voluntary act of giving himself on behalf of others.</p>
          <p>The purpose of this self-giving is stated with a hina clause containing two infinitives:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) "To redeem us from all lawlessness"</hi> (lytrousetai hemas apo pases anomias). The verb lytrousthai ("to redeem, to ransom, to set free by paying a price, to liberate") in the middle voice means "to ransom for himself." The word carries profound resonance from the LXX and Israel's history. The verb lytrousthai and the related noun lytrosis ("redemption, ransom, deliverance") are the primary vocabulary for God's liberation of Israel from Egypt. Deuteronomy 7:8: "the LORD redeemed you (elytrosato hymas) out of the house of slavery." Deuteronomy 13:5 (LXX 13:6): "the LORD your God, who redeemed you (ton lytrousamemon se) out of the land of Egypt." Psalm 130:8 (LXX 129:8): "And he will redeem (lytrosetai) Israel from all his iniquities (ek pason ton anomion autou)." The parallel between Psalm 130:8 (LXX 129:8) and this verse is striking: the Psalm says God will redeem Israel from all his iniquities; Paul says Christ gave himself to redeem us from all lawlessness. The language of redemption from Egypt is applied to redemption from anomia.</p>
          <p>The noun anomia ("lawlessness, iniquity, wickedness, transgression of law") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) + nomos ("law"). Anomia describes the condition of living without law — not merely breaking specific commands but existing in a state of fundamental disregard for God's ordering of life. In Matthew 7:23, Jesus says to those who claim to have prophesied and cast out demons in his name: "Depart from me, you workers of lawlessness (hoi ergazomenoi ten anomian)." In Matthew 24:12: "because lawlessness (anomian) will be increased, the love of many will grow cold." In Romans 6:19: "just as you once presented your members as slaves to impurity and to lawlessness (te anomia) leading to more lawlessness (anomian), so now present your members as slaves to righteousness leading to sanctification." In 2 Thessalonians 2:7: "the mystery of lawlessness (tes anomias) is already at work."</p>
          <p>The adjective pases ("all, every") makes the redemption comprehensive: not from some lawlessness but from all lawlessness. The redemption is total. Christ's self-giving addresses the entire condition of anomia, not merely its individual manifestations. This connects to the false teachers' situation: they profess to know God but are "disobedient" (apeitheis, 1:16) and "unfit for any good work" (1:16) — they exhibit the very anomia from which Christ's self-giving redeems. Redemption from lawlessness is not merely forensic (declared free from penalty) but transformative (actually delivered from the condition of lawlessness into a new pattern of life).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) "And to purify for himself a people for his own possession who are zealous for good works"</hi> (kai katharise heauto laon periousion zelouten kalon ergon). The second purpose of Christ's self-giving is purification. The verb katharizein ("to cleanse, to purify, to make clean") in the LXX describes both ritual purification (Leviticus 14:7, the cleansing of a leper; Numbers 8:6-7, the purification of the Levites) and moral cleansing (Psalm 51:2 LXX [50:4]: "Wash me thoroughly from my iniquity, and cleanse me [katharison me] from my sin"). The verb connects to the purity theme of 1:15: "To the pure, all things are pure." Where the false teachers have defiled minds and consciences (1:15) and attempt to achieve purity through external regulations, Christ's self-giving accomplishes actual purification. The cleansing is real, not ceremonial; it reaches the nous and the syneidesis that the false teachers' program cannot touch.</p>
          <p>The reflexive dative heauto ("for himself") specifies the beneficiary: Christ purifies this people for himself. The people belong to Christ as a result of the purification. This echoes the covenantal language of the LXX, where God takes Israel as his own people.</p>
          <p>The phrase laon periousion — "a people for his own possession" or "a special people" — is one of the most important allusions in the letter. The adjective periousios ("chosen, special, one's own particular possession, set apart as property") appears in the LXX at Exodus 19:5: "you shall be to me a people periousios (laos periousios) from all the nations" — the same phrase. Deuteronomy 7:6: "For you are a people holy to the LORD your God. The LORD your God has chosen you to be a people for his treasured possession (laon periousion), out of all the peoples who are on the face of the earth." Deuteronomy 14:2: "For you are a people holy to the LORD your God, and the LORD has chosen you to be a people for his treasured possession (laon periousion), out of all the peoples who are on the face of the earth." Deuteronomy 26:18: "the LORD has declared today that you are a people for his treasured possession (laon periousion), as he has promised you."</p>
          <p>The phrase laon periousion is a signature designation for Israel's covenant identity: Israel is God's special, treasured, chosen people, set apart from all nations as his own. By applying this phrase to the community of believers in Christ — a community that includes Gentile Cretans, slaves, and members of every social category — Paul makes an extraordinary claim: the community formed by Christ's self-giving is the laos periousios, the people of God's own possession. The identity that belonged to Israel at Sinai now belongs to those whom Christ has redeemed and purified. This is not the replacement of Israel but the fulfillment of the promise contained in the Exodus covenant: God said he would have a people for his own, and through Christ's self-giving, he does. The inclusion of Gentiles in this people is precisely what the circumcision party (1:10) resists, and precisely what the gospel, as Paul understands it, accomplishes.</p>
          <p>The final qualification of this people is zelouten kalon ergon — "zealous for good works." The adjective zelotes ("zealot, zealous person, one who burns with zeal") describes passionate, active commitment. In Acts 21:20, the Jewish believers in Jerusalem are described as "all zealous for the law" (zelotai tou nomou). In Acts 22:3, Paul describes himself as "zealous for God" (zelotes hyparchon tou theou). In Galatians 1:14, Paul was "extremely zealous for the traditions of my fathers" (perissoteros zelotes hyparchon ton patrikon mou paradoseon). The noun carries the sense of burning, passionate devotion — not tepid approval but active, eager pursuit. And the object of this zeal is kalon ergon — "good works," the same phrase that appeared in verse 7 (Titus as a model of "good works") and that contrasts with the false teachers' disqualification from "every good work" (1:16).</p>
          <p>The entire purpose statement of verse 14 thus answers the question the letter has been building toward: what does Christ's self-giving accomplish? It accomplishes two things. First, it redeems from lawlessness — it delivers people from the condition of living apart from God's purposes. Second, it purifies a people for Christ's own possession — a people whose identity echoes Israel's covenant identity at Sinai, and whose character is defined by zealous pursuit of good works. The false teachers' program of external regulation and verbal profession cannot produce this people; only Christ's self-giving can. The behavioral instructions of 2:2-10 are not moral bootstrapping; they are the visible manifestation of what Christ's redemption and purification have produced — a people who live the way they live because they have been redeemed from lawlessness and purified for Christ's own possession.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.2.15">
          <title>Verse 15: The Charge to Titus</title>
          <p>"Declare these things; exhort and rebuke with all authority. Let no one disregard you."</p>
          <p>(Tauta lalei kai parakalei kai elenche meta pases epitages; medeis sou periphroneitō.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The theological ground of transformed living — Christ's redemptive self-giving (2:11-14). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> The extension of behavioral instruction to civic life — submission to rulers, readiness for every good work, gentleness toward all people (3:1-2). <hi type="bold">How this verse connects the two:</hi> Verse 15 is a hinge, closing the instructions of chapter 2 and authorizing Titus to continue with what follows in chapter 3. The verse does not introduce new content but empowers the delivery of all the content that has preceded and will follow.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Declare these things"</hi> (tauta lalei). The demonstrative pronoun tauta ("these things") refers back to everything in the chapter: the behavioral instructions (2:2-10) and the theological ground (2:11-14). The verb lalein ("to speak, to say, to talk") returns from verse 1: there, Titus was to "speak what accords with sound doctrine"; here, the instruction is to keep speaking "these things" — the specific content that accords with sound doctrine has now been supplied, and Titus is to deliver it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Exhort and rebuke with all authority"</hi> (parakalei kai elenche meta pases epitages). Two verbs specify the modes of Titus's speaking:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Exhort</hi> (parakalei). The verb parakalein ("to encourage, to urge, to exhort, to appeal to") describes the positive dimension: Titus is to encourage the community toward the behaviors and attitudes described in the chapter.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Rebuke</hi> (elenche). The verb elenchein ("to refute, to reprove, to convict, to expose") describes the confrontational dimension: Titus is to confront those who resist or contradict. The same verb appeared in 1:9 (the elder must "rebuke those who contradict") and 1:13 ("rebuke them sharply"). The combination of parakalei and elenche — encouragement and confrontation — captures the full scope of Titus's pastoral responsibility. Sound doctrine requires both positive instruction for the willing and firm correction for the resistant.</p>
          <p>The phrase meta pases epitages ("with all authority") specifies the manner of Titus's speech. The noun epitage ("command, order, decree, authority, injunction") appeared in 1:3, where Paul's preaching was entrusted to him kat' epitagen tou soteros hemon theou — "by the command of God our Savior." The connection is deliberate: the authority with which Titus speaks is not his own but derives from the same chain of command that authorized Paul. God commanded Paul; Paul commissioned Titus; Titus speaks with the full weight of that delegated authority. The adjective pases ("all, full, complete") indicates that Titus is not to hold back or dilute his authority: he is to exercise the full authority that has been given to him.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Let no one disregard you"</hi> (medeis sou periphroneitō). The verb periphronein ("to look down on, to despise, to disregard, to think around or past") is composed of peri ("around") + phronein ("to think, to have in mind"). The word appears only here in the New Testament. It describes the act of thinking past someone, looking around them as though they were not there — dismissal through disregard rather than active opposition. The present imperative with medeis ("let no one") is a prohibition: no one in the community is to treat Titus with this kind of contemptuous dismissal.</p>
          <p>The parallel in 1 Timothy 4:12 is instructive: "Let no one despise (kataphroneitō) you for your youth." Timothy's potential liability was his youth; the verb used there, kataphronein ("to look down on, to think against"), describes active contempt. For Titus, the verb periphronein suggests a different dynamic: the risk is not contempt but dismissal — the community might simply ignore Titus's authority, looking past him rather than opposing him directly. The instruction authorizes Titus to insist on being heard: his message carries the authority of the apostolic commission, and the community cannot afford to look past it.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.2">
          <title>Summary of the Chapter's Argument</title>
          <p>Titus 2 answers the question that chapter 1 posed: if false teaching produces people who are "detestable, disobedient, unfit for any good work" (1:16), what does sound teaching produce? The answer unfolds in three movements:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Verses 1-10: Sound teaching produces transformed conduct in every social category.</hi> The chapter opens with a sharp pivot from the false teachers to Titus: "But as for you, teach what accords with sound doctrine" (2:1). What follows is specific behavioral instruction for every category of the community: older men are to be sober-minded, dignified, self-controlled, sound in faith, love, and steadfastness (2:2); older women are to be reverent, not slanderous, not enslaved to wine, but teachers of good who train the younger women (2:3-4); younger women are to love their husbands and children, be self-controlled, pure, industrious, kind, and submissive to their own husbands (2:4-5); younger men are to be self-controlled (2:6); Titus himself is to be a model of good works with teaching marked by integrity, dignity, and sound speech (2:7-8); slaves are to be submissive, well-pleasing, honest, and demonstrably faithful (2:9-10). Two purpose clauses frame the instructions: the younger women's conduct prevents the word of God from being reviled (2:5), and the slaves' conduct adorns the doctrine of God our Savior (2:10). The community's behavior is its primary testimony to the watching world.</p>
          <p>The dominant quality across every category is self-control (sophrosyne in its various forms), which appears in connection with older men, older women's teaching, younger women, younger men, and the theological statement. Self-control is the antidote to the Cretan cultural tendencies described by their own prophet (1:12) and the defining mark of the community formed by sound doctrine.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Verses 11-14: The theological ground — the grace of God has appeared and is training believers.</hi> The behavioral instructions are not arbitrary moral demands but the product of divine action. The grace of God has appeared (past event, first epiphany), bringing salvation for all people (2:11). This grace actively trains believers to renounce ungodliness and worldly desires and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age (2:12). Meanwhile, the community waits for the blessed hope — the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ (2:13, second epiphany). Christ gave himself for us with a twofold purpose: to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for himself a people for his own possession — the laos periousios of the Exodus covenant — who are zealous for good works (2:14). The theological statement transforms the behavioral instructions from human ethics into the fruit of grace, and it connects the community's identity to Israel's covenant identity as God's treasured people.</p>
          <p>The two epiphanies — the appearing of grace (past) and the appearing of glory (future) — create the temporal framework within which the community lives. The present age is not a waiting room but a training ground, where the grace that has already appeared continues to form the people who will greet the glory when it appears.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(3) Verse 15: Titus is charged to declare, exhort, and rebuke with full authority.</hi> The closing verse authorizes the delivery of everything the chapter contains. Titus is not offering suggestions; he speaks with the authority of the apostolic commission, which derives from God's own command (1:3). No one is to disregard him.</p>
          <p>The chapter's architecture reveals a careful logic: behavior flows from doctrine (2:1), doctrine is grounded in God's action in Christ (2:11-14), and God's action creates a people whose very identity is defined by zealous pursuit of good works (2:14). The false teachers of chapter 1 imposed external regulations while their minds and consciences remained defiled (1:15). The sound teaching of chapter 2 works from the inside out: grace trains, Christ redeems and purifies, and the result is a people who live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives — not because they have been commanded to by human authorities, but because the grace of the God who saves has appeared and is actively forming them into the people he purified for his own.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="Titus.3">
        <title>Titus 3 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before (Titus 2:11-15):</hi> The closing section of chapter 2 provided the theological engine for all the behavioral instructions that preceded it. The grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation for all people, and this grace actively trains believers to renounce ungodliness and worldly passions and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age (2:11-12). The community awaits the blessed hope — the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ, who gave himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for himself a people for his own possession who are zealous for good works (2:13-14). The chapter closed with the charge to Titus: "Declare these things; exhort and rebuke with all authority. Let no one disregard you" (2:15). The theological statement established that the behavioral transformation of the community is not a human achievement but the product of divine grace operating through Christ's redemptive self-giving. The community's identity as the laos periousios — the people of God's own possession, echoing the Exodus covenant — was defined not by external regulation but by zealous devotion to good works.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What follows (the letter's conclusion, 3:12-15):</hi> The letter closes with personal instructions about travel arrangements (Artemas or Tychicus will be sent to relieve Titus, who is to join Paul at Nicopolis), a charge to provision Zenas the lawyer and Apollos for their journey, and a final exhortation that "our people" must learn to devote themselves to good works for pressing needs, so that they may not be unfruitful. The final greeting extends grace to "all of you." The closing instructions are practical applications of the letter's central theme: good works are not abstract moral obligations but concrete acts of provision, hospitality, and community support.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">How chapter 3 connects both:</hi> Chapter 3 completes the letter's three-movement structure. Chapter 1 identified the crisis (false teachers who profess to know God but deny him by their works, rendering themselves unfit for any good work). Chapter 2 provided the positive content (sound doctrine producing transformed conduct in every social category) and the first theological grounding statement (2:11-14, the appearing of grace that trains believers). Chapter 3 now extends the behavioral instruction outward from the internal life of the community to its relationship with the surrounding civic order (3:1-2), provides a second theological grounding statement that parallels the first (3:3-7, the appearing of God's goodness and mercy that saves through the washing of regeneration), draws the practical conclusion that believers must devote themselves to good works (3:8), and closes the ring the letter opened by returning to the warning against the kind of controversies the false teachers promote (3:9-11). The chapter's architecture mirrors the whole letter in miniature: behavior (3:1-2) grounded in theology (3:3-7) producing good works (3:8) and requiring the rejection of what opposes them (3:9-11).</p>
          <p>The two theological statements — 2:11-14 and 3:3-7 — function as twin pillars supporting the same structure. Both describe a divine appearing or manifestation. Both describe salvation as God's initiative, not human achievement. Both connect salvation to transformed living. Both culminate in a people defined by good works. The repetition is not redundant but reinforcing: the letter says the same thing twice from different angles to ensure the message is unmistakable.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3.1-Titus.3.2">
          <title>Verses 1-2: Civic Conduct — The Community's Posture Toward the Outside World</title>
          <p>"Remind them to be submissive to rulers and authorities, to be obedient, to be ready for every good work, to speak evil of no one, to avoid quarreling, to be gentle, and to show perfect courtesy toward all people."</p>
          <p>(Hypomimneske autous archais exousiais hypotassesthai, peitharchein, pros pan ergon agathon hetoimous einai, medena blasphemein, amachous einai, epieikeis, pasan endeiknumenous praoteta pros pantas anthropous.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> Titus was charged to declare, exhort, and rebuke with all authority; no one is to disregard him (2:15). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> A reminder of the community's former condition — foolish, disobedient, enslaved to passions (3:3). <hi type="bold">How these verses connect the two:</hi> The charge to Titus at 2:15 authorized the delivery of instruction; 3:1-2 is the next installment of that instruction. The content shifts from the internal life of the community (how older men, younger women, slaves, etc. are to live) to the community's relationship with the civic order. And the connection to 3:3 is causal: the community is to treat the surrounding world with gentleness and courtesy because they themselves were once in the same condition as the outsiders they now encounter. The behavioral instruction of 3:1-2 will be grounded in the theological reminder of 3:3-7 just as the behavioral instruction of 2:2-10 was grounded in the theological statement of 2:11-14.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Remind them"</hi> (hypomimneske autous). The verb hypomniskein ("to remind, to bring to remembrance, to call to mind") is composed of hypo ("under") + mimneskein ("to remember, to call to mind"). The present imperative indicates ongoing action: Titus is to keep reminding them. The verb implies that what follows is not new instruction but something the community has already been taught and needs to hear again. In 2 Timothy 2:14, Paul uses the same verb: "Remind them (hypomnimneske) of these things." In 2 Peter 1:12: "I intend always to remind (hypomniskein) you of these qualities, though you know them." The need for reminder is not a sign of failure but a recognition that communities need repeated instruction, especially when cultural pressures push in the opposite direction. The pronoun autous ("them") refers to the community members Titus is responsible for — the believers in the Cretan towns.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To be submissive to rulers and authorities"</hi> (archais exousiais hypotassesthai). The verb hypotassesthai ("to submit, to be subject to, to place oneself under") is the same verb used in 2:5 for younger women toward their husbands and in 2:9 for slaves toward their masters. Its appearance here extends the submission language from the household sphere to the civic sphere. The objects of submission are archais kai exousiais — "rulers and authorities." The noun arche ("beginning, origin, ruler, magistrate, principality") in its political sense designates the ruling authority, the person or office that holds governing power. The noun exousia ("authority, power, right, jurisdiction") designates the sphere or mandate of that authority. Together, the two nouns comprehensively describe the governing structures of the Roman world — from local magistrates to provincial governors to imperial officials.</p>
          <p>The instruction to submit to governing authorities parallels Romans 13:1-7, where Paul writes: "Let every person be subject (hypotassestho) to the governing authorities (exousiais hyperechousais). For there is no authority (exousia) except from God, and those that exist have been instituted by God." The theological basis in Romans 13 is that God has established the governing structures. The instruction in Titus does not develop this theological basis but assumes it. For a community on Crete — a Roman province governed by a proconsul, with local magistrates in each town — submission to the political order is a practical instruction with concrete implications: paying taxes, obeying laws, honoring civic obligations, and not engaging in the kind of political agitation that would bring the community into conflict with Rome.</p>
          <p>The instruction also has an apologetic dimension, consistent with the letter's repeated concern that the community's conduct commend the gospel to outsiders. In 2:5, the younger women's conduct prevents "the word of God" from being reviled. In 2:8, Titus's sound speech ensures that "an opponent may be put to shame, having nothing evil to say about us." In 2:10, the slaves' conduct adorns "the doctrine of God our Savior." The community's civic submission serves the same purpose: a community that lives as a responsible participant in the civic order removes a potential accusation from opponents and demonstrates that the gospel produces good citizens, not seditious agitators.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To be obedient"</hi> (peitharchein). The verb peitharchein ("to obey, to comply, to submit to authority") is composed of peithesthai ("to obey, to be persuaded") + arche ("ruler, authority"). The word specifically describes obedience to those in authority — it is the natural companion to "submissive to rulers and authorities." In Acts 5:29, Peter and the apostles declare: "We must obey (peitharchein) God rather than men." In Acts 27:21, the same verb describes the sailors' obedience to Paul's counsel. The combination of hypotassesthai and peitharchein reinforces the instruction: the community is both to arrange itself under the civic order and to comply with its directives.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To be ready for every good work"</hi> (pros pan ergon agathon hetoimous einai). The adjective hetoimos ("ready, prepared, willing, at hand") describes a state of preparedness — the community is to be in a condition of readiness for good works, not waiting to be compelled but standing ready to serve. The phrase pan ergon agathon ("every good work") is the positive counterpart of the verdict on the false teachers in 1:16: they are pros pan ergon agathon adokimoi — "unfit for every good work." The verbal echo is precise and deliberate: where the false teachers are disqualified from every good work, the community is to be ready for every good work. The contrast defines the letter's vision of authentic versus counterfeit faith: authentic faith produces readiness for good works; counterfeit faith produces disqualification from them.</p>
          <p>The phrase "every good work" also anticipates 3:8 ("those who have believed in God may be careful to devote themselves to good works") and 3:14 ("our people must learn to devote themselves to good works"). The good-works theme runs through the entire letter as its central practical concern, appearing at 1:16 (false teachers unfit for good works), 2:7 (Titus as model of good works), 2:14 (Christ purified a people zealous for good works), 3:1 (ready for every good work), 3:8 (devote themselves to good works), and 3:14 (learn to devote themselves to good works). The repetition establishes good works as the letter's defining test of genuine faith.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To speak evil of no one"</hi> (medena blasphemein). The verb blasphemein ("to slander, to speak evil of, to revile, to defame, to blaspheme") here takes a human object: medena — "no one." The same verb appeared in 2:5, where the younger women's conduct prevents the word of God from being blasphemed. Here the community is forbidden from directing blasphemia toward any person. The prohibition is universal: not "speak evil of no fellow believer" but "speak evil of no one" — the scope extends to all people, including the pagan neighbors, the civic authorities, and even the opponents of the community. In a culture where the Cretans' own prophet described them as "always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons" (1:12), the temptation for the community to adopt a posture of verbal contempt toward their surrounding culture must have been strong. The instruction forbids it. The community that has been transformed by grace (2:11-14) must not speak with contempt about those who have not yet experienced it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To avoid quarreling"</hi> (amachous einai). The adjective amachos ("peaceable, not quarrelsome, not contentious") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) and mache ("fight, battle, conflict, quarrel"). The word describes a person who does not engage in fights, who avoids contention. In James 4:1: "What causes quarrels (machais) and what causes fights (polemous) among you?" The community is to be characterized by the absence of combativeness. This instruction connects to 3:9, where Titus will be told to "avoid foolish controversies, genealogies, dissensions, and quarrels about the law" — the false teachers' program generates precisely the kind of contentiousness that the community is to avoid.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To be gentle"</hi> (epieikeis). The adjective epieikes ("gentle, yielding, forbearing, reasonable, fair, equitable") describes a quality that goes beyond strict justice to show consideration and clemency. In Philippians 4:5: "Let your reasonableness (to epieikes) be known to everyone." In James 3:17: "the wisdom from above is first pure, then peaceable, gentle (epieikes), open to reason." In 1 Timothy 3:3, the overseer must be epieikes — "gentle." In 1 Peter 2:18, slaves are to be subject not only to good and gentle (epieikesin) masters but also to unjust ones. The quality describes a disposition that does not insist on its own rights to the last measure but yields, accommodates, and shows grace. It is the opposite of the quarrelsome, combative, self-assertive posture that characterizes the false teachers.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To show perfect courtesy toward all people"</hi> (pasan endeiknumenous praoteta pros pantas anthropous). The verb endeiknysthai ("to show, to demonstrate, to display, to prove") in the middle voice means "to display from oneself." The noun praotes ("gentleness, meekness, mildness, courtesy, humility") describes a controlled strength — not weakness but power under restraint. In Galatians 5:23, praotes appears as a fruit of the Spirit. In Galatians 6:1: "you who are spiritual should restore him in a spirit of gentleness (praotetos)." In 2 Timothy 2:25: "correcting his opponents with gentleness (praoteti)." In Matthew 5:5: "Blessed are the meek (praeis), for they shall inherit the earth" — citing Psalm 37:11 (LXX 36:11). In Numbers 12:3 (LXX), Moses is described as praos — "very meek, more than all people who were on the face of the earth." The praos person is not a pushover but a person whose strength is governed by consideration for others.</p>
          <p>The qualifier pasan ("all, complete, every kind of") intensifies: the gentleness is to be comprehensive, covering every situation. And the scope is defined by pros pantas anthropous — "toward all people." As with the prohibition against slander (medena blasphemein), the scope extends to all people without restriction. The community's gentleness is not reserved for fellow believers or for people who deserve it; it is extended to all people — the pagan neighbor, the difficult magistrate, the hostile opponent, the false teacher's follower. The universal scope matches the universal scope of God's saving grace in 2:11: "the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation for all people (pasin anthropois)." The community's courtesy toward all people mirrors God's grace toward all people.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3.3">
          <title>Verse 3: The Reminder — What We Once Were</title>
          <p>"For we ourselves were once foolish, disobedient, led astray, slaves to various passions and pleasures, passing our days in malice and envy, hated by others and hating one another."</p>
          <p>(Emen gar pote kai hemeis anoetoi, apeitheis, planomenoi, douleuontes epithymiais kai hedonais poikilais, en kakia kai phthono diagontes, stygeetoi, misountes allelous.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The community is to show perfect courtesy toward all people (3:2). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> The appearing of God's goodness and loving kindness that saved them (3:4-7). <hi type="bold">How this verse connects the two:</hi> The conjunction gar ("for") makes the connection explicit: the reason the community is to show gentleness and courtesy toward all people is that they themselves were once in the same condition as those people. The verse provides the motivational ground for the behavioral instructions of 3:1-2. And it simultaneously sets up the theological statement of 3:4-7 by establishing the "before" picture that makes the "after" picture intelligible. The structure is: behave this way toward outsiders (3:1-2) because you were once like them (3:3) until God saved you (3:4-7). The argument requires no change of subject — the same community whose civic conduct is being shaped in 3:1-2 is now reminded of the raw material from which that conduct has been formed.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For we ourselves were once"</hi> (emen gar pote kai hemeis). The imperfect verb emen ("we were") describes a past, sustained condition — not a single act but a way of life. The adverb pote ("once, formerly, at one time") specifies that this was the previous condition, now changed. The emphatic pronoun hemeis ("we ourselves") and the intensive particle kai ("also, even") include Paul in the description: this is not a distant "they" but an intimate "we." Paul is not lecturing the community about their past from a position of superiority; he places himself in the same former condition. The same self-inclusion appears in Ephesians 2:3: "Among whom we all (hemeis pantes) once lived in the passions of our flesh." The rhetorical effect is to eliminate any ground for condescension toward outsiders: "we ourselves" were exactly what they are now.</p>
          <p>The catalog that follows describes the former condition with seven terms:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Foolish</hi> (anoetoi). The adjective anoetos ("without understanding, senseless, foolish, unintelligent") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) + noein ("to perceive, to understand, to think"). The anoetos person is one whose nous (mind) is not functioning properly — the faculty of understanding is inactive or disabled. In Galatians 3:1, Paul addresses the Galatians: "O foolish (anoetoi) Galatians! Who has bewitched you?" In Luke 24:25, the risen Jesus says to the Emmaus disciples: "O foolish ones (anoetoi), and slow of heart to believe all that the prophets have spoken!" In Romans 1:21, those who did not honor God "became futile in their thinking, and their foolish (asynetos) hearts were darkened." The foolishness described is not a lack of intelligence but a failure of spiritual perception — the inability to see what is actually true about God and about one's own condition.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Disobedient</hi> (apeitheis). The adjective apeithes ("disobedient, unpersuadable, resistant to authority") appeared in 1:16 as part of the final verdict on the false teachers: "they are detestable, disobedient (apeitheis)." The same word now describes the community's former condition. The implication is uncomfortable but deliberate: what the false teachers currently are, the community once was. The difference between them is not that the community was inherently better but that God intervened (3:4-7). This connection reinforces the argument for gentleness toward outsiders: the community has no ground for contempt because they once shared the very disobedience they now see in others.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(3) Led astray</hi> (planomenoi). The present passive participle planomenoi (from planasthai, "to wander, to go astray, to be led astray, to be deceived") describes a condition of being misled — not merely choosing wrongly but being actively deceived, carried off course by error. In Hebrews 5:2, the high priest can deal gently with "the ignorant and wayward (planomenois)." In 1 Peter 2:25: "For you were straying (planomenoi) like sheep, but have now returned to the Shepherd." In Isaiah 53:6 (LXX): "All we like sheep have gone astray (eplanemethemen)." The passive voice is significant: those who wander are not simply choosing to wander; they are being led astray by forces, influences, and deceptions that operate upon them. The imagery echoes the shepherd-and-sheep language of the prophets, where Israel's wandering is attributed both to the people's own stubbornness and to the false shepherds who misled them (Ezekiel 34:1-6).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(4) Slaves to various passions and pleasures</hi> (douleuontes epithymiais kai hedonais poikilais). The present participle douleuontes (from douleuein, "to serve as a slave, to be enslaved, to be in bondage") describes active enslavement. The language echoes 2:3, where the older women must not be "enslaved (dedoulomenas) to much wine." In Romans 6:6, Paul writes: "our old self was crucified with him in order that the body of sin might be brought to nothing, so that we would no longer be enslaved (douleuein) to sin." In Romans 6:17-18: "you who were once slaves (douloi) of sin have become obedient from the heart to the standard of teaching." The slavery is to epithymiais kai hedonais poikilais — "various passions and pleasures." The noun epithymia ("desire, craving, longing, lust") is the same word used in 2:12 for the "worldly passions" (kosmikas epithymias) that grace trains believers to renounce. The noun hedone ("pleasure, enjoyment, delight") appears in James 4:1: "Is it not this, that your passions (hedonon) are at war within you?" In Luke 8:14, the seed among thorns is choked by "pleasures (hedonon) of life." The adjective poikilos ("various, diverse, manifold, of many kinds") indicates the diversity of the enslaving desires: not a single addiction but a comprehensive bondage to a shifting array of cravings. The picture is of a life governed not by any coherent purpose but by whatever desire happens to dominate at the moment.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(5) Passing our days in malice and envy</hi> (en kakia kai phthono diagontes). The present participle diagontes (from diagein, "to spend time, to pass one's life, to live") describes the ongoing manner of life. The same verb appeared in 1 Timothy 2:2: "that we may lead (diagomen) a peaceful and quiet life." There the manner of life is peaceful; here the manner is defined by two vices. The noun kakia ("malice, wickedness, evil, badness, depravity") describes an active disposition toward harm — not merely the absence of goodness but the presence of ill will. In Romans 1:29: "filled with all manner of unrighteousness, evil (kakia)..." In 1 Corinthians 5:8: "not with the old leaven, the leaven of malice (kakias) and evil." In 1 Peter 2:1: "Put away all malice (kakian)." The noun phthonos ("envy, jealousy, grudging ill-will") describes resentment at another's advantage. In Romans 1:29: "full of envy (phthonou)." In Galatians 5:21: "envy (phthonoi)" appears among the works of the flesh. In Philippians 1:15, some preach Christ "from envy (phthonon)." The pairing of malice and envy describes a social posture: people who wish harm on others and resent others' good fortune. This is the opposite of the epieikeia ("gentleness") and praotes ("courtesy") that 3:1-2 prescribed.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(6) Hated by others</hi> (stygetoi). The adjective stygetos ("hateful, detestable, odious") appears only here in the New Testament. It is related to the verb stygein ("to hate, to abhor, to detest"). The adjective is passive in force: they were hated, found detestable by those around them. The word echoes bdelyktos ("detestable") from 1:16, where the false teachers are described as detestable. The condition of being hated is the natural consequence of the preceding vices: a person governed by malice and envy naturally provokes hostility in return. The hateful person becomes the hated person. This is the social fruit of the life described in the preceding terms: foolishness, disobedience, enslavement to passions, and malice inevitably produce relationships marked by mutual hostility.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(7) Hating one another</hi> (misountes allelous). The present participle misountes (from misein, "to hate, to detest, to regard with hostility") describes active hatred directed toward one another (allelous, the reciprocal pronoun). The final item in the catalog completes the picture: not only were they hated by those around them, but they hated each other. The reciprocal pronoun indicates mutual, multidirectional hostility — a community of people who despised and were despised, who attacked and were attacked. In 1 John 2:9-11: "Whoever says he is in the light and hates his brother is still in the darkness." In 1 John 3:15: "Everyone who hates his brother is a murderer." The mutual hatred described here is the complete antithesis of the community life Paul has been prescribing throughout the letter — the life of mutual instruction (2:3-4), service (2:9-10), and gentleness (3:2).</p>
          <p>The overall effect of the catalog is to establish that the community's former condition was identical to the condition of the surrounding world they now live among. Every quality that might tempt the community to look down on outsiders was once their own defining characteristic. The "we once were" eliminates any basis for spiritual superiority and grounds the instruction to show gentleness toward all people (3:2) in shared experience: you must be gentle toward them because you were them.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3.4-Titus.3.7">
          <title>Verses 4-7: The Second Theological Statement — Salvation by Mercy Through the Washing of Regeneration</title>
          <p>"But when the goodness and loving kindness of God our Savior appeared, he saved us, not because of works done by us in righteousness, but according to his own mercy, by the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Holy Spirit, whom he poured out on us richly through Jesus Christ our Savior, so that being justified by his grace we might become heirs according to the hope of eternal life."</p>
          <p>(Hote de he chrestotes kai he philanthropia epephane tou soteros hemon theou, ouk ex ergon ton en dikaiosyne ha epoiesamen hemeis alla kata to autou eleos esosen hemas dia loutrou palingenesias kai anakainoseos pneumatos hagiou, hou execheen eph' hemas plousios dia Iesou Christou tou soteros hemon, hina dikaiothentes te ekeinou chariti kleronomon genethomen kat' elpida zoes aioniou.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The community's former condition — foolish, disobedient, enslaved (3:3). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> The "faithful saying" and the conclusion that believers must devote themselves to good works (3:8). <hi type="bold">How this section connects the two:</hi> The adversative de ("but") in verse 4 creates the pivot from the "before" picture (3:3) to the divine intervention that changed everything. The structure is identical to Ephesians 2:1-5, where Paul describes the former condition of being "dead in trespasses and sins" (2:1-3) and then pivots: "But God, being rich in mercy... made us alive together with Christ" (2:4-5). The same "once you were X, but God did Y" pattern operates here. The theological statement explains how the community moved from the condition of 3:3 to the life prescribed in 3:1-2, and it grounds the conclusion of 3:8: because God saved them by his mercy, those who have believed must devote themselves to good works. Salvation by mercy is the source, not the substitute, for good works.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 4: The Appearing of God's Goodness and Loving Kindness</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But when the goodness and loving kindness of God our Savior appeared"</hi> (hote de he chrestotes kai he philanthropia epephane tou soteros hemon theou).</p>
          <p>The conjunction hote ("when") marks a temporal point: the intervention occurred at a specific moment. The adversative de ("but") creates the contrast with 3:3: we were once X, but when Y happened, everything changed.</p>
          <p>Two divine attributes are named as the subjects of the appearing:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Goodness</hi> (chrestotes). The noun chrestotes ("goodness, kindness, generosity, uprightness") describes a disposition of active benevolence. In Romans 2:4: "Do you presume on the riches of his kindness (chrestotetoes) and forbearance and patience, not knowing that God's kindness (chreston) is meant to lead you to repentance?" In Romans 11:22: "Note then the kindness (chrestoteta) and the severity of God: severity toward those who have fallen, but God's kindness (chrestoteta) to you, provided you continue in his kindness (chrestoteti)." In Ephesians 2:7: "so that in the coming ages he might show the immeasurable riches of his grace in kindness (chrestoteti) toward us in Christ Jesus." In Galatians 5:22, chrestotes appears as a fruit of the Spirit. The word describes God's disposition as fundamentally generous, actively beneficent — a God whose nature inclines toward doing good to those who do not deserve it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Loving kindness</hi> (philanthropia). The noun philanthropia ("love of humanity, love for people, benevolence, kindness") is composed of philos ("loving, fond of") + anthropos ("human being, person"). The word appears only here and in Acts 28:2 in the New Testament, where the natives of Malta showed "unusual kindness (philanthropian)" to the shipwrecked travelers. In Greek political and philosophical usage, philanthropia was a cardinal virtue of kings and benefactors — the generous disposition of a ruler toward his subjects. Josephus uses the word frequently to describe acts of royal clemency and generosity (Antiquities 6.348; 12.22). In the LXX, the word appears in the Epistle of Aristeas (208, 265, 290) to describe the ideal disposition of a king. By applying philanthropia to God, Paul attributes to God the highest quality that the Greco-Roman world expected of its best rulers: a genuine, generous love for the people under his care. The word would resonate powerfully in a Roman province where imperial propaganda claimed the emperor's philanthropia as justification for his rule. Paul declares that the true philanthropos — the true lover of humanity — is not Caesar but God.</p>
          <p>The verb epephane ("appeared, was made manifest, became visible") is the same verb used in 2:11 for the appearing of grace: "For the grace of God has appeared (epephane), bringing salvation for all people." The parallel is exact and deliberate. In 2:11, what appeared was he charis tou theou — "the grace of God." Here in 3:4, what appeared was he chrestotes kai he philanthropia tou soteros hemon theou — "the goodness and loving kindness of God our Savior." The two statements describe the same event from different angles. Grace appeared (2:11). Goodness and loving kindness appeared (3:4). The appearing is singular: both describe the same divine intervention in history — the coming of Christ and the gospel's proclamation. But the vocabulary differs to illuminate different facets of that intervention. Grace emphasizes the unmerited character of God's favor. Goodness emphasizes the beneficent character of God's disposition. Loving kindness emphasizes God's personal affection for humanity.</p>
          <p>The title tou soteros hemon theou ("of God our Savior") has run through the letter as a signature designation. It appeared in 1:3 in the greeting, in 2:10 in the context of slaves adorning "the doctrine of God our Savior," and now here in 3:4. As noted throughout the analysis, the LXX uses soter ("savior") for God specifically in connection with his demonstrated acts of deliverance. Isaiah 45:21 (LXX): "Who declared this from the beginning?... Was it not I, the LORD? And there is no other god besides me, a righteous God and a Savior (soter); there is none besides me." The title soter applied to God anchors the saving action about to be described in the history of Israel's God, who has always been the one who saves.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 5a: Not by Our Works, but by His Mercy</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"He saved us, not because of works done by us in righteousness, but according to his own mercy"</hi> (ouk ex ergon ton en dikaiosyne ha epoiesamen hemeis alla kata to autou eleos esosen hemas).</p>
          <p>The verb esosen ("he saved") is the aorist of sozein ("to save, to deliver, to rescue, to preserve"). The aorist describes a completed act: God saved us. The pronoun hemas ("us") includes Paul, Titus, and the community — the same "we" who were described in verse 3 as foolish, disobedient, and enslaved. The subject of the saving act is God our Savior from verse 4. The structure is stark: God saved us. The initiative belongs entirely to God; the community contributes nothing to the saving act.</p>
          <p>The basis of salvation is specified by negation and then by affirmation:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Negation:</hi> ouk ex ergon ton en dikaiosyne ha epoiesamen hemeis — "not from works in righteousness that we did." The preposition ek ("from, out of, on the basis of") with the genitive ergon ("works") indicates the basis or ground of salvation: it does not arise from works. The phrase ton en dikaiosyne ("those in/of righteousness") further qualifies: these are works performed in the sphere of righteousness, works aimed at achieving a righteous standing. The relative clause ha epoiesamen hemeis ("which we ourselves did") makes the exclusion comprehensive and emphatic: the pronoun hemeis ("we ourselves") at the end of the clause is emphatic — whatever works we ourselves performed, they are not the basis of salvation.</p>
          <p>The parallel with Ephesians 2:8-9 is close: "For by grace you have been saved through faith. And this is not your own doing; it is the gift of God, not a result of works (ouk ex ergon), so that no one may boast." In Romans 3:20: "For by works of the law (ex ergon nomou) no human being will be justified in his sight." In Romans 3:28: "For we hold that one is justified by faith apart from works of the law (choris ergon nomou)." In 2 Timothy 1:9: God "saved us and called us to a holy calling, not because of our works (ou kata ta erga hemon) but because of his own purpose and grace, which he gave us in Christ Jesus before the ages began." The consistency across the Pauline corpus is clear: the saving act is not grounded in human performance. This statement does not devalue good works — the entire letter is dedicated to producing them — but it locates them properly: good works are the fruit of salvation, not the root of it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Affirmation:</hi> alla kata to autou eleos — "but according to his own mercy." The adversative alla ("but, rather, on the contrary") creates a sharp contrast between what salvation is not based on (our works) and what it is based on (his mercy). The noun eleos ("mercy, compassion, pity, steadfast love") in the LXX translates the Hebrew hesed ("covenant loyalty, steadfast love, mercy, loving-kindness") and rachamim ("compassion, tender mercy"). The word carries the full weight of Israel's covenant vocabulary for God's faithful, compassionate disposition toward his people. Exodus 34:6 (LXX): "The LORD, the LORD, a God merciful (oiktirmon) and gracious, slow to anger, and abounding in steadfast love (polyeleemon) and faithfulness." Psalm 103:8 (LXX 102:8): "The LORD is merciful (oiktirmon) and gracious, slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love (polyeleeos)." Psalm 51:1 (LXX 50:3): "Have mercy (eleeson) on me, O God, according to your steadfast love (to mega eleos sou); according to your abundant mercy (ton oiktirmon sou) blot out my transgressions." The eleos of God is not a spontaneous, undirected emotion but a covenant attribute — the reliable, persistent compassion that God exercises toward his people because of who he is, not because of what they have done.</p>
          <p>The reflexive possessive autou ("his own") emphasizes the source: the mercy originates entirely within God. It is not elicited by human merit or provoked by human need; it flows from God's own character. The phrase kata to autou eleos ("according to his own mercy") identifies mercy as the operative principle of salvation: God saved according to the standard of his own mercy, not according to the standard of human performance.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 5b: The Washing of Regeneration and Renewal of the Holy Spirit</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"By the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Holy Spirit"</hi> (dia loutrou palingenesias kai anakainoseos pneumatos hagiou).</p>
          <p>The preposition dia ("through, by means of") with the genitive indicates the instrument or means of salvation. God saved us through two coordinate operations:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) The washing of regeneration</hi> (loutrou palingenesias). The noun loutron ("bath, washing, the act of washing") appears only here and in Ephesians 5:26 in the New Testament: "that he might sanctify her, having cleansed her by the washing (loutro) of water with the word." In the LXX, the noun and its verbal cognate louein ("to wash, to bathe") are used for ritual washings that effect purification. Leviticus 15:13: "he shall wash (lousetai) his body in running water and be clean." Numbers 19:7: "the priest shall wash (lousetai) his clothes and bathe (lousetai) his body in water." Song of Songs 4:2 (LXX): "Your teeth are like a flock of shorn ewes that have come up from the washing (loutrou)." Ezekiel 16:4: "On the day you were born... you were not washed (elousthes) with water to cleanse you." The noun loutron describes a thorough washing — not a sprinkling but a bath, a complete cleansing.</p>
          <p>The genitive palingenesias ("of regeneration, of rebirth, of new creation") specifies the character of this washing. The noun palingenesia ("regeneration, new birth, renewal, re-creation") is composed of palin ("again") + genesis ("birth, origin, coming into being"). The word appears only here and in Matthew 19:28 in the New Testament, where Jesus speaks of "the regeneration (palingenesia), when the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne." In Matthew's usage, palingenesia describes the cosmic renewal — the restoration of all things at the end of the age. In Stoic philosophy, palingenesia described the cyclical regeneration of the cosmos. But in the present context, the word describes an individual or communal regeneration: the washing effects a new birth, a new beginning, a re-creation of the person.</p>
          <p>The concept has deep roots in Israel's prophetic tradition. Ezekiel 36:25-27 provides the closest Old Testament parallel: "I will sprinkle clean water on you, and you shall be clean from all your uncleannesses, and from all your idols I will cleanse you. And I will give you a new heart, and a new spirit I will put within you. And I will remove the heart of stone from your flesh and give you a heart of flesh. And I will put my Spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes and be careful to obey my rules." The Ezekiel passage combines precisely the elements present in Paul's statement: (a) cleansing with water, (b) internal renewal/new creation, and (c) the gift of God's Spirit. A first-century hearer steeped in the prophetic tradition would recognize Paul's language as the fulfillment of Ezekiel's promise: the washing of regeneration is the clean water that God promised to sprinkle on his people, and the renewal of the Holy Spirit is the new spirit that God promised to put within them.</p>
          <p>Isaiah 44:3 (LXX) reinforces the connection: "For I will pour water on the thirsty land, and streams on the dry ground; I will pour out my Spirit upon your offspring, and my blessing on your descendants." The pouring of water and the pouring of the Spirit are parallel images for the same divine act of renewal. Jeremiah 31:31-34 (LXX 38:31-34) adds the covenant dimension: "I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel... I will put my law within them, and I will write it on their hearts." The washing of regeneration in Titus 3:5 is the concrete realization of what the prophets described: the new covenant act by which God cleanses and remakes his people from within.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Renewal of the Holy Spirit</hi> (anakainoseos pneumatos hagiou). The noun anakainosis ("renewal, renovation, making new") appears only here and in Romans 12:2 in the New Testament: "be transformed by the renewal (anakainosei) of your mind (tou noos)." The related verb anakainoun ("to renew, to make new") appears in 2 Corinthians 4:16: "our inner self is being renewed (anakainoutai) day by day," and in Colossians 3:10: "the new self, which is being renewed (anakainoumenon) in knowledge after the image of its creator." The noun describes the act of making something new — not repairing the old but producing a genuinely renewed reality. The connection to Romans 12:2 is particularly significant: in Romans, the renewal of the mind (nous) is the means by which believers are transformed and able to discern God's will. In Titus 3:3, the community's former condition included foolishness (anoetoi) — a failure of the nous. The renewal effected by the Spirit restores the very faculty that was formerly disabled.</p>
          <p>The genitive pneumatos hagiou ("of the Holy Spirit") can be read as either subjective (the renewal that the Holy Spirit performs) or appositional (the renewal that consists in receiving the Holy Spirit). Both readings are grammatically possible, and both may be in view. The Spirit is both the agent of renewal and the content of the renewal: God renews by giving his Spirit, and the Spirit's presence is itself the renewal. This dual function echoes the Ezekiel 36:26-27 promise: God puts a new spirit within them (the renewal) and puts his Spirit within them (the agent who effects and sustains the renewal).</p>
          <p>The grammatical relationship between loutrou palingenesias and anakainoseos pneumatos hagiou is debated: do they describe two distinct operations or two aspects of a single operation? The single preposition dia ("through") governing both genitives, and the single article-less construction, suggest a hendiadys — two expressions describing a single complex act: the washing-that-regenerates-and-the-renewal-that-the-Spirit-performs are one divine operation, not two sequential stages. God saves through a single act that cleanses, regenerates, and renews by the Spirit.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 6: The Outpouring of the Spirit Through Christ</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Whom he poured out on us richly through Jesus Christ our Savior"</hi> (hou execheen eph' hemas plousios dia Iesou Christou tou soteros hemon).</p>
          <p>The relative pronoun hou ("whom") refers back to the Holy Spirit (pneumatos hagiou). The verb ekchein ("to pour out, to shed, to spill") in the aorist (execheen) describes a completed act of pouring. The imagery is liquid and abundant — not a measured dispensation but a flooding outpouring. The language is drawn directly from the prophetic tradition of Spirit-pouring. Joel 2:28-29 (LXX 3:1-2): "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out (ekcheo) my Spirit on all flesh." This is the text Peter quoted at Pentecost in Acts 2:17: "And in the last days it shall be, God declares, that I will pour out (ekcheo) my Spirit on all flesh." In Acts 2:33, Peter declares that Jesus, "having received from the Father the promise of the Holy Spirit, has poured out (execheen) this that you yourselves are seeing and hearing." In Acts 10:45, when the Spirit fell on the Gentiles: "the gift of the Holy Spirit was poured out (ekkechytai) even on the Gentiles."</p>
          <p>The verb execheen in Titus 3:6 is identical in form to the verb used in Acts 2:33 — both are aorist active third person singular of ekchein. The linguistic connection to Pentecost and its prophetic background in Joel is unmistakable. Paul is describing the community's experience as the fulfillment of the same prophetic promise that Peter announced on the day of Pentecost: God has poured out his Spirit, as Joel prophesied.</p>
          <p>The adverb plousios ("richly, abundantly, generously") qualifies the outpouring. In Colossians 3:16: "Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly (plousios)." In 2 Peter 1:11: "there will be richly (plousios) provided for you an entrance into the eternal kingdom." The outpouring is not stingy or measured but abundant and generous — consistent with the philanthropia ("loving kindness") attributed to God in verse 4. A God characterized by philanthropia pours out his Spirit plousios.</p>
          <p>The preposition dia ("through") with the genitive Iesou Christou ("Jesus Christ") identifies Christ as the agent or mediator through whom the Spirit is poured out. The title tou soteros hemon ("our Savior") now appears applied to Christ, completing the letter's characteristic alternation. In 3:4, "God our Savior" was the source of salvation. Here in 3:6, "Jesus Christ our Savior" is the one through whom the Spirit is poured out. The alternation follows the same pattern as 1:3-4 (God our Savior / Christ Jesus our Savior), 2:10/2:13 (God our Savior / our great God and Savior Jesus Christ). The saving work belongs to God and is executed through Christ. The two are partners in the single work of salvation, sharing the same title because they share the same purpose.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 7: Justified by Grace, Heirs of Eternal Life</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So that being justified by his grace we might become heirs according to the hope of eternal life"</hi> (hina dikaiothentes te ekeinou chariti kleronomon genethomen kat' elpida zoes aioniou).</p>
          <p>The conjunction hina ("so that, in order that") introduces the purpose of the entire sequence: God saved us through the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Spirit in order that we might be justified and become heirs. The purpose clause looks forward, not backward: the saving act aims at a future outcome.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Being justified by his grace"</hi> (dikaiothentes te ekeinou chariti). The aorist passive participle dikaiothentes (from dikaioun, "to justify, to declare righteous, to vindicate, to acquit") describes an act of being declared righteous. The passive voice indicates that the justification is done to the believer, not by the believer: God is the one who justifies. In Romans 3:24: "being justified (dikaioumenoi) freely by his grace (te autou chariti) through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus." In Romans 5:1: "Therefore, since we have been justified (dikaiothentes) by faith, we have peace with God." In Romans 8:30: "those whom he called he also justified (edikaiosen)." In Galatians 2:16: "a person is not justified (dikaioutai) by works of the law but through faith in Jesus Christ." The language of justification in Paul consistently describes God's act of declaring the believer righteous on the basis of grace through faith, not on the basis of works.</p>
          <p>The instrumental dative te ekeinou chariti ("by his grace, by the grace of that one") specifies the means: justification is effected by grace. The demonstrative pronoun ekeinou ("of that one, his") points back to God our Savior (3:4) or Christ our Savior (3:6) — the antecedent is ambiguous and may be deliberately so, since the entire passage has presented God and Christ as co-actors in the single work of salvation. The noun charis ("grace, favor, gift") returns from 2:11, where "the grace of God has appeared." The two theological statements are now linked by the same vocabulary: grace appeared (2:11); we are justified by grace (3:7). The appearing of grace and the experience of justification are connected as cause and effect.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"We might become heirs"</hi> (kleronomoi genethomen). The noun kleronomos ("heir, one who inherits, one who receives an allotted portion") in the LXX and in Pauline usage carries profound covenantal weight. The kleronomos is the person who receives the kleronomia — the inheritance, the allotted portion, the promised possession. In the LXX, the inheritance language is tied to the land promise: God promised Abraham and his descendants an inheritance (kleronomia) — the land of Canaan (Genesis 15:7; Deuteronomy 1:8; 4:1). In Paul, the inheritance concept is expanded beyond the land to encompass the fullness of God's promise. Romans 4:13: "For the promise to Abraham and his offspring that he would be heir (kleronomos) of the world did not come through the law but through the righteousness of faith." Romans 8:17: "and if children, then heirs (kleronomoi) — heirs of God and fellow heirs (synkleronomoi) with Christ, provided we suffer with him in order that we may also be glorified with him." Galatians 3:29: "And if you are Christ's, then you are Abraham's offspring, heirs (kleronomoi) according to promise." Galatians 4:7: "So you are no longer a slave, but a son, and if a son, then an heir (kleronomos) through God."</p>
          <p>The verb genethomen (aorist middle subjunctive of ginesthai, "to become") indicates a change of status: from what they once were (3:3) to what they now are — heirs. The people who were foolish, disobedient, enslaved, malicious, hated, and hateful have become heirs of God's promise. The transformation is not gradual self-improvement but a divine act of re-creation that changes the person's fundamental identity and standing.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"According to the hope of eternal life"</hi> (kat' elpida zoes aioniou). The phrase returns to the letter's opening: in 1:2, Paul's apostleship was grounded ep' elpidi zoes aioniou — "on the hope of eternal life." The same phrase closes the theological ring: the hope that grounded Paul's mission in the greeting now defines the inheritance that the justified heirs await. The preposition kata ("according to, in correspondence with") indicates that the inheritance corresponds to the hope: what has been hoped for is what will be inherited. The noun elpis ("hope") is not a vague wish but a confident expectation grounded in the promise of the God who cannot lie (1:2). The phrase zoes aioniou ("of eternal life, of the life of the age to come") identifies the content of the inheritance: life that belongs to the coming age, life beyond death and corruption, the life that Daniel 12:2 promised and that Jesus spoke of as the goal of faithful living (Mark 10:30; John 3:16; 5:24; 17:3).</p>
          <p>The theological statement of 3:4-7 is now complete, and its structure can be mapped:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item><hi type="bold">The divine initiative:</hi> God's goodness and loving kindness appeared (3:4)</item>
            <item><hi type="bold">The exclusion of human merit:</hi> not by works of righteousness that we did (3:5a)</item>
            <item><hi type="bold">The operative principle:</hi> according to his own mercy (3:5a)</item>
            <item><hi type="bold">The means:</hi> through the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Holy Spirit (3:5b)</item>
            <item><hi type="bold">The mediator:</hi> poured out richly through Jesus Christ our Savior (3:6)</item>
            <item><hi type="bold">The purpose:</hi> that, justified by his grace, we might become heirs according to the hope of eternal life (3:7)</item>
          </list>
          <p>The correspondence between this statement and the one in 2:11-14 is systematic:</p>
          <table>
            <row>
              <cell>2:11-14</cell>
              <cell>3:4-7</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The grace of God appeared (2:11)</cell>
              <cell>The goodness and loving kindness of God our Savior appeared (3:4)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Bringing salvation for all people (2:11)</cell>
              <cell>He saved us (3:5)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Training us to renounce ungodliness (2:12)</cell>
              <cell>Not by works of righteousness (3:5)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>To live self-controlled, upright, godly lives (2:12)</cell>
              <cell>According to his own mercy (3:5)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Waiting for the blessed hope (2:13)</cell>
              <cell>Heirs according to the hope of eternal life (3:7)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Christ gave himself to redeem and purify (2:14)</cell>
              <cell>Through the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Spirit (3:5)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>A people zealous for good works (2:14)</cell>
              <cell>Justified by his grace (3:7)</cell>
            </row>
          </table>
          <p>The two statements are complementary: 2:11-14 emphasizes the training and transformative function of grace, culminating in a people zealous for good works. 3:4-7 emphasizes the mercy and regenerative function of grace, culminating in heirs of eternal life. Together they describe the complete work of God in salvation: God's grace trains for transformed living, and God's mercy regenerates for eternal inheritance. Neither statement is complete without the other.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3.8">
          <title>Verse 8: The Faithful Saying — Believers Must Devote Themselves to Good Works</title>
          <p>"The saying is trustworthy, and I want you to insist on these things, so that those who have believed in God may be careful to devote themselves to good works. These things are excellent and profitable for people."</p>
          <p>(Pistos ho logos, kai peri touton boulomai se diabebaiousthai, hina phrontizousin kalon ergon proistasthai hoi pepisteukotes theo. Tauta estin kala kai ophelima tois anthropois.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The theological statement on salvation by mercy through regeneration and the Spirit (3:4-7). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> The warning against foolish controversies and quarrels about the law (3:9). <hi type="bold">How this verse connects the two:</hi> Verse 8 draws the practical conclusion from the theological statement: because God has saved by mercy and made believers heirs of eternal life, those who have believed must devote themselves to good works. The verse then transitions to 3:9 by implying the contrast: good works are "excellent and profitable," while the controversies of the false teachers (3:9) are "unprofitable and worthless." The verse is the hinge between the theology (3:4-7) and the practical warning (3:9-11).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The saying is trustworthy"</hi> (pistos ho logos). The phrase pistos ho logos ("faithful is the saying, trustworthy is the word") is a formula that appears five times in the Pastoral Epistles (1 Timothy 1:15; 3:1; 4:9; 2 Timothy 2:11; Titus 3:8). Each occurrence marks a statement of particular importance and reliability. The adjective pistos ("faithful, trustworthy, reliable") declares the statement worthy of confidence. The question of which "saying" is being endorsed — whether it refers to what precedes (3:4-7) or what follows (the exhortation about good works) — is best resolved by recognizing that the formula validates the entire theological-practical complex: the saving work of God (3:4-7) and its necessary consequence in good works (3:8b) form a single trustworthy teaching.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And I want you to insist on these things"</hi> (kai peri touton boulomai se diabebaiousthai). The verb boulesthai ("to will, to wish, to desire, to intend") expresses Paul's deliberate intention. The verb diabebaiousthai ("to insist, to affirm strongly, to speak confidently, to assert emphatically") appears only here and in 1 Timothy 1:7 in the New Testament. In 1 Timothy 1:7, those who want to be teachers of the law "do not understand (nooutes) either what they are saying or the things about which they make confident assertions (diabebaiountai)." The false teachers make confident assertions about the wrong things; Titus is to make confident assertions about the right things — the theological truths of 3:4-7 and their practical consequences. The phrase peri touton ("concerning these things") refers to the theological statement just concluded. Titus is not merely to mention these truths in passing but to insist on them, to affirm them with force and confidence.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"So that those who have believed in God may be careful to devote themselves to good works"</hi> (hina phrontizousin kalon ergon proistasthai hoi pepisteukotes theo). The hina clause states the purpose of Titus's insistent teaching. The subject is hoi pepisteukotes theo — "those who have believed in God." The perfect tense of the participle (pepisteukotes, from pisteuein, "to believe, to trust, to have faith") describes persons who have come to faith and remain in that state of faith — they are believers, established in their trust in God. The fact that Paul specifies "believed in God" (theo) rather than "believed in Christ" is notable in a letter that has carefully alternated between God and Christ as the agents of salvation. The believing is directed toward God because the theological statement of 3:4-7 began with God our Savior (3:4) and traced the entire saving work to God's initiative.</p>
          <p>The verb phrontizein ("to be careful, to give thought to, to take care, to be intent on") appears only here in the New Testament. It describes deliberate, purposeful attention — not casual or accidental engagement but careful, intentional focus. What the believers are to be careful about is kalon ergon proistasthai — "to devote themselves to good works." The verb proistasthai ("to manage, to devote oneself to, to maintain, to practice, to be occupied with, to engage in") in the middle voice describes active engagement. In 1 Thessalonians 5:12, leaders are described as those who proistamenous — "those who lead/manage." In Romans 12:8, the one who leads is to do so with zeal. In 1 Timothy 3:4-5, the elder must proistamenon — "manage" his household well. The verb carries the sense of giving active, sustained attention to something, overseeing it, being responsible for it. The believers are to treat good works as a domain requiring their active management and sustained investment.</p>
          <p>The phrase kalon ergon ("good works") is the climax of the letter's central theme. The adjective kalos ("beautiful, noble, fine, good, excellent") describes works that are not merely morally correct but visibly admirable. The phrase has appeared at every critical juncture of the letter: 1:16 (false teachers unfit for every good work), 2:7 (Titus as model of good works), 2:14 (Christ purified a people zealous for good works), 3:1 (ready for every good work), and now 3:8 (believers devoting themselves to good works). The recurrence is a drumbeat: the letter insists, again and again, that genuine faith produces visible, admirable, beneficial action. The theological statement of 3:4-7 — salvation by mercy, not by works — does not diminish this insistence but grounds it: precisely because salvation is by mercy, those who have been saved must devote themselves to good works. The works are not the means of earning salvation but the evidence of having received it and the proper response to the mercy that granted it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"These things are excellent and profitable for people"</hi> (tauta estin kala kai ophelima tois anthropois). The demonstrative tauta ("these things") refers to the good works just mentioned, or more broadly to the entire theological-practical teaching. The adjective kalos ("good, fine, excellent, beautiful") evaluates the teaching as excellent. The adjective ophelimos ("profitable, useful, beneficial, advantageous") appears only here, in 1 Timothy 4:8, and in 2 Timothy 3:16 in the New Testament. In 1 Timothy 4:8: "godliness is profitable (ophelimos) in every way, as it holds promise for the present life and also for the life to come." In 2 Timothy 3:16: "All Scripture is... profitable (ophelimos) for teaching." The teaching about good works grounded in God's mercy is declared both excellent in itself and beneficial for people. The dative tois anthropois ("for people") may refer to the believers who practice them, or to the broader human community that benefits from them, or to both.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3.9">
          <title>Verse 9: The Warning Against Foolish Controversies</title>
          <p>"But avoid foolish controversies, genealogies, dissensions, and quarrels about the law, for they are unprofitable and worthless."</p>
          <p>(Moras de zeteseis kai genealogias kai ereis kai machas nomikas periistaso; eisin gar anopheleis kai mataioi.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> Good works are excellent and profitable for people (3:8). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> The instruction about the divisive person — warn twice, then reject (3:10-11). <hi type="bold">How this verse connects the two:</hi> The contrast between 3:8 and 3:9 is pointed and deliberate. Good works are kala kai ophelima — "excellent and profitable." The controversies are anopheleis kai mataioi — "unprofitable and worthless." The pairing of positive and negative evaluations creates a choice: Titus (and the community) must pursue what is profitable and avoid what is unprofitable. The verse also circles back to the letter's opening crisis: the false teachers of 1:10-16, who promoted Jewish myths and human commands, are the source of the controversies, genealogies, and quarrels about the law described here. The ring is closing.</p>
          <p>The adversative de ("but") creates the contrast with the preceding instruction about good works. The verb periistasthai ("to avoid, to shun, to turn away from, to stand aside from") in the middle imperative means "to place oneself at a distance from, to go around." In 2 Timothy 2:16, Paul uses the same verb: "But avoid (periistaso) irreverent babble." The instruction is to maintain distance: do not engage, do not participate, do not be drawn into these disputes.</p>
          <p>Four items are to be avoided:</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Foolish controversies</hi> (moras zeteseis). The adjective moros ("foolish, stupid, dull") describes what lacks understanding or sense. In 2 Timothy 2:23: "Have nothing to do with foolish (moras), ignorant controversies; you know that they breed quarrels." The noun zetesis ("controversy, debate, question, inquiry, investigation") describes the kind of dispute that consumes energy without producing resolution. The combination describes debates that are not merely unresolved but fundamentally lacking in intellectual substance.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Genealogies</hi> (genealogias). The noun genealogia ("genealogy, tracing of descent, record of ancestry") appeared in 1 Timothy 1:4, where the false teachers devoted themselves to "myths and endless genealogies (genealogiais aperantois), which promote speculations rather than the stewardship from God that is by faith." The genealogies in view are most likely the elaborate ancestral speculations found in Second Temple Jewish literature — the expansion and embellishment of biblical genealogies with invented details about patriarchs, angels, and cosmic events. Works like the Book of Jubilees and portions of 1 Enoch contain extensive genealogical elaborations that served as frameworks for speculative theology. These genealogies distract from the gospel's core message and generate the kind of factional disputes that divide communities.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(3) Dissensions</hi> (ereis). The noun eris ("strife, discord, contention, rivalry") describes interpersonal conflict driven by competitive or adversarial motives. In Romans 1:29: "full of envy, murder, strife (eridos)." In 1 Corinthians 1:11: "there is quarreling (erides) among you." In 1 Corinthians 3:3: "there is jealousy and strife (eris) among you." In Galatians 5:20, eris appears among the works of the flesh. The dissensions are the social fruit of the foolish controversies: when people argue about matters of no substance, personal rivalries and factional loyalties emerge.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(4) Quarrels about the law</hi> (machas nomikas). The noun mache ("fight, battle, quarrel, conflict") is the noun from which the adjective amachos ("peaceable," 3:2) was derived. The adjective nomikos ("pertaining to the law, legal, about the law") specifies the subject matter: these are quarrels about the nomos — the Torah, the law of Moses. The phrase identifies the specific content of the controversies with precision: the false teachers who are "especially those of the circumcision party" (1:10) are generating disputes about the application, interpretation, and requirements of the Mosaic law. These are not debates about whether the law is good (Paul elsewhere affirms that it is, Romans 7:12) but factional quarrels about its binding authority on Gentile believers, its dietary and purity requirements, and its role in the life of the community. Paul's verdict on these quarrels is unequivocal.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For they are unprofitable and worthless"</hi> (eisin gar anopheleis kai mataioi). The conjunction gar ("for") provides the reason for avoiding them. Two adjectives deliver the verdict:</p>
          <p>The adjective anopheles ("unprofitable, useless, producing no benefit") is the direct negation of ophelimos ("profitable") from verse 8. The contrast is explicit: good works are ophelima (profitable); these controversies are anopheleis (unprofitable). The community's energy is a finite resource, and it must be invested in what produces benefit, not wasted on what produces none.</p>
          <p>The adjective mataios ("vain, empty, futile, worthless, purposeless") echoes the description of the false teachers in 1:10 as mataiologoi ("empty talkers"). The root mataios runs through the characterization of the false teaching: their talk is empty (1:10, mataiologoi), and their controversies are empty (3:9, mataioi). The emptiness is the defining quality of what the false teachers produce: it generates heat but no light, conflict but no resolution, activity but no fruit.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3.10-Titus.3.11">
          <title>Verses 10-11: The Divisive Person — Warning and Rejection</title>
          <p>"As for a person who stirs up division, after warning him once and then twice, have nothing more to do with him, knowing that such a person is warped and sinful; he is self-condemned."</p>
          <p>(Hairetikon anthropon meta mian kai deuteran nouthesian paraitou, eidos hoti exestraptai ho toioutos kai hamartanei, on autokatakridos.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The instruction to avoid foolish controversies (3:9). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> Personal travel instructions and the charge to provision Zenas and Apollos (3:12-13). <hi type="bold">How these verses connect the two:</hi> The instruction moves from the general (avoid controversies) to the specific (how to deal with the person who promotes them). The false teachers of 1:10-16 generate the controversies of 3:9; the hairetikos anthropos of 3:10 is the individual embodiment of that problem. The transition to 3:12-13 completes the letter's practical business: after addressing the final disciplinary instruction, Paul turns to personal logistics.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"As for a person who stirs up division"</hi> (hairetikon anthropon). The adjective hairetikos ("factious, divisive, promoting division, sectarian") appears only here in the New Testament. It is derived from the noun hairesis ("choice, sect, party, faction, opinion"). In Acts 5:17, the party of the Sadducees is called a hairesis. In Acts 15:5, "the sect (haireseōs) of the Pharisees." In Acts 24:5, Paul's opponents call the Christian movement a hairesis. In Acts 24:14, Paul responds: "according to the Way, which they call a sect (hairesin), I worship the God of our fathers." In 1 Corinthians 11:19: "there must be factions (haireseis) among you in order that those who are genuine among you may be recognized." In Galatians 5:20, haireseis appears among the works of the flesh alongside eritheia ("selfish ambition") and dichostasiai ("divisions").</p>
          <p>The hairetikos anthropos is a person who promotes haireseis — factions, divisions, parties. The term does not describe someone who holds a private opinion but someone whose activity creates factions within the community, splitting the body into competing groups. Given the letter's context, the hairetikos anthropos is the individual who promotes the foolish controversies, genealogies, and quarrels about the law described in 3:9 — the person whose teaching and behavior divide the community. This is not a merely intellectual disagreement but a social disruption: the divisive person fractures the community's unity.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"After warning him once and then twice"</hi> (meta mian kai deuteran nouthesian). The noun nouthesia ("warning, admonition, instruction, correction") is composed of nous ("mind") + tithemi ("to place, to put"). The word literally means "placing in the mind" — putting something into a person's understanding, correcting their thinking. In Ephesians 6:4: "bring them up in the discipline and instruction (nouthesia) of the Lord." In 1 Corinthians 10:11: "these things... were written down for our instruction (nouthesian)." The related verb nouthetein appears in Acts 20:31, where Paul reminded the Ephesian elders: "for three years I did not cease night or day to admonish (nouthetōn) every one with tears." In Colossians 1:28: "warning (nouthetountes) everyone and teaching everyone with all wisdom." In 1 Thessalonians 5:14: "admonish (noutheteite) the idle."</p>
          <p>The instruction specifies a limited process: mian kai deuteran — "one and then a second" warning. The number is deliberately limited. The divisive person is not to be endlessly engaged in dialogue but given two opportunities to hear and respond to correction. The limit recognizes that repeated engagement with a factious person often feeds the very division it seeks to cure: the more the community debates with the divisive individual, the more attention and platform the individual receives, and the more the community's energy is consumed.</p>
          <p>The process echoes Jesus' instruction in Matthew 18:15-17 about dealing with a sinning community member: first a private confrontation, then witnesses, then the community as a whole, and finally exclusion — "let him be to you as a Gentile and a tax collector." The principle is the same: correction is attempted before separation, and separation comes only after correction has been offered and refused.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Have nothing more to do with him"</hi> (paraitou). The verb paraiteisthai ("to refuse, to reject, to decline, to have nothing to do with, to excuse oneself from") in the present middle imperative means "keep refusing, keep declining." In 1 Timothy 4:7: "Have nothing to do with (paraitou) irreverent, silly myths." In 1 Timothy 5:11: "refuse (paraitou) to enroll younger widows." In 2 Timothy 2:23: "Have nothing to do with (paraitou) foolish, ignorant controversies." The verb does not necessarily describe formal excommunication in a developed ecclesiastical sense but a withdrawal of engagement: after two warnings, the community ceases to interact with the divisive person as though there were still a dispute to be resolved. The divisive person has been warned, has refused the correction, and is now declined — the community moves on without him.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Knowing that such a person is warped and sinful; he is self-condemned"</hi> (eidos hoti exestraptai ho toioutos kai hamartanei, on autokatakridos).</p>
          <p>The participle eidos ("knowing") provides the epistemic ground for the rejection: Titus can act with confidence because he knows what follows.</p>
          <p>The verb ekstrephein ("to turn inside out, to pervert, to twist, to warp") in the perfect passive (exestraptai) describes a settled condition: the divisive person has been turned from his proper course and remains in that twisted state. The perfect tense indicates a completed action with ongoing results: the warping happened in the past and persists. The word appears only here in the New Testament. The image is of a thing bent out of its proper shape — like a piece of wood warped by moisture, no longer able to serve its intended purpose. The divisive person's thinking and character have been distorted to the point where they no longer function as they should.</p>
          <p>The verb hamartanein ("to sin, to miss the mark, to err") in the present tense indicates ongoing sinful activity: the divisive person continues to sin. The present tense suggests that the sin is not a past mistake but an ongoing pattern of behavior — the divisive activity continues.</p>
          <p>The adjective autokatakridos ("self-condemned, condemned by himself") appears only here in the New Testament. It is composed of autos ("self") + katakrinein ("to condemn, to pass judgment against"). The word describes a person who has condemned himself — his own actions serve as the judgment against him. By refusing the correction offered in the first and second warnings, the divisive person has demonstrated his own commitment to division. He has been given the opportunity to repent, and his refusal of that opportunity constitutes his own verdict against himself. The community does not condemn him; he condemns himself by his persistent refusal of correction. The word removes any burden of guilt from the community's decision to separate: the divisive person's own behavior is the judgment.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3.12-Titus.3.14">
          <title>Verses 12-14: Personal Instructions and the Final Charge About Good Works</title>
          <p>"When I send Artemas or Tychicus to you, do your best to come to me at Nicopolis, for I have decided to spend the winter there. Do your best to speed Zenas the lawyer and Apollos on their way; see that they lack nothing. And let our people learn to devote themselves to good works, so as to help cases of urgent need, and not be unfruitful."</p>
          <p>(Hotan pempso Arteman pros se e Tychikon, spoudason elthein pros me eis Nikopolin; ekei gar kekrika parachimasai. Zenan ton nomikon kai Apollon spoudaios propempson, hina meden autois leipe. Manthanetosan de kai hoi hemeteroi kalon ergon proistasthai eis tas anankaias chreias, hina me osin akarpoi.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">What the author was arguing immediately before:</hi> The instruction about the divisive person (3:10-11). <hi type="bold">What the author argues immediately after:</hi> The final greeting (3:15). <hi type="bold">How these verses connect the two:</hi> The letter transitions from its final disciplinary instruction to the practical logistics of Paul's mission. The personal instructions are not mere postscripts but embodiments of the letter's themes: the provision for Zenas and Apollos exemplifies the good works the letter has been urging, and the final exhortation about good works (3:14) restates the letter's central practical concern one last time before the closing.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 12: Travel Plans</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"When I send Artemas or Tychicus to you"</hi> (hotan pempso Arteman pros se e Tychikon). The conjunction hotan ("when, whenever") with the subjunctive pempso ("I send") indicates a future event that is planned but not yet executed. Paul intends to send one of two persons to Crete as a replacement for Titus. The name Artemas (Arteman, accusative) appears only here in the New Testament. Nothing further is known about this individual. The name Tychicus (Tychikon) is better attested: in Acts 20:4, Tychicus of Asia accompanied Paul on his journey to Jerusalem. In Ephesians 6:21-22, Paul describes Tychicus as "the beloved brother and faithful minister in the Lord" and says he is sending him to the Ephesians. In Colossians 4:7-8, the same description appears: "Tychicus will tell you all about my activities. He is a beloved brother and faithful minister and fellow servant in the Lord." In 2 Timothy 4:12: "Tychicus I have sent to Ephesus." Tychicus is a trusted courier and representative — a person Paul relies on for sensitive assignments. The disjunction e ("or") indicates that Paul has not yet decided which of the two will be sent.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Do your best to come to me at Nicopolis"</hi> (spoudason elthein pros me eis Nikopolin). The verb spoudazein ("to make haste, to be diligent, to do one's best, to make every effort") in the aorist imperative commands urgent action: once the replacement arrives, Titus is to make every effort to join Paul. The destination is Nicopolis ("city of victory"). Several cities bore this name in the Roman world; the most likely candidate is Nicopolis in Epirus, on the western coast of Greece, founded by Augustus to commemorate his victory at the Battle of Actium in 31 BCE. It was a major city on the route between Rome and the eastern Mediterranean.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For I have decided to spend the winter there"</hi> (ekei gar kekrika parachimasai). The verb krinein ("to decide, to judge, to determine") in the perfect active (kekrika) indicates a settled decision. The verb paracheimazein ("to winter, to spend the winter") describes the practice of remaining in one location during the winter months when sea travel was suspended (the mare clausum, roughly November through March). The detail is practical: Paul will be at Nicopolis through the winter, and he wants Titus to join him there before travel becomes impossible.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 13: Provision for Zenas and Apollos</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Do your best to speed Zenas the lawyer and Apollos on their way; see that they lack nothing"</hi> (Zenan ton nomikon kai Apollon spoudaios propempson, hina meden autois leipe).</p>
          <p>The verb propempein ("to send forward, to escort, to help on one's journey, to provision for travel") describes a specific act of hospitality: providing a departing traveler with supplies, money, companionship, and whatever else is needed for the journey. In Romans 15:24: "I hope to see you in passing and to be sent on my way (propemphthenal) there by you." In 1 Corinthians 16:6: "so that you may help me on my journey (propempsete)." In 3 John 6: "You will do well to send them on their journey (propempsas) in a manner worthy of God." The propempsis was a concrete expression of hospitality and community support — not merely saying goodbye but actively equipping the traveler for the road ahead.</p>
          <p>The adverb spoudaios ("diligently, eagerly, with haste") intensifies the instruction: Titus is to provision them with urgency and generosity.</p>
          <p>Two persons are named. Zenas (Zenan) is identified as ton nomikon — "the lawyer." The adjective nomikos ("pertaining to the law, legal, learned in the law") in the Gospels describes those expert in the Mosaic law (Luke 7:30; 10:25; 11:45-46, 52; 14:3). Zenas may be a Jewish legal expert who has come to faith in Christ, or the term may here refer to a Roman jurist — a person trained in Roman civil law. The name Zenas appears only here in the New Testament. Apollos (Apollon) is well known from Acts and Paul's letters. In Acts 18:24-28, Apollos is described as "a Jew, born in Alexandria, an eloquent man, competent in the Scriptures" who taught accurately about Jesus, was further instructed by Priscilla and Aquila, and became a powerful apologist for the gospel in Achaia. In 1 Corinthians 1:12 and 3:4-6, Apollos appears as one of the teachers around whom factions formed in Corinth. In 1 Corinthians 3:6: "I planted, Apollos watered, but God gave the growth." In 1 Corinthians 16:12, Paul mentions Apollos's travel plans. The pairing of Zenas and Apollos suggests that they may be the bearers of this letter or fellow workers passing through Crete on their way to another destination.</p>
          <p>The purpose clause hina meden autois leipe — "so that they lack nothing" — specifies the standard of provision: the community is to ensure that Zenas and Apollos depart fully equipped, with every need met. The verb leipein ("to lack, to be deficient, to fall short") recalls ta leiponta ("the things remaining, what was left unfinished") in 1:5 — the unfinished work that Titus was left to complete. There, organizational work was lacking; here, the travelers must not lack. The community that is being organized toward wholeness must also provide wholeness to those who serve the mission.</p>
          <title level="2">Verse 14: The Final Good-Works Charge</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And let our people learn to devote themselves to good works, so as to help cases of urgent need, and not be unfruitful"</hi> (manthanetosan de kai hoi hemeteroi kalon ergon proistasthai eis tas anankaias chreias, hina me osin akarpoi).</p>
          <p>The verb manthanein ("to learn, to be taught, to acquire the practice of") in the present imperative (manthanetosan, third person plural) commands ongoing learning: the community must keep learning this practice. The learning is not merely intellectual but practical: they are to learn by doing, to acquire the habit of good works through practice. The conjunction kai ("also, even") suggests an addition: in addition to what has already been said, our people too must learn this.</p>
          <p>The possessive pronoun hoi hemeteroi ("our own people") is affectionate and identifies: these are Paul's own community members, the believers in Crete. The phrase distinguishes them from outsiders and creates a sense of communal responsibility.</p>
          <p>The phrase kalon ergon proistasthai — "to devote themselves to good works" — repeats exactly the language of 3:8. The repetition is the letter's final statement of its central practical concern. The first occurrence (3:8) drew the conclusion from the theological statement (3:4-7). This second occurrence (3:14) applies the principle to the concrete situation of provisioning traveling workers. The good works that the letter has been urging are not merely a general aspiration but a specific, immediate practice: helping fellow workers who have practical needs.</p>
          <p>The phrase eis tas anankaias chreias — "for cases of urgent need" or "for pressing needs" — specifies the occasions for good works. The adjective anankaios ("necessary, pressing, urgent, indispensable") indicates that the needs are not trivial but genuine. The noun chreia ("need, necessity, requirement, use") identifies what requires attention: real needs of real people. The good works that the letter envisions are responsive to concrete human situations, not performed for display or merit.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And not be unfruitful"</hi> (hina me osin akarpoi). The final hina clause states the negative purpose: the community must practice good works so that they do not become akarpos — "unfruitful, barren, producing nothing." The adjective akarpos ("unfruitful, barren, without fruit") is composed of the alpha-privative (a-) + karpos ("fruit, produce, result"). In Matthew 13:22, the seed choked by thorns becomes "unfruitful" (akarpos). In Mark 4:19, worldly concerns make the word "unfruitful" (akarpon). In 2 Peter 1:8: "if these qualities are yours and are increasing, they keep you from being ineffective or unfruitful (akarpous) in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ." In Jude 12, the false teachers are described as "fruitless trees in late autumn, twice dead, uprooted." The agricultural metaphor treats the community as a planting that must bear fruit. A community that does not produce good works is like a tree that does not produce fruit — it occupies space without fulfilling its purpose. The God who saved them by mercy (3:5), poured out his Spirit on them richly (3:6), and made them heirs of eternal life (3:7) did not do so in order to produce a barren community but a fruitful one.</p>
          <p>The connection to Jesus' teaching in John 15:1-8 is audible: "Every branch in me that does not bear fruit he takes away... By this my Father is glorified, that you bear much fruit" (John 15:2, 8). The expectation of fruit-bearing is not an optional addendum to faith but an inherent dimension of it. A faith that does not produce fruit is a faith that has failed to fulfill its purpose.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3.15">
          <title>Verse 15: The Final Greeting</title>
          <p>"All who are with me send greetings to you. Greet those who love us in the faith. Grace be with you all."</p>
          <p>(Aspazontai se hoi met' emou pantes. Aspasai tous philountas hemas en pistei. He charis meta panton hymon.)</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"All who are with me send greetings to you"</hi> (aspazontai se hoi met' emou pantes). The verb aspazesthai ("to greet, to welcome, to embrace, to salute") is the standard epistolary greeting formula. The phrase hoi met' emou pantes ("all who are with me") indicates that Paul is surrounded by fellow workers and community members who join in the greeting. The pronoun se ("you") is singular, addressed to Titus personally.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Greet those who love us in the faith"</hi> (aspasai tous philountas hemas en pistei). The verb philein ("to love, to have affection for, to be fond of") describes personal affection. The participle tous philountas hemas ("those who love us") identifies a specific group among the Cretan believers: those who have personal affection for Paul and his mission. The phrase en pistei ("in the faith") qualifies the love: this is love that operates within the sphere of faith, love that is grounded in and expressed through the shared faith of the community. The same phrase koinen pistin ("common faith") appeared in 1:4 to describe the faith that Paul and Titus share. The love that is grounded in this common faith is the relational bond of the community.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Grace be with you all"</hi> (he charis meta panton hymon). The final benediction extends grace — the same charis that appeared in 2:11 (the grace of God has appeared) and 3:7 (justified by his grace) — to the entire community. The pronoun hymon ("you") is now plural, expanding the address from Titus alone (se, singular, earlier in the verse) to the whole community (panton hymon, "all of you"). The letter that opened with "grace and peace" (1:4) closes with "grace" — the word that has carried the letter's theological weight from beginning to end. The grace that appeared, that trained, that saved, that justified, that made heirs is now invoked as a continuing presence: may it remain with all of you.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="Titus.3">
          <title>Summary of the Chapter's Argument</title>
          <p>Titus 3 completes the letter by extending its behavioral instruction outward, providing its second and final theological grounding, and closing the ring that the letter opened.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(1) Verses 1-2: The community's posture toward the outside world.</hi> The behavioral instructions move from the internal life of the community (chapter 2's instructions by social category) to the community's relationship with the civic order. Believers are to submit to rulers and authorities, obey, be ready for every good work, speak evil of no one, avoid quarreling, be gentle, and show complete courtesy toward all people. The instruction is both civic and missionary: the community's conduct in the public sphere either commends or discredits the gospel.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(2) Verse 3: The motivational ground for gentleness.</hi> The reason the community must treat outsiders with courtesy is that the community was once in the same condition as those outsiders. The catalog of the former life — foolish, disobedient, led astray, enslaved to passions and pleasures, passing days in malice and envy, hated and hating — eliminates any basis for spiritual superiority and grounds the instruction to show gentleness in shared experience.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(3) Verses 4-7: The second theological statement — salvation by mercy through regeneration.</hi> The core of the chapter is the theological statement that parallels 2:11-14. God's goodness and loving kindness appeared (3:4), and he saved us — not because of works done in righteousness but according to his own mercy (3:5a) — through the washing of regeneration and renewal of the Holy Spirit (3:5b), whom he poured out richly through Jesus Christ our Savior (3:6), so that, justified by his grace, we might become heirs according to the hope of eternal life (3:7). Every element of the statement emphasizes divine initiative and excludes human merit as the basis of salvation. The means of salvation — the washing of regeneration and the Spirit's renewal — fulfills the prophetic promise of Ezekiel 36:25-27, where God pledged to sprinkle clean water on his people, give them a new heart, and put his Spirit within them. The purpose of salvation is the creation of justified heirs — people whose status before God is secured by grace and whose future is defined by the hope of eternal life first announced in the letter's greeting (1:2).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(4) Verse 8: The practical conclusion.</hi> The theological statement issues in a practical demand: those who have believed in God must be careful to devote themselves to good works. The declaration that these things are "excellent and profitable for people" sets up the contrast with the next verse.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(5) Verse 9: The warning against foolish controversies.</hi> In direct contrast to the excellent and profitable good works, the foolish controversies, genealogies, dissensions, and quarrels about the law promoted by the false teachers are declared "unprofitable and worthless." The instruction to avoid them closes the ring with 1:10-14: the insubordinate, empty-talking, mind-deceiving members of the circumcision party who teach Jewish myths and human commands have been the source of the controversies the community must now avoid.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(6) Verses 10-11: The divisive person.</hi> The general instruction to avoid controversies becomes specific: the person who promotes faction is to be warned once, then twice, and then rejected. The divisive person is self-condemned — his refusal of correction constitutes his own judgment against himself. The community is freed from the obligation of endless engagement with a person whose activity destroys unity.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(7) Verses 12-14: Personal instructions and the final good-works charge.</hi> The letter closes with practical logistics — travel plans, provision for fellow workers — that embody the good works the letter has been urging. The final exhortation restates the central theme one last time: "let our people learn to devote themselves to good works, so as to help cases of urgent need, and not be unfruitful." The community's purpose is fruit-bearing — the production of visible, concrete, beneficial acts that demonstrate the reality of the faith they profess and the mercy they have received.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">(8) Verse 15: The greeting and benediction.</hi> Grace — the word that has carried the letter's theological weight from the appearing of grace (2:11) to justification by grace (3:7) — is invoked as a continuing presence for the entire community.</p>
          <p>The letter's overall architecture is now visible in its completeness. Chapter 1 diagnosed the crisis: false teachers who profess to know God but deny him by their works, rendering themselves unfit for every good work. Chapter 2 prescribed the remedy: sound teaching that produces transformed conduct in every social category, grounded in the appearing of grace that trains believers to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives as a people zealous for good works. Chapter 3 extended the remedy outward to civic life, grounded it in a second theological statement about salvation by mercy through regeneration and the Spirit, drew the conclusion that believers must devote themselves to good works, and warned against the controversies that the false teachers promote. The letter began with the hope of eternal life promised by the God who cannot lie (1:2) and ends with heirs of that same eternal life, justified by grace, and charged to demonstrate that inheritance through the good works that their faith demands and their mercy empowers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
